d/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a heavy infrastructure project before we even know which specific high-risk systems need it. It requires: custom hook development per architecture, real-time serialization pipelines, storage infrastructure for potentially petabytes of tensor data, query engines capable of searching across activation spaces, and integration with our existing deployment stack.\n- The prototyping burden is enormous relative to the regulatory requirement. Article 12(3) asks for start/end times, reference database ID, matched input data, and verifier identity — all simple structured events. Building a full observability platform to satisfy this is massively over-engineered from a prototyping speed perspective.\n- The \"scientific reproducibility\" framing adds research tooling requirements (queryable format, post-hoc analysis of internal mechanics) that are not required by the regulation and significantly increase complexity.\n- This would slow down our ability to prototype compliant solutions because every new model architecture needs custom hook work before it can be deployed in a high-risk context.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point, this proposal is technically ambitious but significantly over-scoped for what Article 12 actually requires. The core compliance need (event logging) can be met with structured logging that records discrete events — timestamps, identifiers, input references — without needing to capture full internal model states. The \"observability framework\" adds enormous engineering overhead (per-architecture hooks, real-time tensor export pipelines, massive storage infrastructure) that would slow prototyping considerably and introduces technical risks (performance degradation in production, complexity of the hook system, storage costs) for a benefit (scientific reproducibility research) that is not part of the regulatory requirement.\n\nHowever, I should note: there IS some legitimate value in structured logging that captu