ave to divert their attention or engineering time to compliance plumbing.\n- But there's a cost: building this middleware itself requires significant upfront investment of infrastructure team resources and budget. Is that the best use of scarce funds? For a lab with many high-risk AI projects, yes — it amortizes well. For a lab with only 1-2 such projects, no.\n- The risk is that the infrastructure team gets pulled into building this middleware when they might have other priorities. This is an opportunity cost I need to consider.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal leverages the existing infrastructure team rather than asking each research group to solve it independently. This aligns with using existing structures.\n- However, \"standardized software library\" implies new development — it's not repurposing existing tools. It's a net-new investment.\n- The concern is whether this middleware will actually be adopted by research teams or if they'll bypass it because it adds friction to their workflows. \"Plug-and-play\" needs to mean genuinely low-friction.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is a well-conceived proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by centralizing compliance engineering. It protects scientific ambition by making logging automatic rather than manual. It uses the existing infrastructure team structure appropriately. The main risks are: (1) upfront cost and timeline before teams benefit, (2) the middleware may not cover all Article 12 requirements (especially 12(2)(a) about risk situations), (3) adoption friction if it's not truly seamless for diverse AI architectures.\n\nBut on balance, this is a strong fit for my domain of concern. The efficiency argument is compelling: one build vs. N builds. It doesn't constrain scientific ambition — it enables it by removing compliance overhead from individual projects. It uses the infrastructure team (existing resource) rather than creating a new 