enefit (leadership, community adoption) is real but soft — it doesn't directly reduce compliance risk or operational burden.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** This would require new infrastructure for collaboration governance, open-source maintenance, and community engagement that we don't currently have. It's additive rather than leveraging what we already have. We'd need dedicated personnel to maintain the specification, handle contributions, manage versioning, etc.\n\n**Counter-arguments (why it might be good):**\n- If we're already building logging capabilities for compliance, extracting a benchmark suite is somewhat incremental\n- External collaboration could spread costs\n- It builds goodwill and regulatory relationships\n- Could inform our own implementation by forcing us to articulate what \"appropriate traceability\" means\n\n**My assessment:** From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this proposal asks the lab to take on an open-ended, collaborative, maintenance-heavy project that is tangential to our actual compliance obligation. The qualitative nature of Article 12(2) makes it very hard to build a definitive \"evaluation suite\" — you'd be codifying interpretation, which regulators may not adopt, and which could shift as guidance evolves. The collaboration overhead with other R&D institutions adds coordination costs without guaranteed returns. The \"leadership positioning\" benefit is real but doesn't offset the resource drain for our core mission of actually implementing compliant AI systems within budget.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge there's some merit — articulating what \"appropriate traceability\" means forces internal clarity that benefits our own compliance work. And if done in collaboration with other institutions, it spreads costs. But as a standalone proposal that commits us to an open-source maintenance commitment indefinitely, it strains resource efficiency.\n\nI'll lean toward reject, but with moderate promise (maybe 40-50) because there's genuine value in the internal articulation exercise and some external visibility, but the scope and ongoing commitment make it a poor use of scarce implementation resources relative to directly building our own compliant logging infrastructure.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 38-42. The scientific ambition is somewhat high for what is essentially an interpretive standardization exercise that may not even be adopted by regulators. The resource ask (open-source maintenance + multi-institution coordination) is disproportionate to the direct compliance benefit we'd get.\n",
