ding it on compliance infrastructure makes sense only if done efficiently. An ELK-based solution is a cost-effective approach compared to building custom monitoring from scratch or buying expensive proprietary observability platforms.\n- The proposal explicitly says \"without interrupting the primary development cycle with manual checks\" — this speaks directly to protecting R&D throughput, which aligns with my mandate.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- If the lab already has some form of log aggregation (even for non-AI systems), this could be an extension rather than a new build. The proposal says \"deploy an ELK stack tailored for AI telemetry\" — the word \"tailored\" suggests modification of an existing or standard deployment, not necessarily building from zero.\n- The dashboard converts raw logs into \"actionable technical alerts\" — this is good use of infrastructure because it doesn't just store data (which Article 12 requires) but adds analytical value on top.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1): automatic recording of events/logs over lifetime\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(a): identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(b): facilitating post-market monitoring (Article 72)\n- The \"anomalies\" flagging for \"substantial modification\" is particularly relevant — Article 43 requires conformity assessment after substantial modifications, so early detection via logging is a smart compliance strategy.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Real-time\" observability might be overkill or costly for some of our systems if they don't generate high-frequency events. But ELK handles this well at various scales.\n- The scope seems reasonable — it's focused on log-based monitoring, not a full-blown MLOps platform. That keeps resource commitment bounded.\n- One risk: \"validation baselines\" need to be maintained and updated as systems evolve. This is an ongoing operational cost, but small relative to the R&D budget.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a pragmatic, well-scoped infrastructure investment that directly serves the Article 12 record-keeping requirement while adding genuine operational value (early risk detection). It uses mature technology (ELK), which keeps costs predictable and avoids consuming scarce R&D capacity on novel approaches. The \"without interrupting primary development cycle\" framing aligns perfectly with my mandate to protect scientific throughput. From a resource-efficiency standpoint, this is high-leverage: modest investment in well-understood tooling yields both regulatory compliance AND operational risk reduction.\n\nI would approve this. It's efficient, uses proven technology, doesn't consume R&D scientist time (it's an engineering/DevOps task), and directly addresses the regulatory requirement while adding value beyond mere compliance.\n\nPromise score: This is a strong fit for my vantage point. It's not groundbreaking, but it's exactly the kind of efficient infrastructure investment I want to see funded. I'd say around 78-82. Let me settle on 80.\n",
