ring novel failure modes; it's testing whether logs are complete enough for reconstruction. That's closer to an audit procedure than a research contribution.\n\nThe framing of \"intellectual merit\" applied to log completeness is a stretch. The knowledge frontier in AI is about understanding emergence, interpretability at deeper levels, generalization bounds, new architectures — not about verifying that timestamps and database references were logged correctly.\n\nThis proposal would consume researcher time on what is essentially an operational compliance task while giving it the veneer of peer review and \"intellectual merit.\" From a research impact standpoint, it's a net negative or at best neutral — it doesn't advance knowledge, it enforces documentation completeness through social pressure (peer review requirement) rather than technical innovation.\n\nDecision: reject. The proposal conflates regulatory compliance verification with intellectual research contribution and would divert researcher effort from frontier-advancing work to audit-style tasks without generating novel scientific understanding.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 15-20. There's a tiny kernel of value (ensuring logs are actually useful for post-market monitoring could inform better logging architecture design), but the framing as \"intellectual merit\" and \"peer review requirement\" misallocates research resources. It's not harmful, just misdirected from a knowledge-frontier perspective.\n",
