t (MLflow, DVC, etc.). This proposal formalizes it.\n- The dual benefit of regulatory compliance AND scientific reproducibility (\"streamlining the process of reproducing failed experiments\") means the resource investment serves two masters — not just compliance overhead but also research efficiency.\n- Cryptographic hashing is computationally cheap and provides tamper-evidence without requiring a new storage paradigm — you're just adding hashes to existing metadata stores.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Cryptographically hashing... every dataset\" could be expensive for very large datasets (terabyte-scale). However, you hash the manifest/checksum, not necessarily re-hash every file each time if you use incremental approaches. This is manageable with standard tooling.\n- Scope: \"every model weight used in deployments\" — this is reasonable and bounded to deployed systems, not every experiment.\n- The metadata store linking versions — this could be built on top of existing tools (MLflow tracking server, DVC registry) rather than building from scratch.\n- The proposal is well-scoped: it targets the specific regulatory requirement in Art 12(3)(b) and the general traceability mandate, without over-reaching into areas like full post-market monitoring infrastructure or human oversight logging (Art 14(5)).\n\n**Resource-allocation view:**\nThis is a moderate-scope implementation. It's not building an entire observability platform from scratch; it's adding a versioning/hashing layer to what should already exist in a serious ML lab's CI/CD. The integration into CI/CD means we're not creating a parallel process — we're extending the existing deployment gate. The fact that it also serves research reproducibility means the marginal cost of compliance is partially offset by research efficiency gains. This is a good use of resources: bounded scope, clear regulatory driver, dual benefit, leverages existing infrastructure.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\nThis doesn't 