        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2372\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal frames this as a parser/mapping problem between two document schemas, but the semantic gap is far deeper than field-to-field translation: several mandatory log fields (e.g., Art 12(3)(d) – identification of natural persons in verification) are PII that can never appear in a publication, and compliance audit records (operational timestamps, database-reference checks) lack the explanatory narrative structure peer reviewers expect. A prototype template-filler is trivially fast to build but would produce outputs satisfying neither the legal standard nor academic rigor without substantial human rework, meaning the 'eliminates manual labor' premise does not survive contact with the actual content.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 22\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2372 is about building an \"Automated Compliance to Publication Pipeline\" — a software tool that automatically converts regulatory logs into structured appendices for scientific papers and peer review, mapping EU record-keeping fields onto reproducibility templates used in top AI conferences.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is building a parser/mapper between two document formats: (1) EU AI Act compliance logs with specific required fields, and (2) reproducibility templates used in academic publications.\n- This is essentially a structured data transformation problem. Both sides are text-heavy documents with defined structures.\n- The \"parser\" that maps regulatory fields to academic templates is technically straightforward if both formats are well-defined. However, the reality is:\n  - EU compliance logs will contain operational data (timestamps, database references, input data, person IDs) — these are structured but not necessarily in a format that maps cleanly to academic reproducibility sections.\n  - Academic reproducibility templates (like those for NeurIPS, ICML, etc.) have their own structure: dataset descriptions, hyperparameters, training procedures, evaluation metrics, code availability statements, etc.\n  - The mapping between \"recording of the period of each use\" and a paper's methodology section is... not obvious. Most compliance log fields don't have direct academic counterparts.\n  - Many compliance requirements (like identifying natural persons involved in verification) are legally sensitive PII that would NEVER appear in a publication. This is a fundamental mismatch.\n  - The \"reference database against which input data has been checked\" might map to a dataset description, but the granularity and format will be very different.\n\n- So the technical challenge isn't really building a parser — it's that the two document types serve fundamentally different purposes and have significant semantic gaps. A simple field-mapping parser won't work; you'd need substantial NLP/LLM-based transformation plus human review for anything meaningful.\n\n**Speed of Prototyping:**\n- The prototype could be built relatively quickly if you just do a crude template filling exercise — map some fields, output a document. That's a weekend project.\n- But making it actually useful (i.e., the output would pass peer review AND satisfy legal requirements) is a much larger engineering effort that touches on NLP, domain knowledge of both regulatory and academic publishing norms, and potentially sensitive data handling.\n- The \"eliminates manual labor\" claim is dubious — academic writing involves interpretation, narrative structure, and contextual explanation that can't be auto-generated from log entries.\n\n**Quality concerns:**\n- Converting compliance logs (which are operational/audit records) into publication appendices (which require explanatory context, motivation, methodological justification) is not a 1:1 mapping problem. It's a content generation + transformation problem.\n- The PII issue is a hard blocker for several of the required log fields (Art 12(3)(d) requires identifying natural persons — you can't put names in a paper).\n- The proposal somewhat conflates \"compliance documentation\" with \"scientific reproducibility\" — they overlap but are not the same thing. Compliance docs need to be comprehensive and auditable; academic docs need to be clear, concise, and methodologically focused.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a pure prototyping standpoint, building a basic template-mapping tool is technically feasible and could be prototyped in days. But the promise of \"eliminating manual labor\" while ensuring both legal AND academic standards is overreaching. The semantic gap between compliance logs and publication appendices is significant. Several required log fields (PII, operational audit trails) simply don't translate to publications. The tool would likely produce outputs that satisfy neither standard well without substantial human intervention.\n\nHowever, there IS a narrow use case: for the overlapping subset of information (training data descriptions, model architecture details, evaluation metrics), an auto-population tool could save time. But calling it \"automated\" and claiming it \"eliminates manual labor\" overstates what's technically achievable.\n\nI'd lean toward rejecting this. The technical viability is questionable at the claimed scope — it's not a simple parser problem, it's a complex content transformation with fundamental semantic mismatches between the two document types. A prototype would be easy to build but wouldn't deliver on the stated promise. The PII issue alone makes several required fields unmappable to publications. From my speed/quality prototyping vantage point, I'd spend that time building something where the input→output mapping is actually well-defined and the output quality is verifiable.\n\nLet me settle on a decision: reject. The proposal conflates two document types with fundamentally different purposes and audiences, overstates what a \"parser\" can accomplish, and has hard blockers (PII) that make several core fields simply non-transferable. A prototype would be easy but the deliverable wouldn't work as described.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 20-25. There's a kernel of utility in auto-populating some overlapping fields, but at the stated scope and promise level, it's not something I'd back with implementation resources.\n",
