d in Article 3 as changes that alter compliance with requirements) from operational logs alone is a significant technical challenge.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is framed as a \"research project investigating how...\" - this is exploratory research, not a concrete engineering solution.\n- The scope is quite broad: \"forensic analysis,\" \"statistical anomaly detection,\" \"early warning systems,\" \"systemic risks.\" This is multiple projects in one.\n- The prototyping path is unclear. What would a first prototype look like? You'd need to define what behavioral signals are actually available in the mandated logs, then design an anomaly detector appropriate for those signals. That's doable but the research framing makes it open-ended.\n- However, there IS a concrete starting point: implement the logging infrastructure required by Article 12 (which we need anyway), and then prototype simple statistical monitors on top of that data. This is technically feasible as an initial prototype.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering standpoint, this has some merit because:\n- The logging infrastructure we need to build for compliance IS the data source here, so there's alignment.\n- Basic anomaly detection on operational metrics (input distributions, match rates, verification override rates) is prototypically tractable.\n\nBut it has significant concerns:\n- It's framed as a research project, not an engineering deliverable. \"Investigating how\" suggests open-ended exploration rather than building something specific.\n- The technical gap between mandated logs (which are operational/audit records) and the behavioral signal needed to detect model drift is substantial. The mandated logs don't capture internal model behavior at high resolution.\n- The scope is too broad for a single proposal - it encompasses logging architecture, statistical methods, alerting systems, and \"systemic risk\" identification.\n- The framing as \"transforming regulatory b