is explicitly builds on what we already have (experiment tracking tools, model promotion workflows). It's an integration layer rather than a replacement system.\n- The \"living documentation\" aspect means the docs don't become stale artifacts that need periodic manual refresh - they update with each version promotion, which aligns with Recital 71's requirement that technical documentation \"should be kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\"\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The scope is somewhat broad - \"extracts training metadata, data lineage, and validation metrics\" could be complex depending on our heterogeneity of experiment tracking across teams.\n- However, the proposal frames it as an integration with existing tools, not a replacement, which suggests incremental adoption.\n- There's some risk that the templating engine needs to handle edge cases in model architectures or unconventional training approaches, but this is manageable.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a highly efficient use of resources. It addresses a real recurring cost (manual compliance documentation), leverages existing infrastructure rather than requiring new capital expenditure, and scales with our research output without proportional staffing increases. The alignment with Recital 71's specific requirements (keeping docs up to date, covering algorithms/data/training/testing/validation) makes it directly relevant. From a resource-allocation standpoint, this is one of the higher-leverage investments I could make - moderate upfront development effort for significant recurring savings and risk reduction.\n\nI'd rate this highly from my vantage point. It's not a 100 because there are integration complexities and the \"living documentation\" concept needs to handle edge cases across diverse model types in our portfolio, but it's very strong. I'll say around 82-85. Let me settle on 83.\n\nDecision: approve - this clearly clears my bar for resource efficiency and