r high-risk AI systems subject to Article 12, this is arguably necessary rather than optional overhead.\n- The proposal focuses on \"documentation and logging verification\" which maps well to Article 12 requirements (automatic recording of events, logs, traceability). This isn't just generic compliance — it's specifically tied to the record-keeping obligations.\n- From a resource-efficiency standpoint, front-loading the budget allocation is more efficient than discovering mid-project that you've run out of capacity for mandatory logging infrastructure.\n\nOverall assessment: This proposal directly addresses my core domain concern. It's a pragmatic, well-scoped resource management intervention that makes compliance costs visible and predictable rather than letting them erode scientific work organically. The quarterly review mechanism adds accountability. The specific focus on documentation and logging verification (rather than vague \"compliance\") shows it's grounded in actual Article 12 obligations.\n\nI think this is a strong proposal from my perspective. It's not a research breakthrough, but it's exactly the kind of operational infrastructure that makes R&D sustainable under regulatory constraints. I'd approve it with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my domain. It addresses a real and recurring pain point (compliance eating into science time). The approach is sound — explicit budgeting, separate line items, quarterly review. I'd rate this around 82-85. It's not a 90+ because it's somewhat generic in its specifics (no concrete percentages, no methodology for determining the right allocation), but as a framework proposal that would then be fleshed out in implementation, it's strong.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 84.\n",
