s? From my vantage point:\n- It does serve broader scientific impact through improved reproducibility and reduced overhead.\n- It doesn't directly advance the knowledge frontier — it's enabling infrastructure.\n- Its intellectual merit as a research contribution is low, but its value as a research-enabling tool is moderate to good.\n- The \"prerequisite for compute allocation\" could be seen as either helpful (standardization) or stifling (bureaucratic gate).\n\nI think this clears my bar at a modest level. It's not a research breakthrough proposal, but it does serve the broader scientific impact dimension by improving reproducibility and freeing researcher time. The intellectual merit is low (it's engineering), but the question isn't \"is this intellectually novel?\" — it's whether it serves my domain of concern. Reproducibility and reduced overhead do serve broader scientific impact.\n\nI'd say: approve, with a moderate promise score. It's useful infrastructure that supports research but doesn't push the frontier. Maybe 55-65 range. Let me settle on 60. It's genuinely helpful for reproducibility and researcher time, which are real scientific values, but it's fundamentally an engineering project with indirect benefits to my domain.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"This reduces manual overhead while providing the granular data needed for both regulatory audits and scientific reproducibility.\" The dual framing is good — it's not purely compliance-driven; it explicitly claims a scientific reproducibility benefit. The standardized formats could enable cross-project analysis that advances understanding of what works in AI development, which touches on the knowledge frontier indirectly.\n\nI'll approve at 62. It's solid enabling infrastructure with genuine but indirect value to my domain. Not a frontier-pushing idea, but it removes friction and improves reproducibility, which are real scientific goods.\n",
