red by Article 12(2)(a)-(c).\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), Article 12(3) has very specific minimum logging requirements (start/end times, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). The proposal doesn't address these specific requirements.\n- \"Automated\" compliance documentation is good, but the question is whether the automation is auditable itself — a regulator might ask to see how the pipeline works and verify it's not omitting or altering information.\n- The CI/CD trigger approach is sound for development-phase documentation, but Article 12(1) requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system\" — this includes post-deployment operation. The proposal seems focused on the R&D/development phase.\n- However, the proposal does say it \"converts raw R&D logs and version control data\" which suggests it's building infrastructure that could extend to operational logs.\n\n**From my compliance perspective:**\nThis is a strong proposal. It addresses a core obligation (automatic logging/documentation), reduces compliance risk through automation, and builds institutional capability that will be needed regardless of the specific AI system type. The gap-flagging feature is particularly valuable for maintaining continuous compliance posture. \n\nHowever, I note it's somewhat focused on the Recital 71 documentation side rather than the full Article 12(2) event-recording requirements (especially post-market monitoring events and risk identification events). But as a starting infrastructure piece, it's very sound and addresses the most resource-intensive part of compliance.\n\nI would approve this. It directly serves my function's core mission: ensuring we can demonstrate compliance, maintaining audit readiness, and reducing institutional regulatory risk. The automation aspect reduces the probability of non-compliance due to human error or oversight. From a market reputation standpoint, having