ping to \"Recital 71 requirements\" but Recitals are not legally binding in EU law - they provide interpretation context. The actual binding obligations are in Articles 11 (technical documentation), 12 (record-keeping/logging), and Annex IV (contents of technical documentation). However, the substance of what's described does align with Article 12 and Article 11 requirements. This is a minor framing issue but doesn't undermine the proposal's value.\n- The scope seems to cover technical documentation generation rather than the full logging capability required by Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events). Though it mentions \"training logs\" as an input, the actual automatic event logging during system operation (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, etc.) might be a separate engineering effort. However, the pipeline could encompass this if designed properly.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems specifically, Article 12(3) requires specific minimum logging fields. The proposal doesn't explicitly address these but they could be part of the centralized dashboard.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a highly relevant and well-scoped compliance infrastructure investment. It directly addresses multiple requirements in Articles 11 and 12, supports post-market monitoring (Article 72), and protects against regulatory risk. The automation aspect reduces human error in documentation updates - a key compliance failure mode. From my vantage point of institutional survival and regulatory compliance, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure that makes ongoing compliance manageable rather than an endless manual burden.\n\nThe reference to \"Recital 71\" as the mapping target is slightly imprecise (should be Article 11/12/Annex IV), but the substance described aligns well with binding requirements. This doesn't disqualify it - it's a framing choice that the implementation team can correct.\n\nI would approve this. It's directly on-mission for compliance, redu