le 12 requires that high-risk AI systems *technically allow* for automatic recording of events. It's about logging infrastructure, not about detecting risk drift per se. The logging capability is the obligation; what you log and when it triggers relates to Articles 79(1), 72, and 26(5).\n- This proposal conflates \"logging\" with \"risk detection.\" You can comply with Article 12 by having systems that automatically record events (timestamps, inputs, outputs, reference databases used, etc.) without needing a full suite of synthetic edge-case generators. The obligation is to LOG, not to DETECT and ALERT proactively beyond what the system naturally records.\n- That said, there's some overlap: if you want logs that are \"relevant for identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk,\" you do need some notion of when events become relevant. But this can be achieved with simpler threshold-based logging rather than an entire research suite.\n\n**From my dev engineering vantage point:**\n- This is a research program dressed as an implementation proposal. It doesn't give me a clear prototype to build in the next sprint or even quarter.\n- The \"scientific study of model stability\" framing tells me this is exploratory R&D, not a deliverable I can ship.\n- For actual Article 12 compliance, what I need is: structured logging infrastructure (timestamps, event schemas, reference to input data, reference databases used), configured per system type. That's a well-understood engineering problem with clear specs from the regulation itself (especially 12(3) which gives concrete minimum requirements for biometric systems).\n- The proposal over-engineers the solution relative to what the regulation actually mandates. You don't need \"synthetic edge-case generators\" to comply with logging obligations. You need reliable, structured event recording.\n- However, there IS some value in the idea of monitoring agents that flag when behavior approaches thresholds — this