        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen proposal P2758 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through my compliance lens:\n\n**Proposal P2758: Automated Documentation Pipeline for High-Risk Systems**\n\nThe proposal describes a system that automatically generates technical documentation from training logs, dataset metadata, and version control histories, using CI/CD hooks to capture model hyperparameters, validation metrics, and data lineage during every experimental run. It aims to meet Recital 71 requirements in real-time while reducing manual reporting burden on researchers.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events/logs over lifetime) by building logging into the CI/CD pipeline\n- Addresses Recital 71's requirement for technical documentation containing information on \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations of the system, algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\"\n- The mention of capturing \"model hyperparameters, validation metrics, and data lineage\" maps well to what Recital 71 requires in technical documentation\n- \"Real-time\" documentation generation reduces the risk of gaps between what actually happened and what's documented — a major compliance failure mode\n- Reduces manual reporting burden means fewer human errors in documentation (a common source of non-compliance findings)\n- Version control histories provide traceability, which Recital 71 explicitly calls for\n- This is an engineering solution that embeds compliance into the development workflow rather than treating it as an afterthought — much more robust\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal focuses on documentation generation but doesn't fully address Article 12(2)'s requirement about logging events \"relevant for\" identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. The CI/CD hooks capture development-time data but the proposal seems less focused on runtime/logging of the deployed system's events (Article 12(1) says \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which includes deployment).\n- Article 12(3)'s specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics, etc.) — recording of use periods, reference databases, input data matching, identification of natural persons in verification — these are operational/runtime data that a CI/CD pipeline won't capture. The proposal seems to focus on development-time documentation rather than operational logging.\n- However, the proposal does say \"training logs\" which could encompass some runtime data, and it's framed as a broader system.\n- The compliance risk is partial: this addresses the technical documentation aspect well but may leave gaps in the operational logging requirements of Articles 12(2) and 12(3).\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is clearly valuable for compliance. Automated documentation reduces the risk of non-compliance due to missing or outdated technical documentation. Regulators will check that documentation is \"kept up to date\" per Recital 71 — an automated pipeline is far more reliable than manual processes.\n- It supports audit readiness: if a market surveillance authority requests documentation, having it auto-generated from source data means we can produce accurate, complete records quickly.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating that we've built compliance into our development infrastructure (rather than bolting it on) signals maturity to regulators and clients.\n- Institutional survival: reduces the risk of enforcement actions due to documentation gaps.\n\nHowever, I should note this is a partial solution — it addresses the documentation side but