n in a clear and comprehensive form\" require human judgment and narrative writing that no template engine will generate well.\n- So there's a gap between what the proposal claims to automate (the full technical documentation) and what it can realistically do (populate structured data fields like hyperparameters, dataset versions, model architecture). The \"custom template engine\" might just produce a document with filled-in tables but empty or boilerplate narrative sections.\n- That said, even partial automation of the structured-data portions is genuinely useful and reduces developer friction. It's a viable prototype scope.\n- Technical risk: MLflow/W&B metadata schemas can be inconsistent across teams; some experiments may not capture all needed fields. The \"custom template engine\" needs to handle missing data gracefully. This is manageable engineering work, not a research problem.\n- Speed of prototyping: High. You could have an MVP that ingests a single MLflow project and outputs a PDF/markdown with the structured sections filled in within 2-4 weeks. That's very attractive for our team.\n\n**From my specific vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- Technical viability: The core mechanism is viable — reading metadata from experiment trackers and templating it into a document is well-understood engineering. The risk is over-scoping to include narrative content that can't be automated well. But the proposal as stated focuses on \"hyperparameters, dataset versions, and model architecture details\" which are all structured data already in MLflow/W&B. That's technically very viable.\n- Speed of prototyping: Very high. This is a straightforward data transformation + templating task. No novel algorithms needed. Can be prototyped quickly with existing open-source tools (Jinja2, Pandoc, etc.) plus the MLflow/W&B Python APIs.\n- Quality: The output quality for structured fields will be high and deterministic. The risk is if we expect it 