l documentation (Recital 71) but doesn't fully address Article 12(1)'s automatic logging requirement or Article 12(3)'s specific logging for Annex III 1(a) systems. However, the proposal is specifically scoped to technical documentation pipeline, not the full record-keeping obligation. That's fine — it's one piece of a larger compliance picture.\n- The \"custom documentation generator\" still requires development effort. But given we already have MLflow/W&B data structured, this is more of a templating/formatting layer than building from scratch.\n- Risk: if the mapping to Recital 71 categories is done poorly or incompletely, we could end up with a system that looks compliant but misses elements. However, this is manageable with proper QA.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point:\nThis is a highly resource-efficient proposal. It uses existing infrastructure (MLflow/W&B), reduces recurring manual labor costs (researcher hours spent on compliance reporting), and addresses a clear regulatory requirement in a bounded way. The ROI is strong because the \"cost\" is relatively low (a documentation generator layer) while the savings are recurring (every iteration of development no longer requires manual report compilation). This aligns perfectly with my mandate of resource-allocation efficiency and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe promise score should be high — maybe 82-88. It's not a 100 because:\n- The \"custom documentation generator\" still has non-trivial development cost\n- It only covers part of the Article 12/Recital 71 obligation (technical documentation, not full logging capabilities)\n- There's some risk in the mapping completeness\n\nBut it clears my bar easily. I'd approve this.\n\nLet me settle on a score: 84. Strong resource efficiency play, uses existing infrastructure, bounded scope, clear regulatory alignment. Not perfect because of the development cost and partial coverage, but very compelling from my vantage point.\n",
