rence databases or matched data. However, this might be a high-level summary and the actual implementation could cover more.\n- \"Without requiring researchers to write custom logging code for every experiment\" — this is good for standard events, but Article 12(2)(a) requires capturing situations that \"may result in the high-risk AI system presenting a risk.\" Some of these may be domain-specific and require project-level customization beyond what a generic middleware can capture.\n- The proposal says it's for \"all high-risk projects\" — this is good scope alignment.\n- As a compliance officer, I care about: Is this auditable? Does it create a single source of truth? Can we demonstrate to regulators that our logging is systematic and complete? A unified middleware answers yes to all three.\n- The fact that it's \"baked into base environment images\" means it's harder to circumvent — good for compliance assurance.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is a strong compliance investment. Article 12 is a mandatory technical requirement with no opt-out. If we get logged (pun intended) in an enforcement action for missing or inconsistent logs, that's a significant regulatory penalty and reputational damage. A unified middleware:\n- Reduces the probability of non-compliance through standardization\n- Makes audits cleaner and faster\n- Creates institutional knowledge about what events are being captured\n- Scales as we add new high-risk projects without each team reinventing logging\n\nThe risk is that it's not perfectly tailored to every Article 12(3) requirement, but the architecture (standardized API + storage layer) provides the right foundation. The specific fields can be configured per project type.\n\nI would approve this. It directly addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement in a systematic way. From my compliance perspective, having a single, auditable logging infrastructure is far superior to r