        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2177\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 is an implementation obligation: we must technically deploy automatic logging capabilities and maintain them across the system's lifetime, not publish a theoretical taxonomy of what 'appropriate traceability' means. While a rigorous framing of the 'appropriate to the intended purpose' standard in Art. 12(2) could marginally inform our engineering scoping, that analytical work is far better absorbed into the compliance-engineering build itself; committing scarce implementation resources to an interdisciplinary research study that produces no logging infrastructure, no audit-ready documentation, and no enforceable compliance artifact does not move the needle on regulatory readiness or reduce exposure under a future Art. 79/16 enforcement action.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 25\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate proposal P2177 from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\nP2177: \"Formalizing Traceability Metrics for High-Risk AI Systems. A theoretical study to define what an appropriate level of traceability actually means in a rigorous scientific context. We will conduct an interdisciplinary analysis comparing current logging standards with the needs of post-market monitoring and risk identification. It positions our lab as a thought leader in AI governance, increasing our broader scientific impact.\"\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance officer perspective:\n\n**What it does:**\n- A theoretical study to define what \"appropriate level of traceability\" means rigorously\n- Interdisciplinary analysis comparing current logging standards with needs of post-market monitoring and risk identification\n- Positions lab as thought leader in AI governance\n\n**Compliance assessment (my vantage point):**\n\nArticle 12(2) says \"In order to ensure a level of traceability of the functioning of a high-risk AI system that is appropriate to the intended purpose of the system...\" — this is actually a somewhat open-textured standard. The Act does give some minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems, but for other high-risk systems, \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" is indeed a flexible standard.\n\nHowever, from my compliance officer perspective:\n- This is a THEORETICAL study. It doesn't produce an implementable logging system, doesn't create compliance artifacts, doesn't satisfy any specific regulatory obligation.\n- The EU AI Act is now in force (entered into force August 2024, with phased application). We need to be IMPLEMENTING logging capabilities, not studying what they theoretically mean.\n- A theoretical study doesn't reduce our compliance risk. If a regulator audits us, having a paper on \"what traceability means\" won't substitute for actually implemented logging systems that meet Article 12 requirements.\n- The \"thought leader\" positioning is nice for reputation but doesn't address the core survival/compliance concerns.\n- It commits scarce implementation resources to an academic exercise rather than building actual compliance infrastructure.\n- However, there IS a marginal argument: understanding what \"appropriate\" means could inform our engineering decisions about how much logging to build. But this could be done as part of the engineering/compliance work itself without dedicating a full research project.\n\nThe proposal explicitly says it's a \"theoretical study\" and positions the lab for \"broader scientific impact.\" This is fundamentally an academic/reputational play, not a compliance implementation play. As compliance officer, my concern is that we actually BUILD what Article 12 requires. A paper doesn't build anything. It doesn't create logs, doesn't implement automatic recording, doesn't satisfy traceability requirements.\n\nThe \"appropriate level of traceability\" language in Art. 12(2) does leave room for interpretation, and a rigorous definition could help us defend our logging implementation choices before regulators. But this is a marginal benefit that could be captured in a much smaller internal memo rather than a full interdisciplinary research study.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal is well-intentioned but misaligned with compliance priorities. It's an academic exercise that doesn't produce deliverables that directly satisfy regulatory obligations. The resources would be better spent actually implementing the logging infrastructure. The reputational benefit is real but secondary to actual compliance. I'd lean toward reject — it doesn't clear my bar of \"does this help us comply, survive regulation, and protect our market position?\" A theoretical study at best marginally informs implementation choices; at worst, it's a distraction from building what's actually required.\n\nPromise score: Maybe 25-30. There's some marginal value in understanding the standards landscape, but it's not something I'd push resources toward when we need to be building actual compliance systems. It's not harmful, just misallocated for my function.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise around 25.\n",
