t doesn't specifically address the minimum requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end times, reference databases, input data matching, verification person identification).\n- However, the proposal is scoped as a \"technical documentation pipeline\" which is more aligned with Recital 71's documentation requirements than the logging requirements of Article 12. These are related but distinct obligations.\n- The proposal says \"without manual effort\" — in reality, some human oversight will be needed for compliance sign-off. But this is a minor point about framing.\n- CI/CD hooks extracting hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation results are directly relevant to the documentation requirements (algorithms, data, training, testing, validation processes).\n\n**Assessment from Compliance Officer vantage:**\nThis is a strong proposal that addresses a genuine regulatory requirement. The obligation to keep technical documentation up-to-date throughout the lifetime of the system is explicit in Recital 71 and implied by the broader compliance framework. Automation reduces the risk of documentation drift, which is one of the most common findings in regulatory audits. It supports institutional survival (avoiding penalties) and market reputation (demonstrating robust compliance).\n\nHowever, I should note it doesn't fully cover all of Article 12's requirements (particularly the automatic event logging in para 1-2 and the specific minimums in para 3), but that's not a requirement for this proposal to be approved — it addresses one important aspect.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- It directly supports regulatory compliance (documentation currency)\n- Reduces audit risk\n- Protects institutional survival\n- Supports market reputation by demonstrating systematic compliance\n- The approach is practical and aligned with how R&D labs actually work (version control, experiment trackers already exist)\n\nI would approve this. It's a clear, actionable proposal that addresses a real regulatory gap. The promise score should be high — it's well-aligned with my domain concerns. I'd say around 82-85. It's not a perfect solution to all of Article 12, and \"without manual effort\" is slightly optimistic (compliance sign-off still needed), but the core concept is sound and directly addresses a compliance obligation.\n\nLet me settle on 83 — strong alignment with documentation requirements, practical implementation path, clear risk reduction for audits, but it's one piece of the broader Article 12 picture and the \"without manual effort\" framing slightly overpromises.\n",
