s from manual transcription, faster time-to-compliance for each new model version.\n- It doesn't compete with scientific research budget in a significant way — it's operational infrastructure that enables the lab to remain compliant without eating into research time.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This explicitly builds on what already exists: version control (Git), training logs, CI/CD pipelines. It doesn't require new systems or architectures.\n- Sphinx and Doxygen are standard tools in any engineering org's toolkit. If we're an R&D institution with AI systems, we very likely have some form of these already.\n- The \"real-time\" documentation update is a natural byproduct of the deployment process — no additional data flow needed beyond what's already captured for reproducibility.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Real-time\" might be slightly overstated; it would be per-deployment, not truly real-time. But this is a minor semantic point.\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (Recital 71) but doesn't fully address the logging requirements in Article 12(1)-(3), particularly the automatic recording of events/logs. However, pulling from training logs and version control does partially feed into this. It's not a complete solution to Article 12 as a whole, but it addresses the documentation aspect well.\n- There might be some gaps: the proposal doesn't address all elements of Recital 71 (risk-management system documentation, etc.), but those are harder to automate and may require human input regardless.\n\n**Overall assessment:** This is a high-value, low-cost proposal that directly leverages existing infrastructure to reduce ongoing compliance overhead. From my vantage point on resource efficiency and effective use of R&D infrastructure, this is exactly the kind of pragmatic integration I'd want to see. It converts a recurring manual cost into an automated pipeline step with minimal marginal expense after initial setup. The tools ar