k is manageable because the primary data collection is mandated anyway — you're piggybacking on a compliance obligation.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure**: This is where it shines. The lab already has:\n   - AI systems being developed (the subject matter)\n   - Compliance logging that must be built per Article 12\n   - Research staff who can analyze the data\n   - Existing analytical tools and expertise\n   \n   The \"secure telemetry loop\" is a modest incremental addition to what's already needed for compliance. You're not building parallel infrastructure; you're routing existing compliance data into a research analysis pipeline. This is very good use of existing assets.\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Longitudinal\" implies multi-year commitment. That's a significant resource lock-in. But it also means the findings compound over time, which increases value per unit of ongoing cost after initial setup.\n- The proposal says \"iteratively refine our model safety constraints\" — this creates a feedback loop that improves future systems at low marginal cost once the telemetry infrastructure is in place.\n- The risk: if the deployment environments are customer/partner sites, establishing secure telemetry loops involves coordination costs, legal review (data sharing agreements), and potential pushback from clients. This could slow things down or add unanticipated resource demands.\n- However, Article 12 already mandates these logs exist at the point of deployment. The question is whether they can be *accessed* by the R&D lab for research purposes. That's a legitimate operational concern but not insurmountable.\n\n**Overall assessment**: This is one of those proposals that scores very well on resource efficiency because it converts a compliance cost into a dual-use asset. The scientific ambition is high but bounded and achievable with existing infrastructure plus modest additions. It doesn't require new funding categories or massive headc