        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2328\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This directly operationalises the core mandate of Article 12(1) (automatic recording over system lifetime) at institutional scale, eliminating the single greatest compliance tail-risk in our portfolio: a project whose logging is ad-hoc, incomplete, or forgotten because it depended on individual researcher diligence. A unified interception layer with a central regulatory vault also gives us a defensible, auditable narrative for regulators and enterprise clients ('we have systemic traceability infrastructure'), which protects both institutional survival and market reputation. My principal caveat is that Article 12(3) minimums (reference-database identifiers, verification-personnel IDs for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems) are inherently project-specific; the middleware must expose per-deployment configuration hooks rather than assume a fixed schema, or it will create false confidence while still missing mandatory fields. As an architectural commitment with that extension built in, it is exactly the kind of structural fix my function needs.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 81\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is \"Unified Traceability Middleware\" - a standardized software layer that automatically captures required event logs and metadata across all high-risk AI projects. It acts as a wrapper around model execution to ensure consistent data collection. The engineering team develops a shared internal library that intercepts inputs, outputs, and timestamps, piping them into a secure regulatory vault. This removes the need for researchers to write custom logging code per experiment.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths from a compliance standpoint:**\n1. Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" A unified middleware directly addresses this requirement by ensuring automatic logging is built into the infrastructure rather than left to individual researchers.\n2. Article 12(2) requires traceability appropriate to intended purpose. A standardized layer ensures consistent capture across all projects, reducing the risk that a particular project team's logging is inadequate or inconsistent.\n3. Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics) has specific minimum requirements - a unified system can be designed to capture all these fields by default, ensuring no project inadvertently misses them.\n4. Recital 71 emphasizes keeping records throughout the lifetime of the system and technical documentation kept up to date. A centralized vault supports this.\n5. From an institutional survival standpoint: if a regulator audits us, having consistent, uniform logging across all projects dramatically reduces compliance risk. No project is left behind because a particular researcher forgot to implement logging.\n6. Market reputation: being able to demonstrate to regulators and clients that we have systematic, unified traceability infrastructure shows organizational maturity and commitment to compliance.\n\n**Concerns/Risks:**\n1. \"Wrapper around model execution\" - this could be problematic if the middleware introduces latency or changes behavior in ways that affect system performance. But from a pure logging standpoint, it's reasonable.\n2. The proposal says it intercepts \"inputs, outputs, and timestamps\" - but Article 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" A generic wrapper might not capture project-specific metadata like which reference database was used. This would need to be configurable per project type.\n3. The proposal says it removes the need for researchers to write custom logging code - but some logging requirements are inherently domain-specific (e.g., Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification). A pure middleware wrapper can't capture all of this without project-level configuration.\n4. Single point of failure risk: if the middleware fails, ALL projects lose logging simultaneously. This is a resilience concern.\n5. The \"secure regulatory vault\" language is good - it addresses data protection and integrity concerns.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a strong compliance-oriented proposal. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) (automatic recording) in a systematic, institution-wide way. The biggest risk from my vantage point is that a \"one-size-fits-all\" wrapper might not capture all project-specific requirements (especially Article 12(3) specifics), but as an architectural foundation it's sound. It reduces the probability of compliance gaps across projects, which is exactly what a Compliance Officer cares about - minimizing tail risk of non-compliance at any given project.\n\nFrom institutional survival: if one project gets audited and their logging was ad-hoc or incomplete, that's a potential enforcement action. A unified system dramatically reduces this risk surface.\n\nFrom market reputation: being able to say \"we have institutional-grade traceability infrastructure\" is a strong signal to regulators and business partners.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's not perfect (it needs domain-specific extensions for Article 12(3) fields), but as an architectural commitment, it directly serves the compliance function's core mission. I'd approve it with moderate-high confidence, noting that implementation will need project-type-specific configuration layers on top.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely valuable from my perspective. It addresses the #1 structural risk in our compliance posture (inconsistent logging across projects). But it's not a complete solution by itself - it needs domain-specific extensions. I'd say 78-82 range. Let me settle on 80.\n",
