f prototyping concerns:**\n- This is explicitly framed as a \"scientific study\" and \"research\" proposal, not an engineering deliverable. It won't produce a shippable prototype on any reasonable timeline.\n- The scope is extremely broad: novel compression + interpretability + forensic audit tools + publishing findings. This would consume significant researcher time without producing deployable logging infrastructure.\n- For meeting Article 12 requirements NOW, we need practical logging pipelines (structured event logs, timestamps, input/output capture, system state snapshots). This research doesn't directly deliver that.\n- The \"trade-off between logging granularity and computational performance\" is a real concern but it's already well-studied in systems engineering; publishing on it isn't a technical viability concern for our prototyping pipeline.\n\n**Does it address the regulatory need?**\n- Article 12(1) requires automatic recording of events (logs). This is an engineering task, not a research problem.\n- Article 12(2) specifies what logs should capture - situations presenting risk, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operations. These are event-level logs, not activation-level traces.\n- Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires specific fields (timestamps, reference databases, input data matches, person identification). None of these require \"high-dimensional activation logging.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, this proposal bets on solving an open research problem (interpretable compression of high-dimensional activations) as if it will yield practical tools for regulatory compliance. The premise that Article 12 requires \"deep traceability\" beyond event-level logs is not well-supported by the actual text. The regulation calls for event logging with specific content requirements, not activation-level provenance.\n\nFrom a prototyping speed perspective, this is a multi-year research bet that won't 