lready have some versioning/registry infrastructure (like an internal artifact repository), extending it is cheaper than building new.\n- The proposal mentions \"snapshots of the system's general characteristics and limitations\" - this is somewhat vague on how automated that truly can be. Limitations are often qualitative judgments that resist full automation. But the pipeline could at least flag when a snapshot needs human review.\n- Scope: it covers versioning, data lineage, training hyperparameters, general characteristics, and limitations. That's a moderate scope - not trivial but not enormous. It's a focused engineering project.\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency assessment:**\n- One-time build cost vs. recurring savings in manual documentation effort. For a lab with multiple AI systems under development, the ROI is clear.\n- Uses existing CI/CD infrastructure rather than requiring new platforms.\n- Reduces the \"compliance tax\" on researchers who would otherwise be pulled away from scientific work to update documents.\n- The real-time aspect means compliance is maintained continuously rather than as a periodic scramble before audits.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This doesn't compete directly with research compute or hiring for new scientific projects. It's infrastructure that protects the investment in all other projects.\n- Without it, every high-risk system we develop would need dedicated documentation staff, which scales linearly with number of systems. Automation makes compliance scale sub-linearly.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- CI/CD pipelines already exist for ML model deployment. Extending them to also generate compliance artifacts is a natural and low-overhead addition.\n- Config files, version control, and build metadata are already structured data in most modern ML labs - the parsing layer is straightforward engineering.\n\nOverall: This is a well-scoped proposal that addresses a 