        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2250 from the perspective of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\nP2250: \"Empirical Benchmarks for Substantial Modification\" - This proposes conducting a study to define quantitative thresholds that trigger record-keeping requirements for substantial modifications. It involves sensitivity analyses across model suites to determine which parameter shifts lead to significant behavioral changes, then mapping these to regulatory risk categories.\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n\n**Technical viability:**\n- The core idea is technically sound in principle: running sensitivity analyses across models to identify which parameter changes cause significant behavioral shifts is a well-understood ML engineering practice.\n- However, the scope is extremely broad. \"Across our model suites\" could mean dozens or hundreds of models with varying architectures. This is not a quick prototyping task—it's a massive research/analysis effort.\n- The mapping to \"regulatory risk categories\" is where technical viability becomes questionable. There are no well-defined regulatory risk categories in the AI Act that map cleanly onto parameter shifts. Article 12 mentions \"substantial modification\" but doesn't define quantitative thresholds for what constitutes one. So we'd be trying to reverse-engineer a legal concept into engineering parameters, which is fundamentally a legal/interpretive exercise disguised as an empirical one.\n- The proposal claims to \"replace subjective legal interpretations with empirical evidence,\" but the AI Act's definition of substantial modification is inherently contextual and purpose-dependent (it relates to the intended purpose). A single quantitative threshold won't capture this.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is a research study, not a prototype or implementation. It doesn't directly produce a deployable logging system that satisfies Article 12.\n- The time investment would be substantial: running sensitivity analyses across model suites requires significant compute resources and engineering time that could otherwise go toward actually building the logging infrastructure required by Article 12(1).\n- The output—quantitative thresholds—is of uncertain utility because the regulator has not defined what \"substantial modification\" means quantitatively. We'd be creating benchmarks that may not align with how authorities interpret the regulation.\n- This doesn't directly help us meet the technical requirements: automatic recording of events, logging capabilities for traceability, etc. It's tangential to the actual engineering work needed.\n\n**Key concern from my perspective:** The proposal is essentially a legal interpretation exercise wrapped in an empirical methodology shell. As a development engineer, I need to build systems that log events automatically (Art 12(1)), implement logging capabilities for specific traceability needs (Art 12(2)), and meet minimum logging requirements (Art 12(3)). This proposal doesn't directly advance any of those engineering deliverables. It's a \"nice to have\" research project that diverts scarce implementation resources without producing a concrete technical artifact that satisfies the regulation.\n\nAdditionally, the claim that it provides \"a scientific basis to defend our development cycle against excessive and arbitrary re-certification demands\" is speculative. The AI Act doesn't currently mandate \"re-certification\" in the way described—substantial modification triggers obligations but the specific process is still being developed by standards bodies (CEN/CENELEC). Investing heavily in this now may produce work that's premature or