ototyping that never becomes a product.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- Building a \"universal middleware layer\" across \"various AI architectures\" requires significant upfront investment. This competes with actual R&D work.\n- The proposal conflates the compliance requirement (logging for high-risk systems) with an over-engineered solution (every experiment, all architectures).\n- A more efficient approach might be: implement logging hooks in our specific production pipeline for the systems that will actually be classified as high-risk, rather than building a universal platform.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal suggests \"integrated into our training pipelines\" — this implies we already have training pipelines. Good. But does integrating a middleware layer disrupt them? What's the cost/benefit of retrofitting vs. building alongside?\n- \"Universal\" across \"various AI architectures\" suggests it may not leverage what we already have but instead imposes a new standard on everything.\n- However, if we already have versioning and experiment tracking infrastructure (like MLflow, W&B, etc.), the question is whether this proposal builds on those or replaces them.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core need is real — Article 12 requires automatic logging for high-risk systems. Having a shared, lab-wide approach to compliance logging IS more resource-efficient than each team doing it independently. However, the scope as described (\"universal,\" \"every single experiment,\" \"various AI architectures\") significantly over-invests relative to what's actually required. The regulation applies to high-risk AI systems in production/deployment, not to every exploratory experiment.\n\nThe proposal has a valid kernel (shared compliance logging infrastructure) but is over-scoped in a way that would consume disproportionate R&D resources. It risks becoming a 18-24 month engineering project when a more targeted a