vant for\" certain purposes — it's about the capability to record and make available, not necessarily an AI-powered detection layer.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- This is a research-heavy project. Training a supervisory model on log data requires: (1) having sufficient diverse log data from deployed systems, (2) labeling what constitutes \"risk\" vs normal operation, (3) training compute, (4) validation that the detector actually catches relevant anomalies without excessive false positives, (5) ongoing maintenance as systems evolve.\n- The lab is an R&D institution — building AI monitoring tools might be somewhat in-domain, but this is a compliance infrastructure project, not a core research ambition. It's a means to an end (compliance), and there are cheaper ways to achieve the same regulatory outcome.\n- \"Protects our institutional reputation by detecting systemic failures before they become public or legal liabilities\" — while true, this is somewhat speculative value-add beyond what Article 12 strictly requires.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Does the lab already have log monitoring capabilities? Most deployed AI systems already have some form of logging and monitoring infrastructure (Grafana, Prometheus, ELK stack, etc.). The question is whether a bespoke ML-based detection engine adds value beyond what existing observability tools provide.\n- Building a separate supervisory model duplicates functionality that could potentially be achieved with rule-based alerts on the same log streams, or by extending existing MLOps monitoring pipelines.\n- However, if the lab already has the compute infrastructure and expertise in anomaly detection (common in R&D AI labs), the marginal cost might be lower than it appears.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe proposal conflates \"satisfying Article 12(2)\" with building a full ML-based detection engine. Article 12(2) requires that logging capabilities *enable* recording 