oward reject, because it over-commits resources for a compliance requirement that has simpler solutions. The \"separate supervisory model\" is a research project in itself with uncertain ROI on the specific compliance goal. As Lab Director focused on resource efficiency and not letting scientific ambition outpace funding constraints, this is scope creep dressed as compliance necessity.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The lab IS an R&D institution that develops AI systems. They have expertise in building ML models. The marginal cost of adding one more monitoring model to their pipeline might be lower than I'm assuming. And if they're deploying multiple high-risk systems, the monitoring need is real and growing. A centralized intelligent monitoring layer would scale better than rule-based alerts as the system portfolio grows.\n\nBut still — the framing is that this \"proactively satisfies Article 12(2).\" That's a mischaracterization. Article 12(2) requires the *high-risk AI systems* to have logging capabilities. It doesn't require a separate monitoring engine watching those logs. The monitoring requirement in Article 12(2)(b) is about the logs being structured and available to facilitate post-market monitoring — not that you need an AI to do the monitoring for you.\n\nI'll reject but give it a moderate promise score (maybe 40-50) because the underlying capability has value, just not at this scope/resource commitment as framed, and not primarily as a compliance mechanism.\n\nLet me settle on: reject, promise 38. The core utility exists but is over-engineered for the regulatory purpose stated, commits disproportionate R&D resources, and mischaracterizes what Article 12(2) actually requires of the system's technical architecture vs. an external monitoring tool.\n",
