        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1332\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core engineering loop (stream-ingest logs → compute rolling statistics → compare to stored validation baseline → threshold check → fire alert) is well-trodden territory with existing toolchains (Kafka/Flink, statistical drift detectors, event-driven alerting), so a functional prototype is buildable within weeks once the logging pipeline emits structured events. The main technical risk is that 'predefined thresholds' and 'live telemetry against validation baseline' are left underspecified—metric selection, baseline serialization format, false-positive management for alert fatigue, and the latency/throughput cost of a genuinely real-time layer all require non-trivial design work before the first line of code—but none of these are blockers to a credible v1.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 68\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1332 is an \"AI Risk Detection and Alerting System\" — essentially a real-time monitoring tool that:\n- Analyzes logs in real-time\n- Detects substantial modifications or risk triggers\n- Compares live telemetry against original validation baseline\n- Triggers internal compliance alerts when performance shifts exceed predefined thresholds\n\nLet me assess this from a technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- Real-time log analysis is well-understood engineering. Streaming processing frameworks (Kafka, Flink, etc.) exist. This is technically feasible.\n- Comparing live telemetry against a validation baseline — this requires defining what \"performance shifts\" means quantitatively. For ML systems, drift detection is an established field (statistical tests on feature distributions, prediction distributions, etc.). Feasible but the threshold-setting problem is non-trivial.\n- The \"predefined thresholds\" part is where it gets tricky. What constitutes a \"substantial modification\" vs normal variance? This requires domain-specific calibration per system.\n- Triggering alerts when thresholds are exceeded — straightforward engineering (event-driven architecture, webhook/queue-based alerting).\n\n**Speed of Prototyping:**\n- A basic version could be prototyped relatively quickly: ingest logs → compute rolling statistics → compare to baseline → threshold check → fire alert. Maybe 2-4 weeks for a functional prototype depending on existing infrastructure.\n- However, the \"real-time\" aspect and robustness requirements (no missed alerts, no false positives drowning out signals) add complexity.\n- The comparison against \"original validation baseline\" requires that baseline to be well-defined and stored in an accessible format. If our prototyping pipelines don't already output structured baselines, that's additional work.\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- False positive rate management is critical. Too many alerts → alert fatigue → engineers ignore them → the system becomes useless.\n- The proposal says \"transforms passive record-keeping into a proactive safety mechanism\" — this is good framing but also scope creep beyond Article 12's actual requirements. Article 12 requires logging capabilities; it doesn't mandate real-time detection/alerting as a separate system. However, having such a capability would genuinely support the traceability and monitoring goals in Recital 71.\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level — \"predefined thresholds\" without specifying how they're set, what metrics are compared, etc. But for an ideation exercise, that level of detail might be acceptable.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering standpoint:\n- Technically viable: Yes, this builds on well-known patterns (stream processing, drift detection, alerting).\n- Prototype speed: A functional MVP is achievable in a reasonable timeframe (weeks to a couple months depending on integration complexity with existing logging infrastructure).\n- Quality risk: The main quality challenge is tuning thresholds and managing false positives. This is an operational concern more than a build concern.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic — it doesn't specify which metrics, what \"telemetry\" means concretely for our AI systems, or how the baseline comparison would work technically (statistical tests? distance metrics? ML-based anomaly detection?). But as a direction, it's sound.\n\nThe main concern from my vantage: The proposal is somewhat vague on technical specifics. It says \"compares live telemetry against the original validation baseline\" but doesn't specify HOW — what metrics, what comparison method, what constitutes the baseline format. For a development team to pick this up, they'd need to do significant design work before building. However, that's normal for any proposal at ideation stage.\n\nAnother concern: \"real-time\" is doing a lot of heavy lifting here. If our current logging pipeline has latency (batch processing, async writes), adding a real-time layer on top adds infrastructure complexity and cost. But again, this is manageable.\n\nOverall: This is a technically feasible proposal that aligns well with the spirit of Article 12 (traceability, monitoring). It's a reasonable engineering project with clear deliverables. The prototype risk is moderate — the core loop (ingest → compare → alert) is straightforward; the hard part is tuning and reducing false positives, which is iterative but not blocking for an initial prototype.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for approval from a development viability standpoint. It's buildable, the scope is reasonably bounded, and it produces tangible value. My promise score would be moderate-to-good — maybe 65-72. It's solid but the technical vagueness and the \"real-time\" requirement add some execution risk that keeps me from being fully enthusiastic.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, with a promise of around 68.\n",
