ledge frontier\" here is not particularly clear. Adaptive sampling and compression for telemetry are established fields. Applying them to AI system logging is incremental at best.\n- The \"broader scientific impact\" is limited — this would primarily benefit practitioners deploying high-risk AI systems, not advance fundamental understanding.\n- There's a risk that the \"lossy\" aspect conflicts with the regulatory requirement itself. Article 12 says logs must enable identifying situations presenting risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations. If you've compressed away the critical details, you haven't satisfied the regulation — you've just made it cheaper to pretend compliance.\n\n**Intellectual merit assessment:**\n- The proposal conflates engineering optimization with scientific research. Benchmarking trade-offs is valuable but not deeply novel science.\n- The adaptive sampling idea has some interesting theoretical underpinnings (when can you safely sample a stream without losing critical events?), but this would need much more rigorous framing to be truly at the knowledge frontier.\n- The \"lossy compression for AI telemetry\" angle is interesting from an information-theoretic standpoint, but as framed, it reads more like an applied engineering project than a research inquiry.\n\n**Decision:** From my vantage point on knowledge frontier and intellectual merit, this is a borderline case. It touches on real technical questions but frames them in a way that suggests the \"research\" contribution would be incremental application of known techniques to a new domain (AI system logs). The fundamental contradiction between lossy compression and traceability requirements undermines the premise without being addressed. The broader scientific impact is limited. I'd lean toward reject — it's more engineering optimization than research advancing the frontier, and the core tension (lossy vs. full fidelity for legal compliance) isn't acknowledged as a