        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2295\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core mechanism—using off-the-shelf MLflow/DVC pipelines to auto-generate a version-controlled audit trail of models, data provenance, and training metadata—is proportionate to the Article 12/Recital 71 requirement and can be layered onto infrastructure we likely already maintain (Git-based repos, experiment-tracking stubs), keeping incremental build cost low. However, the 'real-time' framing for risk-assessment linkage is over-engineered: qualitative risk documents don't fit a streaming pipeline, and mandating researcher workflow changes via automated scripts introduces hidden ongoing operational tax (debugging broken hooks, training overhead, friction with research velocity) that will erode the efficiency gains unless scoped tightly.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 70\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Lab Director concerned with resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal is P2295: \"Living Technical Documentation System\" - a dynamic repository linking model versions to training data, validation logs, and risk assessments in real time, using MLflow or DVC for version-controlled documentation pipelines, with automated scripts during training cycles.\n\nLet me assess this from my resource-allocation efficiency perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- MLflow and DVC are already widely available open-source tools that many R&D labs (including likely us) already have in their infrastructure stack. This means the marginal cost of implementation is relatively low compared to building custom solutions.\n- It addresses a genuine regulatory requirement (Recital 71, Article 12) - technical documentation kept up to date throughout the AI system's lifetime.\n- \"Automated scripts during training cycles\" reduces ongoing maintenance burden - you don't want researchers manually updating documents.\n- Version-controlled approach aligns with existing R&D workflows (Git-based versioning is standard in ML research).\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- The proposal says \"real time\" which could be over-engineering. Article 12 requires records to be kept and up to date, but doesn't necessarily mandate real-time synchronization. Real-time infrastructure adds complexity and cost (streaming pipelines, event-driven architectures) that may not be justified by the regulatory text.\n- \"Researchers will be required to update these logs via automated scripts\" - this is a workflow change that has hidden costs: training time, potential friction with research velocity, debugging when automation breaks, etc. This isn't just an infrastructure cost; it's an ongoing operational tax on the research