al doesn't identify a genuine open research problem in AI safety monitoring or interpretability.\n\n**Intellectual merit:** As a research contribution, this is thin. It's an implementation exercise — building a product to satisfy a regulatory requirement. The intellectual content is largely: \"apply existing log analysis and anomaly detection techniques to the specific context of high-risk AI system logs.\" There's no novel methodology, no new theoretical framework, no open scientific question being addressed. The proposal frames it as \"fulfilling the post-market monitoring mandate\" — this is a compliance engineering task, not a research endeavor.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:** A dashboard that alerts on log anomalies for AI systems doesn't advance the broader science of AI safety, interpretability, or machine learning theory. It's a narrow operational tool. The \"broader impact\" would be limited to other compliance engineers looking for inspiration. It doesn't generate new knowledge about how AI systems fail, what makes them risky, or how to fundamentally understand their behavior.\n\nHowever, I should consider: is there any research value? One could argue that developing ML-based triggers specifically tuned to Article 79 risk categories might involve some interesting challenges — but the proposal as stated doesn't articulate any novel research question. It reads as a product spec for an internal tool. The \"heuristic and ML-based\" language suggests a practical engineering approach, not a research investigation into new methods.\n\nAlso worth noting: the proposal says \"we will develop a set of heuristic and ML-based triggers.\" This is vague on the actual scientific content. What would make this scientifically interesting? Perhaps understanding which features in AI system logs are most predictive of downstream risk? But that's not what the proposal describes — it describes building a dashboard with alerts.\n\nFrom my vantage point as a researcher concerned 