        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2097\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly implements the non-discretionary 'shall' in Article 12(1) by mandating a single, consistent logging middleware across all high-risk pipelines, which is the most defensible architecture for demonstrating systemic compliance to regulators and for surviving an audit or incident investigation. From my vantage point, the key gaps are that the proposal mentions only 'input data hashes' rather than the full Article 12(3) field set (notably the reference database identifier in 12(3)(b) and the matched input data itself in 12(3)(c)), and it does not address lifetime retention or archival—both of which a notified body or market-surveillance authority will probe. However, these are implementable within the stated architectural commitment and do not undermine the strategic logic of centralising logging as a hard dependency.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 87\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2097 describes a \"Unified Automated Logging Framework\" - a standardized software library/middleware layer integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines to capture mandatory events automatically per Article 12.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is a mandatory (\"shall\") requirement.\n- A unified framework ensures consistency across all high-risk projects, which is critical for compliance because regulators will expect uniform traceability standards.\n- Recording timestamps addresses Article 12(3)(a) - \"recording of the period of each use of the system (start date and time and end date and time of each use).\"\n- Input data hashes could relate to Article 12(2)(a) and (b) for traceability.\n- User IDs address Article 12(3)(d) - \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification of the results.\"\n- Mandating it as a dependency ensures no project can slip through without compliance logging. This is a strong governance control from my perspective.\n- Reduces manual developer effort, which means less risk of human error in logging implementation - important for reliability and audit-readiness.\n\n**Concerns/Questions:**\n- \"Input data hashes\" vs. the actual requirement: Article 12(3)(c) requires \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" - this is specifically for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). A hash might not be sufficient if the regulator wants the actual data, or it might be insufficient for post-market monitoring under Article 72. However, hashes could be a privacy-preserving approach that still enables traceability.\n- \"Reference database\" from Article 12(3)(b) - the proposal mentions \"input data hashes\" but doesn't explicitly mention recording which reference database was used. This is a gap for Annex III 1(a) systems specifically.\n- The framework needs to be \"appropriate to the intended purpose of the system\" per Article 12(2). A one-size-fits-all middleware might not capture all events relevant to different purposes. However, the proposal says it's a framework/library, which could allow for configuration.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - the proposal doesn't explicitly address retention periods or archival, but as a logging framework, it presumably handles this.\n- The compliance documentation requirement (Recital 71) also calls for technical documentation to be kept up to date throughout the lifetime. A unified framework that generates standardized logs would make maintaining this documentation much easier.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is a strong proposal. The \"shall\" in Article 12(1) makes automatic logging mandatory - there's no discretion. Without a systematic approach to implement this across all high-risk systems, we face:\n- Non-compliance risk (fines up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; €15M/3% for other violations including Article 12)\n- Inability to demonstrate compliance during regulatory audits\n- Inability to conduct proper post-market monitoring\n- Reputational damage if a system causes harm and we can't trace what happened\n\nA unified, mandated framework is the most defensible approach from a compliance perspective. It creates a single audit trail, ensures no project escapes logging obligations, and makes it easier to demonstrate systematic compliance to regulators. The \"mandated as a dependency\" aspect is particularly strong - it removes human discretion as a failure mode.\n\nHowever, I should note that the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly cover all of Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems (specifically the reference database element). But this could be seen as an implementation detail within the framework rather than a flaw in the concept. The proposal is about establishing the architecture, and specific data fields can be configured per system type.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value compliance investment that directly addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement. From my vantage point, I would strongly support this. It's not just \"nice to have\" - it's legally required infrastructure. The main risk is if the framework is too rigid or doesn't capture all necessary fields for specific Annex III categories, but as an architectural decision, it's sound.\n\nI'd rate this around 85-90. It's strongly aligned with my mandate. The slight deduction is because:\n1. The proposal doesn't explicitly address the full scope of Article 12(3) (reference database, specific match data for biometric systems).\n2. \"Hashes\" might be insufficient for some regulatory interpretations - a regulator could want more than hashes.\n3. Lifetime logging and retention/storage strategy isn't addressed in the proposal text.\n\nBut these are implementation details that can be resolved during development. The strategic direction is correct and essential. I'll give it an 87.\n\nDecision: Approve. This directly addresses a mandatory \"shall\" requirement and creates institutional resilience against regulatory enforcement.\n",
