        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1235\",\n      \"rationale\": \"From a technical-viability and prototyping-speed standpoint, this over-engineers the compliance problem: Article 12's core requirement is persistent, queryable event logging, which standard structured-logging infrastructure with pseudonymization, hashing of PII fields, and access controls already satisfies alongside GDPR data-minimisation principles; ZKPs and SMPC for arbitrary log-event attestation are months-to-years from deployable prototypes, carry severe throughput/latency overheads that conflict with high-frequency inference logging, and do not produce the actual data trail a regulator or Article 72 post-market monitor would request during an incident. Committing scarce implementation capacity to a research exploration when a simpler engineering path (structured logs + retention policy + pseudonymisation layer) is achievable in weeks means we trade regulatory-timeline certainty for speculative novelty.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 28\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1235 is about developing privacy-preserving logging mechanisms using zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) or secure multi-party computation (SMPC) to satisfy EU AI Act Article 12 traceability requirements while protecting sensitive data under GDPR.\n\nLet me assess this from a technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- ZKPs and SMPC are real technologies, but they are extremely complex to implement correctly for production-grade logging systems.\n- The core problem: Article 12 requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is a straightforward engineering requirement — you need persistent, queryable event logs. The proposal wants to replace this with ZK proofs that \"prove a specific check occurred without storing raw personal data in the log files.\"\n- There's a fundamental tension here: ZKPs prove statements about computation but don't provide the actual log trail that regulators or post-market monitoring (Article 72) would need. If a regulator asks to see what happened during incident X, a ZK proof doesn't help — you need the actual data trail.\n- SMPC for logging is extraordinarily expensive in terms of computational overhead and latency. For high-throughput AI systems logging every inference event, this is likely impractical.\n- The GDPR tension mentioned is somewhat overstated. Article 12 requires logging events relevant to traceability, not necessarily storing all raw personal data in plaintext. You can hash, pseudonymize, or store references rather than full PII. This is a much simpler engineering solution that doesn't require ZKPs.\n- The \"marketable technical advantage\" framing suggests this is more of a research/positioning play than a practical engineering solution to the actual regulatory requirement.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- ZKP systems are notoriously difficult to prototype quickly. Writing custom ZK circuits for arbitrary logging scenarios would take months to years, not weeks.\n- SMPC protocols have significant performance overhead that would need extensive benchmarking.\n- This is a research project, not an engineering solution. The lab needs to implement Article 12 compliance, which requires practical, deployable logging infrastructure within regulatory timelines.\n- A simple structured logging system with appropriate data minimization (hashes, pseudonymization, access controls) would be far more achievable and still satisfy both Article 12 and GDPR.\n- The prototyping timeline for ZK/SMPC solutions is nowhere near compatible with the speed needed to comply with regulatory deadlines.\n\n**Key concerns from my vantage point:**\n1. Over-engineering: The actual compliance requirement (automatic event logging) can be met with standard engineering practices plus data minimization principles. ZKPs and SMPC are solving a problem that doesn't exist at this scale.\n2. Timeline mismatch: These technologies are not ready for rapid prototyping into production systems. We need working solutions within regulatory timelines.\n3. The \"privacy-preserving\" angle is addressable with far simpler techniques (pseudonymization, hashing, access controls, data retention policies) that don't compromise the actual traceability requirement.\n4. The proposal conflates \"we can't store raw PII in logs\" (a GDPR concern) with \"we need ZK proofs to prove events occurred.\" These are different problems with very different solutions and complexity levels.\n5. Risk of over-scoping: Committing scarce implementation resources to a research project that may not produce deployable artifacts within the needed timeframe, when simpler engineering approaches exist.\n\nThis is an interesting research idea but as a response to Article 12 compliance, it's technically over-complex, slow to prototype, and addresses a problem (GDPR tension) that can be solved with much simpler engineering. From my perspective focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping, this doesn't clear the bar for committing implementation resources.\n\nI'll reject this with a moderate promise score — it's not garbage as research, but it's not viable as a near-term engineering deliverable that addresses the regulatory requirement efficiently.\n",
