l — logs must persist for the entire system lifetime. A centralized middleware ensures consistent retention policies.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- Article 12(3) imposes specific minimum logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics): start/end times, reference database, input data matching, and identification of natural persons in verification. The proposal mentions \"timestamps and data references\" but doesn't explicitly address all the Annex III 1(a) specifics. However, this could be addressed in implementation details.\n- The proposal says it hooks into \"existing training and deployment pipelines.\" But Article 12(3)(d) requires identification of natural persons involved in verification (Article 14(5)) — this may not naturally flow from training/deployment pipelines; it's more about operational use and human oversight. A middleware that only hooks into training/deployment might miss the operational logging requirements.\n- The proposal focuses on \"timestamps and data references\" which is somewhat narrow compared to the full scope of Article 12(2)(a)-(c). It needs to capture events relevant to risk situations, substantial modifications, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring.\n- However, as a foundational infrastructure layer, it's a sensible starting point that can be extended. The core idea — standardized automatic logging across all high-risk projects — is exactly what Article 12 demands.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally the right architecture for compliance with Article 12. A single, standardized logging middleware ensures:\n1. Consistency (all systems log the same way)\n2. Completeness (no project can skip logging)\n3. Auditability (regulators can see a uniform trail)\n4. Resource efficiency (one implementation serves all projects)\n5. Lifetime persistence (centralized management of retention)\n\nThe main risk is scope creep or under-scoping — the proposal mentions \"timestamps and data references\" which is narrower than what Article 12(3) requires for Annex III 1(a) systems. But as a foundational platform, this is approvable with the understanding that implementation must cover all specific requirements in paragraphs 2 and 3.\n\nFrom my compliance perspective: I'd approve this. It directly addresses a core mandatory obligation (Article 12(1)), reduces systemic compliance risk by eliminating per-team variability, and builds the institutional infrastructure needed for audit readiness. The \"resource efficiency\" framing also aligns with ensuring we can sustain compliance over time without burning out individual teams.\n\nPromise score: This is a high-value proposal from my vantage point. It's not perfect (the scope description is narrow), but the fundamental architecture is correct and directly maps to a mandatory legal requirement. I'd rate this around 82-85. The slight deduction is because the proposal as described focuses on \"timestamps and data references\" which under-represents the full Article 12(3) requirements, and there's a risk that hooking only into \"training and deployment pipelines\" misses operational-use logging (especially for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems where the human verification step is critical). But the core concept is strongly aligned with my compliance mandate.\n\nI'll go with 83.\n",
