ing the right abstraction for \"system state\" across different model types. But it's absolutely buildable with current technologies.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable proposal that addresses a real regulatory need through standard engineering patterns. It would improve prototyping speed once built (removes manual logging burden) while ensuring compliance. The main risk is execution complexity across heterogeneous pipelines and performance overhead during active development cycles, but these are manageable with good design.\n\nI'd rate this fairly highly. It's not groundbreaking but it's the right kind of infrastructure investment for our domain. Technically viable: yes. Speed/quality impact on prototyping: net positive after initial build cost. I'll approve with a promise score around 78-82. Let me think... \n\nThe proposal is technically sound, addresses a clear need, and uses proven patterns. The main concerns are scope (all experiments vs. just high-risk deployments), integration complexity across heterogeneous pipelines, and ensuring it doesn't become a prototyping bottleneck during active development. But these are implementation details that can be addressed in design. I'll go with 80 — strong approval, technically viable, good fit for our domain, but not without execution risk on the integration side.\n",
