redentials, writes to an append-only store with user ID + timestamp + reference to matched data.\n- The \"immutable\" requirement might slow things down if you want to over-engineer it (e.g., building on blockchain). But a simple hash-chained append-only log is prototypable in days.\n- Dependencies: needs access to the existing authentication/identity system, and needs to know what \"matched input data\" reference looks like in the pipeline.\n\n**Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The scope is narrow enough that a prototype could be high-quality and demonstrably functional quickly.\n- Risk: The proposal says \"immutable record\" but doesn't specify the mechanism. In prototyping, you'd start with a simple append-only table and add cryptographic chaining later. That's fine for a v1.\n- One concern: \"alongside the matched input data\" - this implies the log entry needs to reference or embed the actual data that was matched. If that's large (e.g., biometric templates), storage and privacy considerations kick in. But for prototyping, even just storing a hash/reference is sufficient to demonstrate the concept.\n- The proposal is appropriately scoped to Article 12(3)(d) specifically - it doesn't overreach into 12(1), 12(2), or other parts of 12(3). This makes it a clean, bounded deliverable.\n\n**Potential Issues from my vantage:**\n- \"Immutable\" is a strong word that could lead to over-engineering if not scoped carefully. But as a prototype, you can demonstrate the concept with reasonable effort.\n- The proposal assumes a clear \"final verification step\" exists in the workflow. If the current architecture doesn't have such a clean separation point, integration work adds time.\n- However, for Annex III biometric/identity systems, there IS typically a human-in-the-loop verification step (per Article 14), so this assumption is reasonable.\n\nOverall: This is a technically viable, well-scoped proposal that maps directly to a specific regulatory requirement (Article 12(3)(d)). I