) requires very specific data points: start/end of each use, reference database used, input data matched, and identification of natural persons verifying results. The proposal doesn't mention these at all.\n- \"Over the lifetime\" — how long is that? The proposal says immutable storage but doesn't specify retention periods aligned to system lifecycle.\n- The proposal says \"execution timestamps and system events automatically\" which is somewhat narrow. Article 12(2) requires recording of events *relevant* to specific purposes, not just generic execution logs.\n\n**From my vantage point (compliance, institutional survival, market reputation):**\n- This is a solid foundational building block. It addresses the core technical mandate of 12(1). Without it, we're in clear violation.\n- However, as written, it's incomplete for full Article 12 compliance. A reviewer or regulator would check whether our logs capture the specific event types in 12(2) and (3). If we only have timestamps and generic events, that's a gap.\n- That said, the proposal is framed as a \"framework\" and \"library\" — it could be designed to accommodate the additional fields later. The core architecture (immutable, time-stamped, automatic recording, standardized format) is correct and necessary.\n- From an institutional survival standpoint: having a unified framework is far better than ad-hoc implementations per team. It reduces the risk of a single non-compliant system jeopardizing the institution's standing.\n- Market reputation: demonstrating a coherent, systematic compliance approach (rather than piecemeal patches) is favorable in audits and regulatory interactions.\n\n**Decision:** The core concept is sound and necessary for Article 12(1) compliance. It establishes the right architectural principles (automatic, immutable, standardized, lifetime). While it doesn't fully spell out the 12(2)/(3) specifics, those can be implemented as configuration/extension of the framework. As a foundation, this is essential. I would approve it as the necessary scaffolding, noting that implementation must extend to cover all sub-articles.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My job is to judge strictly on merits. The proposal as written:\n- Addresses 12(1) well (automatic recording, lifetime)\n- Standardization across teams = good for consistent compliance\n- Immutability = good for audit/traceability\n- But it's somewhat thin on the specific event categories required by 12(2)(a)-(c) and 12(3)(a)-(d)\n\nStill, as a \"framework\" proposal that establishes the core capability, it's a necessary and sufficient first step. The gaps in 12(2)/(3) are implementation details within this framework. I'd approve because without this foundation, nothing else works, and the architectural decisions (immutability, standardization, automation, lifetime storage) are all correct from a compliance standpoint.\n\nPromise score: This is genuinely important for compliance survival. But it's not complete as written — it covers 12(1) well but the 12(2)/(3) specifics need to be baked in. I'd say around 78-82. It's clearly the right direction and necessary, but as a standalone it doesn't fully close the Article 12 compliance loop without explicit attention to the specific event categories.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 80.\n",
