tion that satisfies Article 12? What are the technical constraints? Are there compression techniques that preserve all required data points while reducing storage? This information is essential for designing compliant systems.\n\nHowever, I have concerns:\n- The framing of \"lowest cost point\" rather than \"appropriate level per intended purpose\" (Article 12(2) language) is slightly concerning but not disqualifying - it's just the author's shorthand.\n- As a compliance officer, I would want this study to explicitly reference Article 12 requirements and ensure that no optimization crosses below the legal threshold. The proposal says \"meets legal requirements\" which addresses this.\n- This is genuinely useful for compliance: if you don't know your technical limits, you might over-provision (wasting resources) or under-provision (compliance risk). Understanding the landscape helps.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a legitimate engineering-compliance intersection question. As a Compliance Officer, I need to ensure our systems are compliant AND sustainable - if logging infrastructure becomes so expensive that it threatens institutional survival (my domain!), then understanding optimization is prudent. But the key compliance guardrail is that \"meets legal requirements\" must be non-negotiable, not aspirational.\n\nI'll approve this but with moderate promise score - it's useful but not a top priority compared to actually implementing the logging systems and ensuring they meet Article 12. It's a supporting optimization exercise. Maybe 55-65 range. Let me think... \n\nActually, as a Compliance Officer specifically concerned with institutional survival: if logging infrastructure becomes a major cost center that threatens the institution's viability, that IS my concern. But this is an optimization study, not a core compliance implementation. It's useful but secondary to actually having compliant logs in place. I'll say 58-62. Let me go with 60.\n\nWait - let me reconsi