ucture; it maps what exists against regulatory requirements. It's an information-gathering exercise. However, it requires cross-functional coordination (legal + technical) which can disrupt lab workflows.\n\n**Assessment:**\n- This is a prerequisite activity. Without knowing which systems are high-risk and what gaps exist, you cannot efficiently allocate implementation resources for Article 12 compliance. Every other proposal in the candidate set would be shooting in the dark without this foundational knowledge.\n- The cost is bounded: it's a finite exercise (review projects, document gaps, produce register). It doesn't create ongoing infrastructure costs.\n- The benefit is significant: prevents misallocation of scarce resources by identifying exactly where compliance effort is needed.\n- Risk: If the lab has many projects and limited legal/technical review capacity, this could take a long time and delay actual implementation work. But it's still the logical first step.\n- From my perspective as Lab Director concerned with efficiency: this is the most cost-effective way to ensure that subsequent resource allocation for compliance is targeted rather than scattered. It prevents wasting resources on non-applicable projects or under-investing in critical ones.\n\nI would approve this. It's a bounded, one-time (or periodic) investment that directly enables efficient allocation of all other compliance resources. Without it, every other compliance activity risks being misdirected. The promise score should reflect that while I value it highly for its enabling function, it doesn't itself build capability—it's diagnostic. But in terms of resource-allocation efficiency, knowing where to point your limited budget is perhaps the single most important thing a Lab Director needs.\n\nI'll give it around 78-82. It's high-value as a prerequisite, bounded cost, but it's not building infrastructure or advancing science—it's purely administrative/diagnostic. From a pure \"does this make