nstitutional survival because if compliance is too burdensome, researchers will find workarounds or resist, creating gaps\n- Tamper-evident ledger supports traceability (Recital 71 emphasizes traceability) and helps with verifying compliance with the Regulation\n- Captures system state, input data, decision timestamps - aligns with what Recital 71 says should be documented\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal is somewhat high-level. It doesn't explicitly address Art 12(2)(a)-(c) specifics (identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation under Art 26(5))\n- Doesn't specifically address the Annex III point 1(a) minimum requirements in Art 12(3) - but those are for biometric systems specifically\n- \"Tamper-evident ledger\" is good for integrity but I need to consider whether this actually satisfies the full scope of Article 12, which requires logs over the LIFETIME of the system. What about data retention? Storage architecture? Access controls?\n- The proposal says \"baseline compliance\" - from a compliance officer's perspective, I want more than baseline. But for an internal ideation exercise, establishing a foundation is reasonable.\n- \"Without manual developer intervention\" - this is actually very strong from a compliance standpoint because it removes human error as a failure mode\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This is HIGHLY relevant to my domain. Article 12(1) uses \"shall\" - non-compliance means the system cannot be placed on the market in the EU. This is existential for the institution.\n- A standardized framework reduces the risk that individual projects develop ad-hoc logging that doesn't meet requirements\n- The tamper-evident aspect supports our ability to demonstrate compliance to regulators (market reputation)\n- Reducing researcher overhead helps institutional survival because it makes compliance sustainable rather than a tax that erodes R&D ca