uestion — it's an implementation challenge.\n- There's no mention of evaluation methodology beyond \"tested on one pilot project.\" No metrics defined, no baselines, no comparison against existing tools.\n- The framing \"turns a tedious regulatory burden into a scientific opportunity\" is somewhat disingenuous from a research standpoint. It's primarily an internal compliance tool with a thin research veneer. The intellectual contribution would be limited to perhaps a case study or a small technical report.\n- No clear path to publication, no novel dataset creation (the AI Act text is public; code repos are internal), no generalizable methodological contribution.\n\n**Broader Scientific Impact:**\n- The impact would be primarily internal — saving the lab time on documentation compliance for one specific regulation.\n- It doesn't advance the state of software engineering research, NLP research, or regulatory technology (RegTech) in a meaningful way that others could build upon.\n- The \"broader scientific impact\" is minimal. If anything, it's a small applied project that might produce an internal tool, not a contribution to the knowledge base of any field.\n\n**Counterarguments / steelman:**\n- One could argue that mapping natural language regulatory text to structured code artifacts is a non-trivial NLP challenge with some novelty in the specific domain of AI regulation.\n- However, this is a very narrow application, and the proposal doesn't even frame it as needing novel methods — it says \"LLM-based agents,\" suggesting off-the-shelf LLMs with prompting/fine-tuning.\n- The research contribution would be at best a conference workshop paper or an industry report.\n\n**My assessment:** From the knowledge frontier / intellectual merit / broader scientific impact vantage point, this proposal is essentially an applied engineering project for internal compliance that has been reframed as \"research.\" It lacks novel scientific questions, doesn't push any frontier meaningfully, and its broader impact is limited to one institution's documentation workflow. The promise score should be low — maybe 15-25 range. There's a sliver of interest in the regulatory NLP mapping problem, but as proposed, it's thin on research substance.\n\nI'll reject this from my vantage point with a modest promise score reflecting that there's a tiny kernel of something interesting buried under the compliance-tool framing.\n",
