vide pluggable adapters for domain-specific data (like reference database references).\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- PRO: This directly helps prototyping speed by removing the burden from individual research teams to figure out compliance logging each time. A team can spin up a new prototype with built-in logging in minutes rather than days.\n- PRO: Integration into CI/CD means you don't forget to add logging; it's automatic and consistent. This reduces quality issues in production (missing logs, inconsistent formats).\n- CON: There's an upfront investment cost. While the dedicated team builds the framework, no project gets its custom logging done. For a very early-stage prototype that might not end up being high-risk, this could be premature. But the proposal says \"all high-risk AI projects currently under development\" — so it targets things already known to be in scope.\n- CON: Risk of over-engineering or slow delivery if the team tries to make the API too comprehensive for all system types before shipping a v1. This is a classic platform-team risk. But with a \"small, dedicated team\" and a clear regulatory driver, there's good reason to keep scope tight.\n- PRO: From a prototyping perspective, having a standard logging layer means you can iterate on the AI logic without worrying about compliance infrastructure each time. The separation of concerns is clean.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Unified\" is the risky word. If interpreted as \"one monolithic library that must handle every case perfectly before any team can use it,\" this could be slow. But if interpreted as \"a core logging SDK with well-defined extension points for domain-specific events,\" it's very viable.\n- The CI/CD integration is a smart choice — it means compliance isn't opt-in, which addresses the common failure mode of teams forgetting to instrument.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is technically sound and well-scoped. It solves a real problem (redundant effort across teams), has