ference narratives for log data is not justified when the regulatory requirement can be met with straightforward structured logging infrastructure. The scientific ambition here significantly outstrips what's needed, and the proposal doesn't demonstrate that this research would materially improve compliance efficiency or leverage existing infrastructure in a cost-effective way.\n\nHowever, I should note there IS some kernel of value — if the lab is already building logging infrastructure, adding some metadata/semantic tagging might be relatively low-cost and could help with post-market monitoring (which Article 12 does reference). But the proposal as written is much broader than that: it's a full research project on NLP-based narrative generation and causal inference.\n\nDecision: Reject. The scope far exceeds what compliance requires, it's primarily driven by publication ambitions rather than operational efficiency, and it commits scarce resources to an open-ended research question with uncertain payoff. The lab can meet Article 12 obligations through standard structured logging without needing a semantic interpretation layer.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe a 25-30. There's some intellectual appeal and a tiny sliver of potential operational benefit, but from my resource-allocation vantage point, it doesn't justify the commitment. The framing as \"transforming a regulatory requirement\" is misleading — Article 12 can be fully satisfied without any of this. I'll say 25.\n",
