        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P1395 from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**Proposal P1395: Unified Traceability Framework for High-Risk AI**\n\nKey elements:\n- Standardized internal API and middleware for automatic capture of system events, inputs, versioning data\n- Serves as technical foundation for mandatory logging (Article 12)\n- Lightweight logging layer integrating into existing ML pipelines\n- Stores metadata in an immutable ledger\n- Implemented via shared containerized libraries\n- Reduces manual researcher burden\n- Ensures regulatory compliance for all high-risk prototypes\n\n**My professional vantage point: resource-allocation efficiency, balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.**\n\nLet me think about this critically:\n\nPros from my perspective:\n1. \"Integrates directly into our existing ML pipelines\" — this speaks to effective use of existing R&D infrastructure rather than building parallel systems. Good.\n2. \"Lightweight logging layer\" and \"shared containerized libraries\" — suggests a modular, reusable approach that doesn't require massive new infrastructure investment. Resource-efficient.\n3. \"Reduces manual researcher burden\" — frees up researcher time for actual scientific work, which is critical when funding is constrained. Researchers shouldn't be spending hours manually logging events.\n4. Standardization across all high-risk projects means you build once and reuse, rather than each project team building their own ad-hoc logging. This is resource-efficient at scale.\n5. \"Serves as the technical foundation for mandatory logging\" — this is