        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2476\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This proposal directly operationalises the core mechanical mandate of Article 12(1)—automatic, technical log-recording over the system's lifetime—and the standardisation across all high-risk prototypes is precisely the kind of embedded, non-optional control that regulators expect to see during conformity assessments and post-market audits. From a compliance-integrity standpoint, an immutable, middleware-level capture layer removes reliance on individual researcher diligence (a known audit failure mode) and creates a defensible, consistent evidentiary trail; the main gaps are that it does not yet enumerate the Article 12(3)(a)-(d) field-specific requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems or address GDPR-sensitive handling of logged input data, but those are configuration/scope extensions rather than fundamental design flaws in a foundational wrapper.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 84\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to act as a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems under the EU AI Act. I need to screen proposal P2476 from my vantage point: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P2476: Standardized Observability Wrapper for High-Risk Models**\n- A shared software library (Python decorator or API middleware) that intercepts requests to model execution\n- Automatically captures timestamps, input data, and versioning metadata\n- Logs to an immutable database\n- Removes manual logging burden from researchers\n- Ensures consistent compliance across all prototypes\n\nLet me assess this from the Compliance Officer's perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n1. Directly addresses Article 12(1): \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is exactly what an automated logging wrapper does.\n2. Addresses Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) by capturing events relevant to risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring.\n3. The \"immutable database\" aspect supports traceability and integrity of logs — important for regulatory audits.\n4. Standardization across all prototypes is critical: compliance can't be ad-hoc per project; it needs to be systematic. This reduces the risk that one team forgets a logging requirement.\n5. Removing manual burden from researchers means less chance of human error in logging, which directly supports compliance integrity.\n6. Timestamps and versioning metadata align with Recital 71's requirements for \"general characteristics, capabilities and limitations... algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\"\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and versioning metadata\" — but Article 12(3) requires specific things for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): reference database used, input data that led to a match, identification of natural persons involved in verification. A generic wrapper might not capture all these domain-specific requirements without additional configuration per use case.\n2. \"Immutable database\" is good but the proposal doesn't address retention periods (lifetime of the system), access controls for regulators, or format requirements.\n3. The proposal says \"across all prototypes\" — compliance applies to high-risk systems specifically, not all prototypes. Over-logging could create data protection issues (GDPR) if input data contains personal information.\n4. It's a Python-specific solution (decorator/API middleware) which might not cover all model serving stacks in the institution.\n5. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention how it handles the \"lifetime\" aspect — logs need to persist for the entire lifetime of the system, including after decommissioning.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\n- This is a strong structural investment that directly implements a core Article 12 requirement. Without automated logging, the institution would be non-compliant by default for any high-risk system it deploys.\n- Standardization reduces audit risk and demonstrates to regulators (and the market) that compliance is embedded in the development process, not bolted on.\n- The \"removes manual burden\" angle also supports institutional survival because researchers are more productive when compliance tooling handles the tedious parts — this avoids the classic tension where compliance slows down R&D.\n- However, it's a necessary but not sufficient measure. It doesn't fully cover all of Article 12(3)'s specific requirements for certain categories, and it needs to be integrated with broader technical documentation (Article 11), risk management (Article 9), etc.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a high-value proposal from a compliance standpoint. It addresses the core mechanical requirement of Article 12(1) and provides infrastructure that supports multiple other compliance obligations. The standardization aspect is particularly valuable for an R&D institution with multiple teams working on different systems — consistency is what regulators want to see during audits.\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score: it's a strong, directly relevant implementation of a core regulatory requirement. It's not perfect (doesn't cover all Article 12(3) specifics natively, doesn't address retention/GDPR edge cases), but as a foundational infrastructure piece, it's very promising. I'd put it around 82-85. Let me settle on 84 — it's clearly the right direction and directly addresses the letter of Article 12(1) and supports (2), but needs supplementation for full coverage of (3) and integration with broader compliance architecture.\n",
