evelopment cycle. Instead of discovering at audit that log entry (c) \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" isn't being captured, you catch it on every PR merge.\n- Provides a concrete, repeatable technical signal - binary pass/fail per requirement rather than subjective review.\n- The test suite becomes documentation of what \"compliant logging\" means in practice - useful for onboarding and handoff.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- The scope could balloon if you try to cover every possible event type across the system's lifetime. But the proposal specifically targets Article 12's enumerated requirements, which is bounded.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" (Art 12(1)) means logs need to persist - testing that persistence/retrieval works over long timeframes is harder in a CI environment. You'd need to simulate or at least verify the schema supports it. But this is manageable with test fixtures.\n- The probes need to actually trigger the relevant events in the system, which requires some coupling between test infrastructure and production code paths. This is standard integration testing though.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage:**\nThis is a high-viability, low-complexity proposal that directly serves the developer's workflow. It's essentially \"write integration tests for your logging subsystem with assertions mapped to regulatory requirements.\" The technical risk is very low - worst case you discover some events are hard to trigger in test (mitigable with mocks/stubs). The prototyping speed is high because the pattern is well-known. The quality payoff is significant: it converts a vague \"make sure logs comply\" requirement into executable, repeatable checks.\n\nThis clears my bar easily. It's technically sound, quick to prototype, and directly improves the development process. I'd approve this without much hesitation. Maybe not a perfect 100 because there are some implementation details that need work (defining exactly which events map to