ering solution would suffice. We have scarce implementation resources. Spending them on a formal research project with publication goals, rather than building practical logging infrastructure that meets Article 12's requirements, misallocates those resources. The \"intellectual challenge\" framing suggests this is driven by scientific ambition rather than regulatory necessity.\n\n3. **Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** We already have systems in development. The efficient path is to integrate logging capabilities into those existing pipelines — start/end timestamps, reference database references, input data matching records, verifier identification (per Article 12(3)). That's an engineering task using our current infrastructure. A \"formal research project\" with conference publication goals suggests building a separate, parallel effort that doesn't directly serve the compliance timeline or the operational needs of our deployed systems.\n\n4. **The core issue:** The proposal conflates \"logging events in high-risk AI systems\" (a well-understood engineering task — structured event logging, telemetry, audit trails) with something much harder and more speculative (compression of latent space states, novel visualization). Article 12 doesn't require us to log latent space representations or develop novel compression. It requires automatic recording of relevant events: use periods, reference databases, input data matches, verifier IDs. The proposal significantly over-engineers the problem from a resource perspective.\n\n5. **However:** There's a small kernel of utility — if our systems genuinely produce very high-volume logs, some efficiency in storage and retrieval could matter for \"over the lifetime of the system.\" But that's a systems engineering concern, not a research project about latent space compression and visualization tools for conference publication.\n\n**Decision:** Reject. The proposal misallocates scarce implementation resources toward a speculative research program with academic publishing goals when the regulatory requirement is met by standard engineering practices (structured event logging, audit trails, telemetry). It confuses compliance obligation with scientific frontier. From my vantage point on resource efficiency and effective use of infrastructure, this diverts effort from building what we actually need into producing publishable novelty that doesn't serve the regulatory deadline or operational requirements.\n\n**Promise score:** The idea has some tangential relevance (efficient logging at scale is a real engineering concern), but as framed — a formal research project with conference publication goals focused on latent space compression and visualization — it's poorly matched to what Article 12 actually requires from us. I'd give it maybe 15-20. There's a sliver of genuine value in thinking about log storage efficiency over system lifetime, but the framing, scope, and resource commitment are misaligned with my priorities. I'll say 18.\n",
