dizing the approach. The \"maps to specific record-keeping requirements\" language suggests awareness of the detailed obligations. From my vantage point, this significantly reduces the risk that any given high-risk system ships without adequate logging capability — which would be an immediate regulatory violation and potential enforcement action threatening institutional survival.\n\nThe main gap is whether it truly covers ALL sub-requirements (especially 12(3) specifics and 12(2)(a)'s risk-event identification), but the proposal's stated intent to \"map internal system events directly to the specific record-keeping requirements of Article 12\" suggests this is in scope. The commitment to build it implies these details will be worked out.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a foundational compliance infrastructure investment that directly serves institutional survival (regulatory compliance) and market reputation (demonstrable, systematic compliance). The promise score should reflect that while I strongly support the direction, there are implementation details that need to be nailed down to ensure full Article 12 coverage — particularly for Annex III 1(a) systems and the risk-event identification under 12(2)(a).\n\nI'd give it around 82-85. It's a clear \"yes\" from compliance, but I want to note that the generic description leaves some specific sub-requirements (reference database logging, match-specific input data, verification personnel ID) as things that must be explicitly verified in implementation.\n",
