h-risk system we develop.\n- It aligns with Article 12's requirement for \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" — the system already produces these logs; this proposal just channels them into compliance-ready format.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- Building and maintaining an auto-doc engine requires upfront engineering investment. Is that efficient? For a lab developing multiple high-risk systems, yes — it amortizes well.\n- \"Scrapes model configurations\" — the word \"scrapes\" suggests some ad-hoc parsing rather than structured export at the source. This might be less robust than designing structured metadata from the start. However, as an initial implementation that evolves, it's pragmatic.\n- The proposal focuses on technical documentation (Recital 71) more than on runtime logging capabilities (Article 12(1)-(3)). Article 12(1) requires \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system\" — this is about the AI SYSTEM itself having logging capability, not just documentation generation. However, the CI/CD hooks and structured format could support both.\n- Actually, re-reading: Article 12(1) says \"High-risk AI systems shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs)\" — this means the deployed system must log its own runtime events. The proposal seems more about generating documentation from development artifacts rather than ensuring the deployed system has runtime logging. But the \"training logs\" and \"CI/CD pipeline\" aspects do cover the development/training phase, which is what Recital 71 emphasizes.\n- Wait — but my vantage point is resource-allocation efficiency. Even if it doesn't cover ALL of Article 12 (particularly runtime logging in deployment), it covers a significant and expensive portion of the compliance burden: the technical documentation that must be \"kept up to date throughout the lifetime.\" That's a huge ongoing cost saved.\n\n**Decision from my perspective:**\nThis is high