        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2629\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 mandates automatic event logging with specific data fields (especially for Annex III 1(a) systems) and traceability sufficient for post-market monitoring; it does not require causal tracing of internal activation patterns or real-time semantic explanations of why a model made a decision. This proposal is a speculative research project whose deliverables are uncertain and whose technical scope (real-time causal attribution during inference) far exceeds the regulation's concrete logging requirements, meaning scarce implementation resources could be consumed without producing the mandatory log artifacts on which our compliance posture depends. Additionally, if such 'semantic traces' were embedded into documented system behaviour, any failure or inaccuracy in the causal mapping would create a new surface of regulatory and reputational liability that does not exist under straightforward Article 12(3) compliance.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 28\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from the vantage point of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nMy domain of concern: institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2629 is about \"Semantic Logging for Model Interpretability\" — evolving simple event logs into semantic traces that explain why a specific high-risk decision was made, using causal tracing mechanisms mapping internal activation patterns to required regulatory log entries in real time during inference.\n\nLet me assess this strictly against Article 12 and its recital:\n\n**What Article 12 actually requires:**\n- Automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime\n- Recording of events relevant for: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation\n- For biometric identification systems (Annex III point 1(a)): specific minimum data points (period of use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons in verification)\n\n**Assessment from Compliance perspective:**\n\nThe proposal is essentially a research project to go well beyond what Article 12 requires. Article 12 mandates:\n- Technical capability for automatic logging\n- Specific event categories relevant to traceability, post-market monitoring, and operational monitoring\n- For certain systems (biometric ID), specific minimum data fields\n\nWhat the proposal adds: \"semantic traces that explain WHY a specific high-risk decision was made\" via \"causal tracing mechanisms that map internal activation patterns to the required regulatory log entries in real time during inference.\"\n\nThis is significantly beyond what Article 12 requires. The Act asks for logging of events, not causal explanations of internal model activations. While having interpretability could be beneficial, it's not a regulatory requirement under Article 12 specifically. Article 13 deals with transparency and provision of information to deployers, but even that doesn't require real-time causal tracing of activation patterns.\n\n**Compliance risks/concerns:**\n1. This is a research project, not an implementation plan. The lab needs to meet specific regulatory requirements by specific dates. A research project on \"semantic logging\" doesn't directly satisfy Article 12's clear mandates.\n2. The scope creep is significant — \"causal tracing mechanisms that map internal activation patterns\" is far beyond what the regulation asks for. This could consume scarce resources without delivering the actual compliance artifacts needed (the specific log fields, the automatic recording capability).\n3. However, there IS a partial alignment: if you can generate the required logs AND also explain why decisions were made, that could strengthen post-market monitoring capabilities and risk identification (Art 12(2)(a)). But \"real time during inference\" is an enormous engineering challenge that may not be feasible for all model architectures.\n4. The framing as \"transforms a compliance burden into an intellectual contribution\" is appealing for reputation but risky from a survival perspective — if the research doesn't deliver, you've spent resources without meeting minimum requirements.\n\n**From institutional survival:** This is a research project competing with actual implementation needs. Article 12 has clear, specific requirements that need to be met. A speculative research project on causal tracing of activations is high-risk for resource allocation. The regulation doesn't ask for interpretability — it asks for logs. You can comply without this.\n\n**From regulatory compliance:** This goes beyond what's required and introduces complexity. If the \"semantic traces\" become part of your documented system, they become subject to scrutiny themselves. Also, if you claim in documentation that you provide semantic explanations but the mechanism fails or produces incorrect causal attributions, that could be a liability. The regulation asks for specific things; adding speculative capabilities creates more surface area for non-compliance claims.\n\n**From market reputation:** There's some PR value in \"AI transparency\" research, but if it doesn't deliver reliable results, it could backfire — suggesting the lab is overpromising on interpretability. Also, competitors may not have this, so it doesn't create a compliance moat.\n\n**Decision:** I'm leaning toward reject. The proposal:\n- Does not directly implement any specific requirement of Article 12 (the actual logging requirements are simpler and more concrete)\n- Is a research project with uncertain outcomes competing for scarce implementation resources\n- Introduces additional complexity and potential liability surface\n- The \"causal tracing of activation patterns in real time during inference\" is technically very ambitious and may not be feasible or reliable enough to depend on for compliance\n- Complying with Article 12 does NOT require this level of interpretability\n\nHowever, I should note: there's a partial upside. If the lab were already building logging infrastructure, having some semantic layer could help with post-market monitoring (Art 72) and risk identification (Art 79(1)). But as framed — as a standalone research project on causal tracing — it's not clearing my bar for committing scarce implementation resources when the actual regulatory requirement is more straightforward.\n\nI'll reject with a moderate promise score. It has some alignment with the spirit of traceability but is over-scoped, speculative, and doesn't address the concrete requirements efficiently.\n",
