e, verifiable traceability\" — this reframes a compliance requirement as an opportunity for scientific innovation. While that's appealing, it risks over-engineering the solution. The regulation asks for logs; cryptographic hashing is one way but not mandated. Investing research resources in novel provenance methods when existing, well-understood logging infrastructure could satisfy the requirement is a resource allocation concern.\n- \"Most ambitious high-risk projects\" — integrating experimental prototypes into production-critical projects risks disrupting those projects' timelines and introducing instability. This is poor use of existing R&D infrastructure. You don't test unproven cryptographic hashing in your most critical, high-stakes deployments.\n- The proposal conflates compliance (a cost center) with research ambition (potentially a prestige center). While the framing is attractive (\"transforms a compliance burden into publishable scientific contribution\"), from a resource-allocation standpoint, I need to be disciplined: if the goal is compliance, use existing engineering resources and well-established methods. If the goal is novel research, that's a separate budget line with its own justification.\n- The \"small team\" qualifier is reassuring but vague. How small? What's the duration? What's the opportunity cost of pulling those researchers from their current work?\n- From an infrastructure standpoint: if we already have logging infrastructure (most serious R&D labs do), adding a research project to make it \"tamper-proof\" via cryptographic methods is incremental at best, or over-engineering at worst.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal is well-intentioned and the scientific ambition is genuine, but from my resource-allocation vantage point, it's problematic because:\n1. It diverts scarce research resources toward a compliance requirement that can be met with existing, well-understood engineering solutions.\n2. Testing experimental prototypes in \"most ambi