set for reproducibility and rigorous peer review\" is appealing from my vantage point because it means compliance isn't seen as pure overhead but as something that strengthens the scientific output.\n\nHowever, let me think about whether this truly advances the knowledge frontier or intellectual merit:\n- It's primarily an institutional workflow/process change, not a research breakthrough in itself.\n- But it does have broader scientific impact: if done well, it means every experiment at the lab is better documented, more reproducible, and peer-review-ready by default. This elevates the quality of all downstream research.\n- The \"mapping to specific research milestones\" aspect could create structure that prevents researchers from skipping validation steps because they know those logs feed into both compliance AND their own reproducibility record.\n\nPotential concerns:\n- It's somewhat generic — many labs already use MLOps tools (MLflow, W&B, etc.) that do automatic logging of hyperparameters, dataset versions, and run metadata. What's novel here is specifically tying it to the EU AI Act's Article 12 requirements and structuring it around research milestones.\n- The \"transformation\" language is aspirational — whether this actually works depends on implementation details not specified here.\n- It doesn't directly advance the knowledge frontier in AI itself; it advances the process around AI research.\n\nBut from my vantage point (broader scientific impact, intellectual merit), I think this has genuine value:\n- Reproducibility is a major crisis in ML/AI research right now. Systematic automatic logging of all experimental parameters is directly addressing this.\n- Tying it to regulatory milestones gives institutional teeth — it's not optional, it's built into the workflow.\n- The \"scientific asset for peer review\" framing means this could improve publication quality across the lab.\n\nThis is a solid, practical proposal that has clear benefits from a research pe