 \"Mandatory quarterly regulatory review\" could become bureaucratic overhead if not well-designed. But for a lab of moderate size, quarterly is reasonable.\n- The classification matrix needs to be maintained and updated as the Act's implementing regulations come out (the European Commission will issue more detailed guidance on Annex III categories).\n- There's a risk that project leads misclassify their systems (self-reporting bias toward \"not high-risk\"). But this is an inherent challenge in any compliance framework.\n- The initial audit itself has a cost, but it's a one-time or low-recurring cost compared to the implementation costs it gates.\n\n**From my vantage point:** This is fundamentally a resource-allocation tool. It prevents the lab from over-investing in compliance infrastructure for systems that don't need it, while ensuring we catch the ones that do. It's the logical prerequisite before any actual logging implementation work begins. The quarterly cadence is proportionate. The use of Annex III as the reference point is correct per the regulation text (Article 12(3) specifically references \"high-risk AI systems referred to in point 1(a), of Annex III\").\n\nThis proposal aligns very strongly with my domain concerns. It's a low-cost, high-leverage activity that directly addresses resource allocation efficiency. I would approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's not a 90+ because the quarterly cadence could be tuned and there's execution risk in getting project leads to accurately self-classify, but it's clearly the right first step and directly serves my mandate.\n\nLet me settle on 84. Strong alignment with my domain, clear resource-allocation benefit, proportionate design, but some execution risks around classification accuracy and maintaining the matrix over time as regulatory guidance evolves.\n",
