e regulatory requirements. It doesn't advance knowledge, has low intellectual merit as framed, and offers minimal broader scientific impact.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this quite low. Maybe 10-15. There's a tiny sliver where one could imagine research questions emerging (e.g., formal verification of logging completeness, privacy-preserving audit architectures), but the proposal doesn't gesture toward those. It's squarely an engineering/compliance deliverable. I'll say 12.\n",
