afterthought that doesn't actually prevent the waste because by the time the monthly board meets, the engineering team may have already started building logging infrastructure.\n\nHowever, I need to judge strictly on its own merits. The core value proposition is: prevent wasted engineering resources on unnecessary logging compliance. That IS within my domain (speed/quality of prototyping — not wasting cycles). But the mechanism is weak for an R&D environment where things move fast and classification can be ambiguous or change.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The intent is right but the execution as proposed would likely create friction in a fast-moving R&D lab without providing sufficient technical rigor in the classification decision itself. From my vantage point, I'd say it's mildly useful but not something I'd enthusiastically back as written. It addresses a real concern (resource waste) but the monthly cadence and thin panel make it unlikely to actually prevent the prototyping waste in practice — by month-end, engineering has already committed resources.\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n- Monthly cadence is incompatible with R&D velocity where intended purpose can change within weeks\n- Two-person panel (one researcher + compliance officer) lacks technical depth for proper classification of complex AI systems\n- The proposal doesn't specify how reclassification mid-project would be handled\n- It's a governance/process proposal, not really a technical solution, and from my engineering vantage point it doesn't address the actual technical challenge of implementing logging in a way that supports fast prototyping\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The question is whether this clears MY bar as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality. Does this proposal help or hinder me? \n\nIt could help by preventing me from being asked to build unnecessary logging infrastructure. But the monthly delay means I might already be building it before the board convenes. And if classification is wrong (a false negative), I'd have to retroactively add logging to a running system, which is much harder than designing it in from day one — that would actually HARM prototyping quality and speed.\n\nI'll reject it with a moderate promise score because the underlying concern is valid but the mechanism as proposed would likely be counterproductive in an R&D setting focused on fast iteration.\n",
