ment their code with logging, prototyping speed improves — they just wrap their pipeline in the middleware and compliance events are captured automatically.\n- On the other hand, building this middleware itself requires significant upfront engineering investment before any project benefits. If the lab is resource-constrained (as stated), committing scarce resources to build a shared infrastructure piece might slow down individual prototyping efforts in the short term.\n- The \"without manual researcher intervention\" claim is appealing but potentially misleading — getting the hooks to correctly capture all relevant events (especially for novel research pipelines) will likely require per-project configuration and debugging, which IS a form of manual intervention.\n- For prototyping speed specifically: if the middleware adds latency or complexity to the dev environment, it could slow iteration cycles during active prototyping. A good design would have \"dev mode\" vs \"compliance mode.\"\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a solid, well-scoped engineering proposal. The core idea — centralized logging infrastructure via API hooks/middleware — is technically sound and aligns with how modern MLOps platforms handle observability. It addresses Article 12(1) and (2) directly by providing the \"automatic recording of events\" capability in a reusable form.\n\nFrom my vantage point:\n- Technical viability: High. This is standard middleware/telemetry engineering, not research-grade innovation. The main risk is scope creep trying to handle all model types uniformly.\n- Speed of prototyping: Mixed positive. Reduces per-project burden long-term but requires upfront investment. The \"plug-and-play\" promise may be slightly optimistic for truly heterogeneous AI systems.\n- Quality: A unified approach ensures consistency in what gets logged, which is good for compliance and debugging.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar. It's a technically feasible engineering project with clear value for