        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2067 from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, with focus on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Regulatory Stress-Testing and Audit Protocol. A periodic internal audit process to verify that logs accurately capture high-risk events as required by law. The lab will run simulated failure scenarios to check if the automated logs trigger the specific risk alerts defined in Article 12. It protects our reputation by proving we can actually trace failures before a government regulator discovers them.\"\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance vantage point:\n\n**Relevance to Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires high-risk AI systems to technically allow automatic recording of events (logs) over the system's lifetime.\n- Article 12(2) specifies what logging capabilities must enable: identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operation.\n- Article 12(3) adds specific minimum requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems.\n\nThe proposal proposes periodic internal audits to verify that logs actually capture the required events, plus simulated failure scenarios to test whether automated logs trigger risk alerts.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses Article 12's core requirement that logging must work as specified. If you claim compliance but your logs don't actually capture what they're supposed to, you're non-compliant in substance even if the system \"technically allows\" it.\n- Simulated failure scenarios are a recognized best practice in regulatory compliance (similar to penetration testing for cybersecurity, or disaster recovery drills). This is not just paper compliance — it's operational verification.\n- The proposal explicitly ties to Article 12(2)(a) — identifying situations that may present risk under Article 79(1) or