liance. It directly supports the traceability purpose stated in Recital 71. The simulated failure scenarios are particularly valuable because they test whether the logging actually functions as required under Article 12(2) — not just that the feature exists, but that it captures the right events in real operational conditions.\n\nFrom my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):\n- This directly reduces the risk of a non-compliance finding by regulators.\n- It creates an audit trail demonstrating proactive due diligence.\n- It protects reputation by catching issues internally.\n- The cost is moderate (periodic testing, not building new infrastructure).\n- It's clearly within scope and relevant to Article 12.\n\nI would approve this. It's a practical, well-scoped compliance verification activity that directly supports the regulatory requirements. My promise score should reflect strong confidence but note it could be improved with more specific frequency/scope definitions. I'd put it around 78-82. It's clearly valuable and on-point, but as stated it lacks some specificity (frequency, exact scope of scenarios, documentation of results). From a pure compliance perspective, it's a strong \"yes\" — the idea is sound and directly serves Article 12 compliance verification.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 80. The core concept is exactly what a compliance officer would want to see. The vagueness in frequency and scope are implementation details that can be addressed during execution rather than deal-breakers at the proposal stage.\n",
