        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen proposal P2935 from my vantage point as a Development Engineer at an R&D institution that develops AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal through that lens:\n\n**Proposal P2935:** \"High-Risk System Certification Gate\" - A mandatory internal review process that audits a project's logging and traceability capabilities before any deployment. It involves a checklist-based verification phase with the lead researcher and a compliance officer during the final testing stage. The stated benefit is preventing costly retrospective fixes by identifying gaps in record-keeping before systems enter post-market monitoring phases.\n\n**From my vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n\nArguments for:\n- Catching logging/traceability gaps early does prevent expensive rework. If you're building a high-risk AI system and you forget to implement automatic event recording (Art. 12(1)) or the specific Annex III point 1(a) logging requirements, retrofitting that after deployment is genuinely painful. You'd need to go back through your architecture, add instrumentation, re-test, potentially redeploy.\n- A \"checklist-based verification phase\" during final testing is relatively lightweight in terms of engineering overhead compared to building new systems or redesigning architecture. It's a process gate, not a technical implementation per se.\n- It aligns with the spirit of Art. 12 — ensuring the system technically allows for automatic recording before it goes live.\n\nArguments against / concerns:\n- This is primarily a *process* proposal, not a *technical solution*. It doesn't actually solve anything engineering-wise. It's a governance gate. From my vantage point, I care about whether we have good logging architectures, instrumentation frameworks, observability stacks, etc. A checklist review by a \"compliance officer\" (who may not be technically deep) adds process overhead without necessarily improving the technical quality of the logging implementation.\n- It could slow down prototyping cycles. Adding a mandatory gate with two sign-offs (lead researcher + compliance officer) in the final testing stage adds latency to every high-risk system deployment. For an R&D lab focused on speed of prototyping, this is friction.\n- The proposal is vague: \"checklist-based verification phase\" — what's actually on the checklist? Who designs it? How does it stay current as systems evolve? This feels more like a bureaucratic placeholder than a concrete engineering solution.\n- It doesn't address the actual technical challenge: designing logging infrastructure that captures the right events (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verifier identities) in a way that's performant and doesn't bloat the system. A review gate doesn't help you build that better or faster.\n- \"Prevents costly retrospective fixes\" is true but also somewhat obvious — of course you want to check your logging before deployment. The question is whether this specific mechanism (a two-person checklist at final testing) is the right engineering approach vs., say, building logging as a first-class component in the architecture from day one with automated tests for log completeness.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my vantage point on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping: This proposal is a process/governance mechanism, not a technical solution. It adds a gate that could slow prototyping without necessarily improving the engineering quality of logging implementations. The actual value would come from having good logging architecture patterns, automated instrumentation testing, and perhaps CI/CD checks for log completeness — none of which this proposal addresses. A \"checklist-based verification\" by a compliance officer is likely to be shallow on technical depth. It's not harmful per se (a pre-deployment check is reasonable), but it doesn't advance the engineering capabilities we need. It's more of a compliance theater than an engineering enabler.\n\nI'd lean toward reject. It doesn't meaningfully improve our ability to prototype faster or build technically sound logging solutions. It adds process overhead that could slow cycles without proportional technical benefit. The actual engineering work (designing logging architectures, instrumenting systems, testing log completeness) isn't addressed here.\n\nPromise score: I'd give it maybe 25-30. It's not terrible — catching issues pre-deployment is sensible — but from my specific vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, it doesn't do much. It's a governance checkbox that doesn't advance engineering capability. Maybe 25.\n\nDecision: reject.\n",
