o build. The scope is enormous: causal attribution methods + new metadata schemas + high-dimensional state capture + interpretability layer on top.\n- For prototyping purposes, the existing Article 12 requirements are actually quite tractable: log events with timestamps, reference databases, input data matches, and verification personnel IDs (for Annex III point 1(a) systems). The \"minimum\" logging requirements in paragraph 3 are concrete and implementable today.\n- This proposal doesn't address the minimum compliance bar at all — it jumps to a much more ambitious interpretation. It's solving a problem that may not exist yet (the regulation says \"logs of events,\" not \"causal traces of internal activations\"). The recital mentions \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" for technical documentation, but the logging requirement in Article 12(1) is about \"automatic recording of events.\"\n- From a prototyping standpoint: I can build an event-logging pipeline with structured schemas, retention policies, and query interfaces in weeks. I cannot prototype \"causal tracing of internal activations across arbitrary model architectures\" in any near-term timeframe. The research uncertainty is too high to commit implementation resources to this as the path forward.\n\n**Key concern for my function:**\nThe lab's scarce implementation resources would be better spent on building a robust, production-ready event-logging system that satisfies Article 12(1) through 12(3). This proposal is a research moonshot that:\n1. Has no clear technical path to a working prototype (causal attribution across diverse architectures is unsolved in general)\n2. Has massive performance/storage implications if you're logging high-dimensional internal state at inference time\n3. Over-specifies relative to what the regulation actually requires for \"logs\"\n4. Cannot be prototyped quickly — there's no MVP here, just open research questions\n\nThis is a good scientific contribution (as they note), but it's not technically viable as an implementation path for compliance in any reasonable timeframe, and it can't be prototyped at the speed/quality I need to deliver. It's the wrong tool for the regulatory problem at hand.\n\nDecision: reject. The research is interesting scientifically but from a technical-viability-and-prototyping standpoint, it has no clear path to a shippable artifact, enormous unresolved technical uncertainty, and doesn't address what the regulation actually requires in terms of logging infrastructure.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 15-20. It's not zero because the ideas aren't crazy — interpretability research could eventually inform better audit trails. But from my vantage point of \"can I prototype this, will it work technically, can I deliver it,\" it scores low. The fundamental question is whether we even need causal tracing to comply with Article 12 — and I'd argue strongly no. The regulation asks for event logs, not neural activation traces. Let me settle on 15.\n",
