uddies the intellectual contribution.\n- The scope seems somewhat diffuse: \"cryptographic proofs,\" \"Merkle trees AND distributed ledgers,\" \"new standard for data versioning\" — this is a lot of ground to cover, and it's unclear what the core research question actually is. Is it the cryptographic construction? The standardization effort? The system architecture?\n- \"Tamper-proof records\" is an overstatement. Merkle trees provide tamper-evidence (detectability), not tamper-proofness in absolute terms. This suggests the proposal may not have a precise enough technical framing.\n- From a pure research frontier perspective, cryptographic proofs of data provenance are well-studied (e.g., certificate transparency logs, code signing). The specific application to AI training data is somewhat incremental — it's applying known crypto primitives to a new domain rather than developing new formal methods.\n- However, the \"new standard for data versioning in AI\" aspect could be genuinely novel and impactful if pursued rigorously.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:**\n- If done well, this could contribute to reproducibility standards in ML research broadly.\n- It bridges regulatory science and systems/crypto research — an interesting interdisciplinary angle.\n- The potential to create a reusable framework for any AI system (not just high-risk) increases its impact.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable proposal with genuine intellectual merit, but it has some fuzzy framing issues (conflating Merkle trees with distributed ledgers, overclaiming \"tamper-proof,\" somewhat diffuse scope). The core idea — cryptographic data lineage for AI training — is at the frontier but not radically new; it's an application of established crypto to a new domain. The broader impact potential is real if it produces a standard for AI data versioning.\n\nFrom my vantage point, I'd say this clears the bar. It has genuine intellectual merit (applying formal/crypto methods to AI provenance), touches the knowledge frontier (new standards for AI data versioning are genuinely underdeveloped), and has broader scientific impact beyond compliance. The framing issues don't disqualify it — they're things that would be worked out in execution.\n\nI'd give this a promise score around 62-70. It's solid but not groundbreaking. The core crypto techniques are well-known; the novelty is in the application and standardization. I'll go with 65.\n\nDecision: approve — it meets my bar for intellectual merit and has legitimate frontier potential, even if the framing needs tightening.\n",
