ance by default, reducing the chance of a research team shipping a high-risk system without adequate logging.\n6. Simplifies audit for regulators — this is critical for market reputation and institutional survival. If a regulator comes asking for logs, having them in a unified format dramatically reduces risk of non-compliance findings.\n7. The \"centralized API\" approach ensures consistency in what gets logged, which matters for the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) (period of use, reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons).\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. It says \"all high-risk AI pipelines\" — this is correct scoping. Not all AI systems are high-risk, but the proposal correctly limits to high-risk ones. Good.\n2. The implementation risk: a single centralized API could become a bottleneck or a single point of failure. But from a compliance standpoint, the alternative (fragmented logging) is worse for demonstrating compliance.\n3. \"Every model must call\" — this is strong language but appropriate for compliance. Article 12(1) uses \"shall technically allow,\" meaning it's mandatory.\n4. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or data protection considerations (GDPR interplay), but the core architecture direction is sound.\n5. It mentions \"input matches\" which aligns with Article 12(3)(c) for biometric identification systems specifically, but the framework as described seems broader — it's a general-purpose logging layer that can be configured per system type.\n\n**From institutional survival perspective:**\n- Fragmented logging is a major compliance risk. If any one high-risk system lacks proper logs and gets caught in an audit or post-market monitoring check, the institution faces fines (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations). A universal framework eliminates this category of risk.\n- Market reputation: being known as a lab that has robust, audit-ready logging infrastructure is a competitive advantage and signals regulatory maturity.\n\n**From my bar:** This proposal directly addresses the core obligation in Article 12. It's not just \"nice to have\" — it's implementing a mandatory requirement in a structured way. The fact that it's baked into deployment templates means compliance becomes default rather than aspirational. This clears my bar comfortably. I would approve this.\n\nPromise score: From a compliance standpoint, this is one of the most directly impactful things you can do for Article 12 compliance. It transforms an obligation from \"each team must remember to implement logging\" into \"the infrastructure guarantees logging exists.\" I'd give it a high score — maybe 88-92. It's not perfect (doesn't address retention, data minimization, GDPR interplay explicitly), but the core architecture direction is exactly what's needed and it eliminates a whole class of compliance risk.\n\nLet me settle on 90. It directly implements Article 12(1) as a structural guarantee, supports all sub-requirements in 12(2) and 12(3), reduces audit risk, and makes compliance the default state for new projects. The minor gaps (retention policy, GDPR data minimization in logs, specific handling of Art 12(3)(d) natural person identification) are implementation details that can be addressed within this framework without changing its architecture.\n",
