ns involved in verification (Art 14(5)) - not mentioned at all ✗\n\n**From my compliance perspective:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Addresses the core requirement of automatic logging (Art 12(1))\n- The \"without modifying core model logic\" aspect is good for implementation speed and reduces risk of introducing bugs in safety-critical code\n- Standardizing across \"all high-risk prototypes\" ensures consistency, which regulators will appreciate\n- Removes developer burden, reducing the chance of non-compliant logging being skipped or done incorrectly\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The proposal is somewhat generic - it says \"inputs, outputs, and timestamps\" but Article 12(3) requires very specific data points for certain categories (reference database, match events, verification personnel IDs). The proposal doesn't explicitly address these.\n- \"Sidecar or decorator pattern within internal Python libraries\" - this is an implementation detail that's fine architecturally, but I need to ensure it covers ALL high-risk systems, not just those built in Python. What about systems with components in other languages?\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" (Art 12(1)) - the proposal doesn't explicitly address data retention, storage durability, or what happens when a system is decommissioned. Logging over the full lifetime implies long-term storage which has its own compliance implications.\n- The proposal focuses on prototypes (\"all high-risk prototypes\") but Article 12 applies to deployed high-risk AI systems throughout their operational life. There's a gap between prototype-stage logging and production lifecycle logging.\n- No mention of log integrity (tamper-evidence), access controls, or the technical documentation requirements from Recital 71 that logs must be kept \"up to date\" and in \"clear and comprehensive form.\"\n\nHowever, as an infrastructure investment, this is fundamentally the RIGHT kind of thing to build. Without a unified logging layer, compliance with Article 12 will be ad hoc, inconsistent, and error-prone. This is a foundational capability that every other compliance measure depends on. The fact that it's \"automatic\" directly satisfies the regulatory language.\n\nMy assessment: This clears my bar as a compliance officer. It addresses the core obligation of automatic logging, standardizes the approach (reducing inconsistency risk), and removes developer burden (reducing human error). While the implementation details need to be fleshed out to meet all sub-requirements of Article 12(3) and address data retention/lifetime concerns, the architectural decision itself is sound and necessary. Approving this commits resources to building infrastructure we will absolutely need regardless - the question isn't whether to have automatic logging but how to implement it consistently.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's fundamentally correct and necessary, but the description is somewhat high-level and doesn't fully address all Article 12 sub-requirements (especially 12(3) specifics). It's a strong foundation but not a complete solution by itself.\n",
