        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal is: \"Living Technical Documentation Protocol\" - a dynamic documentation system that auto-updates via Git hooks when hyperparameters or training datasets change, implemented as a version-controlled wiki linked to repositories.\n\nLet me assess this from my engineering/prototyping perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core mechanism (Git hooks triggering documentation updates on code/data changes) is technically straightforward and well-understood. Webhooks + CI/CD pipelines can do this today with existing tools (e.g., GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, or custom scripts).\n- A version-controlled wiki linked to repos is a standard pattern. Tools like Sphinx, MkDocs, Docusaurus, or even simple Markdown in-repo with auto-deploy exist.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect is slightly overblown - it's more accurately \"event-triggered on commit/merge,\" which is fine and actually what the regulation needs (up-to-date, not literally real-time).\n- However, there are practical challenges: hyperparameter changes might be in experiment trackers (MLflow, W&B) rather than Git. Training dataset changes might be in data versioning systems (DVC) or S3 buckets. So \"Git repositories\" alone may not capture all the changes that need to trigger doc updates. This is a gap but not a deal-breaker - it can be extended.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The infrastructure overhead: setting up webhooks, CI pipelines for doc generation, maintaining the wiki structure, ensuring the auto-generated content is actually readable and compliant - this takes engineering time upfront.\n- BUT the proposal explicitly says \"minimizing the administrative burden placed on our primary researchers.\" From my perspective, this is a double-edged sword. The initial setup cost is borne by us (dev engineers), which is fine. But if the auto-generated docs are low quality or incomplete, we end up debugging documentation issues constantly, slowing down prototyping.\n- The risk: over-engineering. Building an elaborate real-time doc system might take 2-4 sprints to get right, during which time actual AI systems aren't being built/tested. A simpler approach (structured README + changelog auto-generated on merge) would achieve 80% of the compliance benefit in a fraction of the effort.\n\n**Relevance to Article 12 specifically:**\n- Recital 71 says documentation should be \"kept up to date\" - this proposal addresses that directly.\n- Article 12(1) is about automatic recording of events (logs), which is different from documentation. This proposal is more about the technical documentation requirement (Article 11/Annex IV) than the logging requirement per se. But it's still relevant to the broader record-keeping obligation.\n- The proposal doesn't address the actual \"automatic recording of events\" (logging) that Article 12(1) mandates - it addresses the documentation update side from Recital 71. This is a partial coverage issue.\n\n**My professional assessment:**\nThe technical approach is sound and buildable. Git-triggered doc updates are a well-trodden path. The main concerns are:\n1. Scope creep risk - \"real-time\" and \"all model characteristics, data sources, and validation results\" is ambitious for an auto-generated system\n2. It addresses documentation currency but not the actual logging/events recording that Art 12(1) requires\n3. The upfront engineering cost is moderate (maybe 2-3 weeks for a solid MVP), which is acceptable\n4. The \"minimizing burden on researchers\" framing means we inherit all the maintenance, but that's our job\n\nFrom my vantage point: it's technically feasible, relatively quick to prototype an initial version, and addresses a real compliance gap (documentation currency). The main risk is over-scoping the \"real-time\" aspect. I'd approve it with the caveat that we start with a simple event-triggered approach rather than a full real-time system.\n\nDecision: Approve - it's technically viable, buildable in a reasonable timeframe, and addresses a genuine documentation gap. But my promise score should reflect that there are scope concerns and it only partially covers Article 12 (more Recital 71 / Annex IV territory).\n\nI'd give it around 62-68. It's solid but not groundbreaking, has some scoping issues, and the \"real-time\" framing might lead to over-engineering that slows prototyping.\n",
