onal labor cost.\n\n**Arguments for caution/rejection:**\n1. Development cost: Building test scripts that \"trigger simulated high-risk events\" and validate \"all required regulatory fields\" is non-trivial engineering work. It requires someone to deeply understand Article 12's requirements (including the Annex III specific provisions in paragraph 3) and encode them into test logic. This has upfront labor cost.\n2. Maintenance burden: As regulations are interpreted further by the Commission through implementing acts, guidelines, or standardization, the test suite needs updates. Ongoing maintenance is a resource drain.\n3. Scope risk: \"All required regulatory fields\" could expand scope significantly if interpreted broadly. The Annex III point 1(a) specific requirements (paragraph 3) add complexity beyond generic logging.\n4. Does this duplicate what already exists? If the lab already has some CI infrastructure, the marginal cost might be lower. But if it doesn't, building from scratch is more expensive.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a strong proposal. The key insight is that it converts a recurring, labor-intensive administrative task into an automated one-time development investment with ongoing low-cost operation. For a lab developing high-risk AI systems subject to the EU AI Act, this will be needed repeatedly for every prototype and every iteration. The compounding savings are significant.\n\nThe scope is reasonably bounded — it's specifically about Article 12 logging validation, not a general \"AI compliance platform.\" It fits naturally into existing engineering workflows (CI/CD). The upfront cost is moderate (developing test scripts) but the payback period for any lab shipping multiple high-risk AI systems would be short.\n\nThe main risk is maintenance and scope creep, but those are manageable with proper scoping discipline.\n\nI'd approve this. It's a clear efficiency play that aligns perfectly with my ma