mal direct impact on prototyping speed since it's a periodic exercise, not a continuous development blocker. But by catching gaps early, it prevents rework later which would slow things down more significantly.\n- The proposal is well-scoped and technically straightforward to implement. It's essentially a test harness that queries the logging system with randomized audit-style requests.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This is a low-cost, high-value verification exercise that doesn't compete with prototyping resources (it's periodic, bounded). It directly validates that our technical implementation of Article 12(1)-(3) works as required. The engineering investment to set this up is minimal - it's basically a test script + scheduling. I see no reason not to approve this.\n\nHowever, I should consider: does this fall within \"my\" domain? My concern is technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. This proposal doesn't advance prototyping per se, but it does validate the quality of our implementation. It's a quality gate that ensures we don't ship broken logging. From a development engineering perspective, catching a logging gap in an internal stress test vs. discovering it during a regulatory audit (which would require emergency patches under time pressure) is clearly better for both speed and quality.\n\nI'll approve this with a solid promise score - maybe 72-78. It's clearly useful and technically sound, but from my specific vantage point of prototyping velocity, it's not the most exciting thing I could be working on. It's more of a \"yes, absolutely do this because it protects us\" rather than \"this makes us build faster.\" But it does protect quality which is part of my mandate.\n\nLet me settle on 74 - clearly valuable for quality assurance of our logging implementation, low resource cost, technically straightforward to implement, but not directly advancing prototyping capabilities.\n",
