nd minimum compliance requirements and invests in proactive safety analysis from its own telemetry data signals to partners, clients, and regulators that it takes AI governance seriously. This is a positive signal for market positioning.\n\n5. **Concerns**: \n   - The proposal says \"during testing\" — but Article 12(1) requires logging \"over the lifetime of the system,\" which includes deployment/production use. If this research initiative only focuses on test-phase logs, it doesn't fully address the ongoing monitoring obligations in production. However, the text says \"generated by our high-risk AI systems during testing\" which could be a limitation.\n   - The proposal is framed as advancing \"intellectual merit of our work in AI safety while fulfilling monitoring mandates.\" This dual framing is good — it justifies resource allocation both on scientific and compliance grounds.\n   - It's a \"dedicated research initiative\" — this implies committing resources to something that may take time before producing deployable tools. But the output (anomaly detection methods, causal inference algorithms applied to telemetry) would directly feed into our post-market monitoring capabilities under Article 72.\n\n6. **Risk of rejection**: The main risk is that it's a research initiative rather than an implementation project. However, the methods developed would be directly applicable to compliance with Articles 12, 72, and related provisions. It's not a speculative moonshot — anomaly detection on system logs is well-understood territory being applied to a specific regulatory context.\n\n**Decision**: This proposal aligns strongly with my domain of concern. It directly supports compliance with Article 12 (by making the mandatory logs actually useful for risk identification), Article 72 (post-market monitoring), and Article 26(5) (monitoring operations). It strengthens our institutional position by showing proactive engagement with safety obligations beyond passive compliance.