rticle 12(3)(b) requires recording \"the reference database against which input data has been checked by the system.\" The proposal mentions \"input data, output matches\" but doesn't explicitly mention capturing the reference database identifier. This is a gap for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification).\n- Article 12(2)(a) requires logging to enable identifying situations that may result in risk or substantial modification — this is broader than just input/output matching. The proposal seems focused on the more mechanical aspects.\n- Article 12(2)(b) mentions facilitating post-market monitoring (Art. 72) and 12(2)(c) monitoring operation (Art. 26(5)) — these require logging capabilities beyond basic input/output capture.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — need to ensure the architecture accounts for retention periods, archival, and accessibility over potentially long lifetimes. The proposal doesn't address data retention or archival strategy explicitly.\n- The scope says \"high-risk projects\" — good that it's scoped appropriately. But does it cover all categories in Annex I and III?\n- The proposal is primarily an engineering implementation detail. From a compliance standpoint, the architecture itself is sound but the execution details matter (e.g., immutability of logs, access controls, audit trails of who can modify logging configuration).\n\n**Compliance Officer Assessment:**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic recording of events. Making it a mandatory wrapper integrated into CI/CD is exactly the kind of structural compliance mechanism I'd want. It reduces reliance on individual research teams to remember or correctly implement logging, which is where compliance failures typically occur.\n\nHowever, there are gaps:\n- No mention of reference database identification (Art. 12(3)(b))\n- No explicit mention of log immutability, retention periods, or archival\n- The \"