 path is: use existing compression (zstd for structured data), columnar formats, tiered storage (hot/warm/cold). We could prototype this in weeks.\n- A \"scientific project\" exploring \"lossy and lossless compression algorithms specifically tuned for AI event telemetry\" sounds like it would take months or years to produce results that might be marginal improvements over off-the-shelf solutions.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what the actual engineering deliverable would be. It's research, not a solution I can build on quickly.\n\n**Does it solve a real problem for us?**\n- Storage costs for logs are real but manageable with existing infrastructure practices. Unless we're at massive scale (billions of events per day), this is an optimization, not a viability blocker.\n- The \"lifetime logging\" requirement does mean long-term storage, and cold storage costs add up. But again, S3 Glacier-class solutions handle this well enough for now.\n\n**Regulatory fit:**\n- Article 12 requires logs to be kept \"over the lifetime of the system.\" It doesn't mandate any particular format or compression. Using standard compression is fine — it's reversible (lossless) and preserves all information.\n- Lossy compression is a red flag from a compliance standpoint. If you can't reconstruct the original event, have you truly \"recorded\" it?\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom my development engineering vantage point:\n1. The core problem (log storage costs) is real but solvable with existing technology in a matter of weeks, not a research project's worth of months/years.\n2. The \"research\" framing means no concrete prototype or deliverable I can integrate into our systems quickly.\n3. The lossy compression angle introduces regulatory risk that could actually create MORE compliance burden, not less.\n4. There's no clear engineering specification — what would the output be? A library? A format spec? A paper?\n5. The \"publishable scientific contribution\" framing suggests the primary motivati