se logging paths actually work when they matter most.\n- The proposal is focused, targeted specifically at Article 12 compliance, and addresses a clear gap: you can write code that's supposed to log, but without stress-testing in a controlled environment, you may discover gaps only during production incidents or regulatory inspections.\n\nThis is clearly aligned with my professional mandate. It's not speculative R&D - it's a compliance validation activity. The resource commitment (compute clusters for staging) is proportionate to the regulatory risk of non-compliance. Early identification of logging gaps directly protects against:\n- Regulatory enforcement actions\n- Market reputation damage from system failures where logs don't capture required events\n- Costly late-stage redesigns that would delay product timelines\n\nI should approve this. The promise score should be high - it's directly on-mission for compliance, proportionate in scope, and addresses a concrete regulatory requirement with clear ROI in risk reduction. I'd give it around 85-90. It's not a 100 because a testbed alone doesn't guarantee compliance (you still need the logging implementation to be correct), and \"simulated failures\" may not capture all edge cases of real-world operation. But from my vantage point, this is strongly aligned.\n\nLet me settle on 87 - very strong alignment with Article 12 compliance validation needs, but I note it's one component in a broader compliance program and the value depends on the quality of failure scenarios designed.\n",
