 proposal's description is somewhat generic (\"timestamps, state changes, error events\"). It doesn't explicitly mention the specific data points required by Article 12(3) for biometric systems. However, as a framework/library, it could be designed to support those specific fields. The proposal says \"standardized API\" which suggests extensibility.\n\n**From my compliance vantage point:**\n\nStrengths:\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) — automatic recording\n- Standardization prevents gaps where one team's bespoke solution might miss a required data point\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" is addressed by central infrastructure (persistent storage)\n- Reduces risk of non-compliance through inconsistency across teams\n- A shared framework means one audit target rather than N disparate systems — better for regulators and market reputation\n- Prevents the common failure mode where logging is bolted on after the fact\n\nWeaknesses/Concerns:\n- The description is somewhat high-level; \"timestamps, state changes, error events\" may not be sufficient for all Article 12(3) specific requirements (reference databases, input data matches, natural person identification)\n- However, as a framework with a standardized API, it could accommodate these specific fields\n- Central infrastructure dependency: single point of failure? But from compliance perspective, centralization is actually better for auditability\n- \"Over the lifetime\" — need to ensure retention policies are built in, but that's an implementation detail\n\n**Institutional survival:** A unified framework means if a regulator comes knocking, we have ONE consistent logging system to demonstrate. If each team built their own, there would be gaps and inconsistencies that could lead to enforcement action. This is critical for institutional survival.\n\n**Regulatory compliance:** Directly implements the mandatory requirement of Article 12(1). Standardization ensures no system falls through t