ging (Article 12).\n\n3. **Modularity**: A modular library with standard API calls is a sound engineering pattern. Wrappers for primary deep learning frameworks (PyTorch, TensorFlow, JAX) are feasible and have precedents in the industry (e.g., MLflow, Weights & Biases do some of this, though not specifically for EU AI Act compliance). The concept of automating timestamps, database references, and user identification is straightforward engineering.\n\n4. **Speed/quality of prototyping**: From my perspective as a development engineer focused on prototyping speed:\n   - A centralized middleware adds an abstraction layer. For quick prototypes, this could be overhead — researchers may want to spin up a proof-of-concept without integrating into the compliance framework first. This is a classic tension between \"shift-left compliance\" and \"fast iteration.\"\n   - However, if well-designed with opt-in hooks (decorators, context managers), the per-project burden can be minimal. The claim that it \"minimizes per-project engineering burden on researchers\" is plausible IF the middleware is mature and well-documented at the point of use.\n   - Building this middleware itself requires significant upfront engineering effort from the core team. During the development phase, no projects benefit. This is a lead-time concern for prototyping speed.\n\n5. **Technical feasibility**: The specific requirements in Article 12(3) are actually quite straightforward to implement:\n   - Start/end timestamps: trivial with logging middleware\n   - Reference database identification: requires knowing which DB the system queries — feasible but architecture-dependent\n   - Input data that led to a match: requires intercepting query results — feasible but needs careful design for different architectures\n   - User identification: ties into authentication/authorization systems — feasible but depends on existing auth infrastructure\n\n6. **Risk**: The main technical risk is over-engineering. If the mi