nt gap for compliance — Recital 71 and Article 12 are related but distinct obligations.\n- However, the proposal positions itself as serving \"Recital 71's demand\" specifically, which is fair. It doesn't claim to cover all of Article 12.\n- The Git workflow integration is practical and leverages existing developer habits — good for adoption and thus actual compliance in practice (not just on paper).\n- It doesn't explicitly mention retention periods or access by authorities, but that's arguably out of scope for this specific proposal.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\nThis proposal directly supports one of the core record-keeping obligations under Article 12 / Recital 71. The technical documentation requirement is mandatory for high-risk AI systems. Without it, we cannot demonstrate compliance to notified bodies or market surveillance authorities. The Git-integrated approach is practical and creates a verifiable audit trail.\n\nThe fact that it doesn't cover the automatic event logging (Article 12(1)-(3)) is a limitation but not a disqualifier — the proposal is scoped to what it addresses, and it addresses it well. As a compliance officer, I need both pieces, but this one is clearly necessary and correctly scoped.\n\nFrom an institutional survival standpoint: if we get audited and cannot produce current technical documentation showing how our systems were developed, trained, validated, and modified — that's a compliance failure with serious consequences. This proposal builds the infrastructure to prevent that.\n\nFrom market reputation: demonstrating robust documentation practices is a competitive advantage in procurement (public sector buyers increasingly require AI Act compliance evidence).\n\nMy decision: This clearly meets my bar. It addresses a mandatory regulatory requirement with a practical implementation approach. I approve it.\n\nPromise score: This is highly relevant to my core mandate. It's not a 100 because it only covers the documentation side (Recital 7