ce, scaling as the number of high-risk model calls grows.\n- \"Total compliance\" is a strong claim. Article 12(2) requires logging capabilities appropriate to intended purpose, and Article 12(3) has specific minimums for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification). A generic middleware capturing inputs/outputs/timestamps may not capture all required fields — e.g., \"the reference database against which input data has been checked,\" or \"identification of natural persons involved in verification.\" So the claim of \"total compliance\" might be overpromising.\n- However, as a foundation, it's the right architectural choice. It can be extended to capture additional fields specific to Annex III 1(a) systems.\n- The proposal says it intercepts and logs BEFORE the model processes — this means you get inputs but potentially not outputs (since it's before processing). Actually re-reading: \"intercepts requests and logs them to a secure database before the model processes them\" — this only captures pre-processing data, not outputs. That's a gap for compliance. But the text also says \"automatically captures inputs, outputs, and timestamps\" so maybe the architecture includes both request and response logging despite the \"before\" phrasing being slightly imprecise.\n- Resource efficiency: One centralized solution vs. N distributed solutions is clearly more efficient in terms of total engineering hours, maintenance burden, and consistency.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding:**\n- The upfront investment is moderate (one middleware layer on existing infrastructure) but the ongoing benefit is high (every project gets compliance logging for free)\n- It protects researcher time — researchers don't need to spend cycles writing custom logging code\n- This aligns with preserving R&D capacity while meeting regulatory obligations\n\n**Effective use of existing infrastructure:**\n- Building on the API gateway is smart — it's where traffic already flows\n