ompliance documentation burden.\n\nConcerns/Limitations:\n- Training alone doesn't satisfy Article 12. The law requires the SYSTEM to technically allow automatic logging (Article 12(1)). A training program is a supporting measure, not a substitute for engineering implementation. It's necessary but not sufficient.\n- Monthly workshops may be resource-intensive and could be seen as overhead by engineering teams, creating cultural friction.\n- \"Mandatory\" + tied to milestone approvals is good for enforcement but could create resentment if not well-designed.\n- The proposal doesn't specify who delivers the training (internal legal? external counsel?), what the actual curriculum covers in detail, or how competency is assessed.\n- It's a process/culture initiative rather than a technical implementation. From a compliance standpoint, it supports but doesn't deliver the actual logging capability required by Article 12(1).\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom my compliance vantage point, this is a strong supporting measure. The biggest risk to institutional survival under Article 12 isn't just building the logging infrastructure — it's ensuring that every team producing high-risk AI systems actually maintains proper technical documentation, understands what events must be logged (Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c)), and treats versioning of training data as non-negotiable. A culture where compliance is an afterthought will produce gaps even with good tooling.\n\nThe proposal addresses a real gap: the human/cultural layer. It's not a substitute for engineering work, but it multiplies the effectiveness of any technical implementation by ensuring people know what to do and why.\n\nI'd approve this. It's not a silver bullet — it won't make the system compliant on its own — but it's a necessary enabling measure that directly supports institutional survival by reducing the risk of non-compliant documentation practices across teams. The milestone-approval gating is smart enforcement.\n\nPromise scor