ot the training pipeline. However, Recital 71 does mention that technical documentation should include \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes used.\"\n- So there IS a regulatory hook here, but it's more about the technical documentation (Article 11) than Article 12's logging requirement per se. The proposal frames this as an Article 12 response but it's really more of an Article 11 / Recital 71 response with some overlap into Article 12 for training-phase events.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Building a shared API integrated into the compute cluster is a significant infrastructure project. It requires:\n  - Designing and implementing the API\n  - Modifying or wrapping all existing training scripts (or providing SDKs)\n  - Setting up the database schema for \"compliant\" storage\n  - Ongoing maintenance as new frameworks/models are added\n- This is a multi-quarter infrastructure project, not a quick prototype. It's the kind of thing that could take 6-12 months to get right across heterogeneous research codebases.\n- However, it does address a real pain point: researchers currently log experiments inconsistently or not at all. Automating this removes friction and improves quality of outputs.\n- The \"removes manual burden from individual researchers\" claim is true but also means you need researcher buy-in for the instrumentation layer, which can slow adoption.\n\n**Does it clear my bar?**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint: The architecture is sound and proven (it's essentially MLflow/W&B in-house). It's technically feasible. However:\n- It's a large infrastructure investment with uncertain scope (\"every R&D experiment\" across the lab is a moving target)\n- The regulatory mapping is somewhat loose — it addresses training-phase documentation more than Article 12's runtime logging requirement\n- But it DOES create the foundation that Article 12 compliance will need (you can't log \"the data used in training\" at deployment if you