        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2369\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12(1) is an unqualified 'shall' obligation—high-risk AI systems must technically allow automatic logging over their full lifetime—and this proposal correctly treats that as a mandatory infrastructure concern rather than a per-team research task, which is the only architecture that can guarantee institution-wide compliance and survive a regulator's audit. However, from my vantage point I flag two material gaps: (i) an 'immutable ledger' that captures input data and natural-person identifiers under Art 12(3)(c)-(d) collides with GDPR Articles 16/17 unless retention limits and pseudonymisation are engineered in before build, creating a cross-regulation compliance exposure; and (ii) the proposal's specific log fields (start/end times, reference-database queries) map tightly to Annex III point 1(a) biometric systems but do not yet articulate how the framework will satisfy the broader Art 12(2)(a)-(c) traceability duties for other high-risk categories (Annex I products, medical devices under 1(b), critical infrastructure, etc.), nor does it address the obligation to make logs available to market-surveillance authorities and deployers. These are resolvable in implementation, but they mean the framework as scoped cannot yet be signed off as full Article 12 coverage.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 83\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze proposal P2369 from my vantage point as Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal: \"Unified Compliance Logging Framework\" - a standardized software library integrated into all high-risk AI development pipelines to automate event recording, with consistent API for timestamps and input data, middleware layer intercepting system calls, immutable time-stamped ledger storage, eliminating risk of individual teams forgetting logging requirements.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Directly addresses Article 12(1) requirement for automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system - this is a \"shall\" obligation, not optional.\n- Addresses Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) by providing traceability appropriate to intended purpose.\n- The middleware approach to intercepting system calls and auto-recording start/end times, reference database queries aligns well with Article 12(3)(a) and (b) for biometric identification systems under Annex III point 1(a).\n- Immutable time-stamped ledger is a strong compliance control - prevents tampering, supports audit trails, demonstrates good faith to regulators.\n- Standardization across all high-risk AI pipelines addresses the \"lifetime\" requirement consistently.\n- Eliminates reliance on individual research teams' diligence - this is critical for compliance because if one team forgets, we're non-compliant as an institution.\n- The \"consistent API\" ensures uniformity which helps with regulatory audits and demonstrates systemic compliance rather than ad-hoc patchwork.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Integrated into all high-risk AI development pipelines\" - this is ambitious but the scope might be unclear. What counts as a \"pipeline\"? Some research work may not have clear pipeline boundaries.\n- The proposal mentions start/end times and reference database queries specifically, which maps to Article 12(3) (Annex III point 1(a) systems). For other high-risk AI systems under Annex I or other Annex III categories, the logging requirements are different (per 12(2)). The proposal seems somewhat narrowly scoped to biometric-type systems but presents itself as \"all high-risk AI development pipelines.\"\n- \"Immutable ledger\" - need to consider data protection implications (GDPR). If logs contain personal data (especially for biometric identification under 12(3)(d)), immutability may conflict with GDPR's right to erasure. This is a compliance tension I'd want addressed.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention logging \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" (12(3)(c)) or \"identification of natural persons involved in verification\" (12(3)(d)). Though it says \"input data\" generally.\n- No mention of how logs will be made available to notified bodies, market surveillance authorities, or deployers as required by other articles.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses the core obligation of Article 12(1). The \"shall technically allow\" language means this is non-negotiable - we MUST have automatic logging. Having it built into the pipeline as middleware rather than relying on individual teams is the right architectural approach for compliance. The immutability feature supports audit readiness and demonstrates institutional commitment to transparency, which protects market reputation.\n\nHowever, I have concerns about:\n1. GDPR tension with immutable logs containing personal data\n2. Scope clarity (does it cover all high-risk systems or just Annex III 1(a)?)\n3. Whether the \"lifetime\" requirement is adequately addressed (what happens when a system is decommissioned? Where do logs go?)\n4. No mention of making logs available to authorities per other articles\n\nDespite these concerns, this is fundamentally the RIGHT thing to build from a compliance perspective. Article 12(1) is a hard \"shall\" - we must comply. Building it as infrastructure rather than leaving it to individual teams is the only way to ensure institutional-wide compliance. The risk of non-compliance (fines up to €35M or 7% global turnover for prohibited practices, though high-risk violations carry €15M/3%) makes this existential.\n\nMy decision: I would approve this because it addresses a mandatory obligation with the right architectural approach (systematic, automated, institution-wide). The concerns about GDPR and scope can be addressed in implementation. Without something like this, we're relying on individual researchers to remember compliance requirements - that's an unacceptable institutional risk.\n\nPromise score: This is very strong from my perspective. It directly addresses a \"shall\" requirement with the right level of automation and standardization. I'd give it around 82-85. Not higher because of the GDPR tension (immutable logs vs. data subject rights), the scope ambiguity, and the fact that it doesn't address log availability to authorities. But these are fixable implementation details rather than fundamental flaws.\n\nI'll go with 83.\n",
