e (commit hashes, model versions, etc.).\n- The proposal addresses Article 12(1) well: automatic recording of events over the lifetime of the system.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), it would need to capture specific data points (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, verification personnel). A generic framework would need configurable schemas per system type — this adds complexity but is manageable.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Pro: Reduces per-project compliance burden. Researchers don't each have to figure out logging from scratch. This accelerates prototyping because the compliance scaffolding is pre-built.\n- Pro: \"Without altering core algorithmic logic\" — this means researchers can iterate on models without worrying about breaking compliance infrastructure. Good for prototyping speed.\n- Con: Building a unified library across ALL projects is itself a significant engineering investment upfront. There's a risk of over-engineering or the framework becoming a bottleneck if it doesn't fit well with new architectures.\n- Con: \"Unified\" might become constraining. If a researcher needs to prototype something novel (new inference pattern, edge deployment, streaming), the middleware might not support it yet, creating friction.\n- Con: The proposal is somewhat high-level. \"A unified software library\" and \"middleware layer\" are broad strokes. The actual implementation complexity for truly covering diverse AI systems is non-trivial.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable approach. The middleware/instrumentation pattern is well-understood and proven. It directly addresses Article 12(1)'s requirement for automatic logging. From my perspective as a dev engineer:\n- The core technical concept (middleware hooks into inference pipeline) is sound and implementable.\n- The \"unified across all projects\" scope is ambitious but the direction is right — building it once and reusing beats 