        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1324\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core pattern—streaming anomaly detection over live inference logs against a validated baseline—is technically well-trodden (Prometheus/Grafana, streaming Flink jobs, drift-detection libraries like Evidently or Alibi-Detect), so the basic version is prototypable in days. However, the proposal's stated scope ('behavior deviates from the initial technical documentation specifications') conflates a generic threshold monitor with a system-specific conformance checker that would require translating free-text capability/limitation descriptions into machine-evaluable predicates; that translation work is highly bespoke per deployment and will dominate timeline, pushing realistic prototyping of a *meaningful* (not just latency/error-rate) version into multi-week territory. As written it reads more like an architecture direction than a shippable module.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 64\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to screen a single proposal (P1324) from the vantage point of a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act.\n\nLet me analyze the proposal:\n\n**P1324: Real-time Risk Detection and Alerting Module**\n\nThe proposal describes:\n- A monitoring system for identifying substantial modifications or risk triggers per Article 12(2)\n- An anomaly detection layer that monitors live logs for performance drops against a validated model baseline\n- Triggers alerts when behavior deviates from initial technical documentation specifications\n- Goal: facilitate post-market monitoring without manually scrubbing through massive log files\n\nNow let me assess this from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on **technical viability** and **speed/quality of prototyping**:\n\n**Technical Viability Assessment:**\n1. The concept is sound — anomaly detection on live logs against a baseline is a well-understood pattern in MLOps/DevOps monitoring. Tools like Prometheus, Grafana, or custom streaming analytics can do this.\n2. However, there are significant technical challenges:\n   - \"Performance drops against a baseline of the validated model\" — defining what constitutes a \"performance drop\" for an AI system is non-trivial. For many AI systems (especially LLMs or complex multi-modal models), there isn't a single scalar \"performance\" metric that cleanly maps to \"the model is doing something wrong.\" You might track latency, token usage, output distribution shifts, etc., but mapping these to \"risk triggers as defined in Article 12(2)\" requires domain-specific engineering.\n   - \"Behavior deviates from the initial technical documentation specifications\" — this is essentially a conformance checker. The technical documentation will describe capabilities and limitations, but translating those into machine-checkable thresholds is a significant engineering task that's highly system-specific. It's not a generic module you can build once.\n   - The proposal says \"substantial modifications or risk triggers as defined in Article 12(2)\" — but Article 12(2) defines what the *logging capabilities* should enable (identifying situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation). It doesn't itself define specific risk triggers. The actual risk definitions come from Article 79(1) and the risk management system under Articles 9-10. So there's a slight misalignment in scope.\n   - \"Without manually scrubbing through massive log files\" — this is a reasonable goal, but real-time anomaly detection on high-throughput AI system logs has non-trivial infrastructure requirements (streaming pipelines, stateful computation, alert fatigue management).\n\n3. From a prototyping speed perspective:\n   - A basic version (track latency, throughput, error rates against thresholds) can be prototyped quickly — days to a couple of weeks.\n   - But the \"anomaly detection layer\" that actually catches meaningful deviations relevant to AI Act compliance is much harder. You need to define what \"deviation from technical documentation specifications\" means in code, which is highly system-specific.\n   - The proposal reads somewhat generically — it's more of a monitoring/observability framework than a specific implementable module. It would require significant scoping work before prototyping could begin.\n\n4. Quality concerns:\n   - Alert fatigue: if you set thresholds too loosely, you get noise; too tight, you miss real issues. Calibrating this for AI systems specifically (where \"normal\" behavior can be stochastic) is hard.\n   - The proposal conflates several things: anomaly detection, conformance checking against documentation, and risk trigger identification. These are related but distinct engineering problems with different difficulty levels.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\n- The core idea (automated monitoring/alerting on logs to support post-market monitoring) is technically viable and valuable — it's standard MLOps practice extended into a compliance context.\n- However, as stated, the proposal is too vague to be directly implementable. \"Anomaly detection layer\" and \"behavior deviates from initial technical documentation specifications\" are hand-wavy descriptions that would require significant design work before prototyping.\n- The speed of prototyping is moderate: you can build a basic version quickly (threshold-based alerts on standard metrics), but the full scope described would take considerably longer and is highly system-dependent.\n- Technical viability is reasonable for the basic case, uncertain for the ambitious case.\n\nI think this clears my bar as a viable technical direction — it's not a fantasy, the core pattern exists in production systems today. But it needs more specificity to be truly implementable, and the \"speed of prototyping\" concern means I'd want to see a narrower first prototype (e.g., just threshold alerts on 3-5 key metrics for one specific system) rather than the full \"anomaly detection layer.\"\n\nDecision: I'll lean toward approve with moderate promise. The technical direction is sound and feasible, it addresses a real gap in the logging/monitoring pipeline, and the basic version can be prototyped relatively quickly. But the ambition of the description (\"anomaly detection layer\" that detects deviation from \"technical documentation specifications\") adds complexity that slows down initial prototyping and raises questions about what's actually implementable vs. aspirational.\n\nI'll give it around 62-68. The core is viable, but the scope as described is a bit broad and would need narrowing for fast prototyping. Let me settle on 65.\n",
