le, complete logging.\n\nConcerns:\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention the specific minimum data points for Annex III 1(a) systems in Art 12(3). However, a good implementation would include these as part of the \"specific event data required by Article 12.\"\n- It says \"modular wrapper around existing telemetry tools\" — this could be a risk if existing telemetry doesn't capture all required events. But this is an implementation detail, not a conceptual flaw in the proposal.\n- The scope is limited to high-risk AI projects, which is appropriate (Art 12 applies specifically to high-risk systems).\n- There's no mention of log retention periods or access controls for logs, but that might be out of scope for this particular proposal or handled elsewhere.\n\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective:\n- This directly supports the \"shall\" obligation in Art 12(1) by building automatic logging into the development framework.\n- It institutionalizes compliance rather than relying on individual researcher awareness.\n- It creates an audit trail at the institutional level, which is critical for demonstrating compliance to regulators (market reputation and survival).\n- The centralized nature means that if regulations update or interpretations evolve, there's one place to update logging logic.\n- This reduces the risk of a single project being non-compliant due to oversight, which could trigger regulatory action threatening institutional survival.\n\nThis is a strong proposal from a compliance standpoint. It addresses the core \"shall\" requirement in a systematic, enforceable way. The main risk is implementation quality (does it actually capture all required events?), but as a proposal for committing resources, the direction is clearly right and high-value for compliance.\n\nI'd rate this quite highly. It's not 100 because:\n- It doesn't explicitly address log retention, immutability, or access controls (which are implied by traceability requirements)\n- It doesn't explicitly map to all Art 12(3)(a)-(d) minimum data points for biometric systems\n- The success depends heavily on the infrastructure team's execution quality\n\nBut it's a fundamentally sound and necessary investment from a compliance perspective. I'd put this around 82-85. It's clearly something I would back, but with notes about ensuring complete coverage of all Art 12 sub-requirements in implementation.\n\nDecision: Approve.\n",
