        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1987 is about \"Automated Root-Cause Analysis via Regulatory Log Mining\" - developing AI agents to scan high-risk system logs (those required by Article 12) to identify triggers of biased or unsafe behavior, using causal inference and temporal pattern recognition.\n\nLet me assess this from my professional vantage point:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core idea is taking structured logs (timestamped events per Art 12(1), specific fields per Art 12(3)) and running causal inference/temporal pattern analysis on them. This is technically feasible as a research problem. Causal inference on event streams is an active area of research.\n- However, there's a significant gap: the proposal treats \"compliance logs\" as if they already exist in a rich enough form to do meaningful root-cause analysis. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events, but the granularity and structure of those logs depends heavily on how the system is designed. The logging capability requirements in Art 12(2) are purposeful (identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring), not specifically designed for causal inference research.\n- For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), the minimum log fields are: start/end timestamps, reference database, input data that matched, and identifiers of verifying persons. This is relatively sparse - it's enough for traceability but may not contain sufficient signal for \"precise triggers of biased or unsafe behavior.\" You'd need to know what model decisions were made, what features drove them, confidence scores, etc., which aren't explicitly in the Art 12(3) minimums.\n- The proposal is framed as a \"research project\" - developing AI agents capable of doing this. That's a long-horizon R&D effort with uncertain technical payoff.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a research project, not an implementation task. From my vantage point, the question is: can we prototype something meaningful quickly? The answer is somewhat mixed. We could build a prototype log-mining pipeline relatively quickly IF we have well-structured logs with sufficient signal. But the \"AI agents\" and \"causal inference techniques\" parts are research-heavy and won't yield quick, high-quality prototypes.\n- The dependency on existing Art 12 logging infrastructure means this is downstream of other engineering work. If our logging systems aren't built yet or aren't rich enough, there's nothing to mine.\n- The scope is quite broad: \"develop AI agents capable of scanning... identify precise triggers... apply causal inference techniques and temporal pattern recognition.\" That's multiple research problems bundled together.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint, the core idea has merit - structured event logs are indeed valuable data for understanding system behavior. However, several concerns:\n\n1. The proposal conflates \"compliance logging\" (which is designed for traceability and basic monitoring) with \"debugging/telemetry logging\" (which would be needed for root-cause analysis). Art 12's minimum requirements may not provide enough signal for the ambitious claims made here.\n2. It's framed as a research project to \"develop AI agents\" - this is high-risk, long-duration work from my prototyping perspective.\n3. The value proposition (\"turns mandatory compliance data into a tool\") assumes the logs will be rich enough, which depends on implementation choices we control but aren't specified here.\n4. From speed/quality of prototyping: I could prototype a basic log correlation engine in weeks, but \"AI agents with causal inference\" is months-to-years of research work. The gap between prototype and production insight is large.\n\nHowever, there IS a viable core: if we design our Art 12 logging to be sufficiently rich (which we control as implementers), then building tooling to mine those logs for patterns is technically straightforward in principle. The \"AI agents\" and \"causal inference\" framing might be over-engineering what could start as simpler pattern-matching and correlation tools.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The underlying idea has technical merit, but the specific framing (research project developing AI agents with causal inference) is too ambitious and uncertain for my bar of \"technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping.\" I can see a viable prototype path if scoped down, but as written, it's a research bet on multiple unsolved problems.\n\nI'll lean toward reject with a moderate promise score - the idea isn't bad, but the technical risk is high, the scope is too broad for quick prototyping wins, and there's a fundamental question about whether Art 12 logs (as minimally specified) contain enough signal for the claims being made.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My role is to judge \"technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\" \n\n- Technical viability: The concept is viable in principle (log mining, pattern detection). But the specific technical path (AI agents + causal inference on compliance-grade logs) has significant uncertainty. Causal inference requires strong assumptions about confounders; applying it to sparse event logs is non-trivial.\n- Speed/quality of prototyping: A prototype could be built relatively quickly for basic log correlation and anomaly detection. But the \"precise triggers\" claim via causal inference on AI agents is not something you can prototype credibly in a short timeframe.\n\nI'll reject it - not because the idea is bad, but because as scoped (a research project to develop AI agents with causal inference), the technical risk is too high and the path to a demonstrable, high-quality prototype is too long and uncertain. The promise score should reflect that there's a ker