et-reputation play.\n- The \"regulatory triggers\" language suggests it's designed with compliance requirements in mind, not just engineering safety.\n\nConcerns/weaknesses:\n- A dashboard alone doesn't satisfy Article 12(1) — it needs the underlying automatic logging capability to exist first. But this proposal assumes logs are being generated; it's a consumption/analytics layer on top. That's fine — it's complementary, not substitutive.\n- \"Predefined safety thresholds\" — who defines these? If they're engineering-defined without compliance input, they might miss regulatory triggers. However, the text does say \"regulatory triggers,\" which suggests some compliance alignment is intended.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly mention retention periods for logs, which is critical for compliance (you need to keep records over the lifetime of the system). A dashboard that only shows real-time data without persistent storage could be a gap. However, it says \"analyzes event logs,\" implying the logs are stored separately.\n- No mention of access controls or audit trails for who views/modifies the monitoring parameters — but this might be out of scope for a single proposal.\n- It's described as an engineering team building task, which is appropriate for a technical implementation.\n\nFrom my compliance vantage point:\n- This directly supports multiple Article 12 requirements and Recital 71 principles.\n- Real-time monitoring aligns with the \"lifetime\" requirement — you can't monitor what you don't observe continuously.\n- The reputation protection angle is valid: early detection prevents escalation into regulatory enforcement actions.\n- It's a focused, well-scoped proposal that maps to specific regulatory requirements.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a legitimate compliance-supporting infrastructure piece. It doesn't replace the logging requirement itself but adds the analytical/monitoring layer that makes those logs operationally useful for compliance verification