y for Annex III point 1(a) systems with specific minimum logging requirements (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, identification of natural persons).\n- The scope is somewhat bounded to Article 12 specifically. This is actually a good thing from a resource-allocation perspective — it's not an open-ended project.\n- \"Mathematically prove\" might set unrealistic expectations. Logs can be validated against criteria, but there will always be edge cases and interpretive questions about what counts as \"sufficient traceability.\" However, this doesn't invalidate the core approach.\n- The team would need to understand both the technical logging architecture AND the regulatory requirements. This is a cross-functional effort but manageable.\n\n**Resource-allocation assessment:**\n- This is a bounded, well-defined project with clear success criteria (logs meet Article 12 requirements).\n- It's reusable infrastructure — once built, it applies to every high-risk system we deploy.\n- It prevents costly regulatory non-compliance penalties and rework.\n- It uses existing R&D testing infrastructure rather than requiring new platforms.\n- The ROI is clear: build once, verify repeatedly across systems and over the lifetime of each system.\n\n**Scientific ambition vs. funding constraints:**\n- This doesn't distract from scientific work; it's a compliance enabler that allows us to actually deploy our research as products. Without it, we can't bring high-risk systems to market in the EU.\n- It's a necessary investment rather than an optional one for any lab with high-risk AI ambitions in the EU market.\n\n**Effective use of existing infrastructure:**\n- We likely already have test harnesses, log analyzers, and CI/CD pipelines. This proposal builds ON those rather than replacing them.\n- Synthetic test case generation can reuse existing data pipeline tools.\n\nMy assessment: This is a solid, bounded, necessary investment that aligns well with resource efficiency. 