nt is a separate concern that this proposal doesn't clearly address.\n- Wait, let me re-read: \"archive technical documentation automatically during the training process\" - this is specifically about training-time metadata capture, not runtime/operational event logging. The Article 12 requirement is broader - it's about logs over the lifetime of the system (which includes deployment), and specifically for Annex III point 1(a) systems, recording periods of use, reference databases, input data matches, etc.\n- So there's a scope gap: this addresses Recital 71's documentation requirements (technical documentation) but not Article 12's operational logging requirements per se. However, the proposal frames itself as serving \"the record-keeping requirements outlined in Recital 71\" - which is partially accurate but incomplete for full Article 12 compliance.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Building a custom centralized platform is heavy. For prototyping speed, you'd want to leverage existing tools (MLflow, DVC, W&B) rather than build from scratch. The proposal says \"implement a centralized tracking platform with custom hooks\" which suggests building new infrastructure.\n- However, if the lab already has some experiment tracking in place and just needs to add data lineage and documentation archiving, the incremental work could be manageable.\n- The promise of \"without requiring researchers to manually write reports for every single iteration\" is attractive from a prototyping speed perspective - removing manual steps accelerates iteration cycles.\n- Risk: over-engineering at the start. A full centralized platform with custom hooks could take months before it's useful, whereas a lighter-weight solution (even scripting) could get basic traceability in days/weeks.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\n- The core idea (automate metadata capture during training to support technical documentation requirements) is technically sound and well-motivated.\n- However, from my vantage