        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2542\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Article 12 obliges us to ensure our own high-risk systems technically allow automatic event recording and that logs capture the specific categories in paragraphs (2)–(3); it does not require—or even mention—publishing an open-source API standard or positioning ourselves as a community thought leader. The proposal diverts scarce engineering capacity toward an external-ecosystem deliverable while doing nothing to close our own compliance gap on logging capabilities, and it introduces reputational risk: if the EU's eventual implementing measures or CEN/CENELEC harmonised standards specify log formats that diverge from our 'standard,' we would have a public artefact conflicting with binding requirements. From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation), this is an activity whose primary beneficiary is the lab's brand narrative rather than its Article 12 posture.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 25\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to screen a single proposal (P2542) from the perspective of a Compliance Officer at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nLet me analyze Proposal P2542: \"Open-Standard AI Traceability Interface\" — an open-source API standardizing how high-risk AI systems expose internal logs to external auditors, released as a community standard through peer-reviewed publication, positioning the lab as a global thought leader.\n\nFrom my Compliance Officer vantage point:\n\n**Compliance with Article 12:**\n- Article 12(1) requires that high-risk AI systems \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" This is about the system's own logging capability, not about external interfaces.\n- Article 12(2) specifies what logs should capture: situations presenting risk, facilitating post-market monitoring, and monitoring operations per Art 26(5).\n- Article 12(3) specifies minimum logging for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification): recording periods of use, reference databases, input data matched, and identification of natural persons verifying results.\n\nThe proposal is about creating an *open-source API standard* for how logs are exposed to *external auditors*. This is a broader ecosystem initiative, not a direct implementation of Article 12's requirements in our own systems.\n\n**From the compliance perspective:**\n- The EU AI Act doesn't mandate that we create or adopt open-standard APIs for log exposure. It requires us to HAVE logging capabilities and maintain technical documentation. The interface through which auditors access logs is not specified as needing to be an \"open standard.\"\n- Article 12 focuses on the *system's* ability to record events automatically. The proposal addresses a downstream concern (how external parties consume those logs) rather than the core compliance obligation (the system must technically allow automatic recording).\n- However, having standardized log formats could facilitate compliance with Article 12 by ensuring our systems can produce structured, auditable records that align with what regulators expect.\n- The \"peer-reviewed publication\" and \"community standard\" angle is more about thought leadership and market positioning than about direct regulatory compliance.\n\n**Institutional survival:** Committing scarce resources to building an open-source community standard is a significant investment. It doesn't directly ensure our own systems comply with Article 12. We could be spending engineering time on an external standard rather than implementing the mandatory logging in our actual high-risk systems.\n\n**Market reputation:** Positioning as \"global thought leader in scientific definition of AI safety standards\" has some reputational upside, but it's somewhat tangential to what Article 12 actually requires. Regulators care about whether WE comply, not whether we publish a standard. Also, there's a risk: if our open-source standard doesn't perfectly align with how the EU eventually interprets or implements logging requirements (through implementing acts, harmonized standards by CEN/CENELEC), it could create confusion or even put us in a position where our \"standard\" conflicts with eventual regulatory specifications.\n\n**Risk considerations:**\n- Releasing an open-source API standard is a commitment that's hard to retract. If the EU's implementing measures specify different logging formats, we'd have a public standard that contradicts regulation.\n- The proposal doesn't address whether our own systems actually meet Article 12 requirements — it assumes they do and moves on to how to share the logs externally.\n- \"Open-source\" has its own compliance implications: licensing, security of internal log schemas potentially revealing system architecture.\n\n**My assessment:** This is a well-meaning but misdirected proposal from a compliance standpoint. It addresses an ecosystem-level question (standardization across industry) rather than our direct regulatory obligation under Article 12. Our obligation is to ensure OUR systems have the required logging capabilities and that we maintain technical documentation. Creating a community standard doesn't make us more compliant, and it diverts resources from actual implementation. The reputational angle is speculative and could backfire if the standard conflicts with eventual EU specifications. I would reject this on compliance grounds — it's not necessary for Article 12 compliance and creates resource risk without clear regulatory benefit to our institution specifically.\n\nHowever, there IS a modest argument that structured, well-defined log schemas (even if not \"open source community standards\") are part of good compliance practice under Article 12(2) which requires logging capabilities appropriate to the intended purpose. But the proposal as framed — building an open-source API standard for external auditors and publishing it peer-reviewed — goes well beyond what's needed and into thought-leadership territory that doesn't serve our core compliance obligation.\n\nDecision: Reject. The promise score should reflect that while there are some peripheral benefits (structured thinking about log formats), the proposal as a whole is not aligned with my function's priorities. I'd say around 25-30 — there's a kernel of useful thinking about structured logging, but the execution as proposed (open-source community standard, peer-reviewed publication, thought leadership positioning) is not what a compliance officer would prioritize.\n\nLet me settle on 28.\n",
