ons \"input data, reference database queries, and user IDs\" but doesn't explicitly mention start/end timestamps of each use period (Art. 12(3)(a)). However, it does say \"precision timestamping\" in the opening sentence, so this is likely covered.\n- It says \"across all active projects\" — but Article 12 applies specifically to high-risk AI systems. The proposal says \"all high-risk AI pipelines\" which is correctly scoped.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address the logging requirements of Article 12(2)(a)-(c) (identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation per Art. 26(5)). However, a well-designed framework would need to capture events relevant to these purposes. The proposal is somewhat high-level on what \"events\" are captured beyond the Annex III specific requirements.\n- No mention of data retention periods or storage security, which are practical compliance concerns but might be addressed in implementation.\n- \"Engineering team will develop\" — this is a commitment of engineering resources, which from my vantage point is fine since it's an institutional investment that serves regulatory compliance.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong, directly relevant proposal that addresses the core requirement of Article 12 in a systemic, institution-wide way. From a compliance officer's perspective, this is exactly the kind of infrastructure investment that reduces regulatory risk, ensures demonstrable compliance during audits, and protects market reputation by showing regulators we've built compliance into our engineering process rather than bolting it on per-project. It prevents the \"one team forgot to log\" scenario that could result in a non-conformity finding.\n\nThe slight gaps (explicit mention of 12(2) event categories, retention policies) are implementation details that would be addressed during development and don't undermine the fundamental soundness of the proposal. The core architecture is correct and well-s