ab developing AI systems subject to the Act, it's likely multiple projects will need this.\n- Maintenance burden: someone needs to keep the library updated as regulations evolve or as our systems change. But this is still more efficient than N separate teams maintaining N separate logging solutions.\n- Could create a bottleneck if the shared module becomes a single point of failure or if updates are slow.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation perspective, this is a strong proposal. It directly reduces duplicate engineering effort, which is one of the biggest hidden costs in compliance. The investment is bounded (build a logging library with templates based on Article 12 requirements), and the payoff scales with the number of high-risk AI projects. It also aligns well with \"effective use of existing R&D infrastructure\" — we're not asking for new hardware or exotic capabilities, just a software abstraction layer that teams can integrate. The balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints is improved because researchers spend less time on boilerplate compliance code and more time on their actual research questions.\n\nI would approve this. The promise score from my vantage point: This is highly aligned with my concerns about resource efficiency and infrastructure use. It's a pragmatic, well-scoped engineering investment that pays off across multiple projects. I'd give it a high score — maybe 85-90. It's not a 100 because there are some risks around maintenance overhead, adoption friction, and the initial build cost eating into short-term research capacity, but overall it's very much in my wheelhouse of \"do this to make the lab more efficient.\"\n\nLet me settle on 87.\n",
