nically feasible in its core form, but needs significant scoping refinement to avoid overpromising on automation coverage. From my vantage point, I'd want it scoped as \"auto-populate derivable technical documentation fields from existing CI/MLflow/Git infrastructure\" rather than the broader \"comprehensive compliance reports.\" But as a proposal for committing implementation resources, the core engineering task is well-defined enough to start prototyping quickly — build the schema mapping first, see how much coverage you get, then decide on scope.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The promise score should reflect how promising this is from MY vantage point (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping). \n\n- Technical viability: 7/10 for the core concept, but the proposal as stated has architectural concerns (\"scraping,\" over-scoped automation)\n- Speed benefit: Real but incremental — saves maybe a few hours per week of manual documentation\n- Quality risk: Auto-generated docs could create false compliance confidence if they don't flag gaps clearly\n\nI'll approve it because the core engineering task is viable and would genuinely help, but my promise score will be moderate — maybe 58-62. It's a useful tool but not transformative for prototyping speed, and the proposal as written has some technical naivety that would need correction during implementation.\n",
