ntific ambition.\n\n**Intellectual merit:**\n- The core algorithmic contribution (anomaly detection on time-series/structured logs) is well-trodden territory. Novelty would come from the specific domain application and the constraint of working only within Article 12's log schema.\n- There's a real question about whether the mandated logs contain enough signal richness to support meaningful scientific discovery, as opposed to being primarily audit/compliance metadata. The logs described (timestamps, reference DB, input matches, verifier IDs) are fairly coarse-grained operational records, not the kind of rich internal-state data that would enable deep model understanding.\n- The proposal conflates \"compliance logging\" with \"scientific telemetry.\" A real research program on drift detection would want far richer instrumentation than what Article 12 mandates. So there's a gap between the ambition and the available data.\n\n**Broader scientific impact:**\n- If successful, it could demonstrate that regulatory compliance data can serve dual purposes (compliance + safety science), which is a nice policy-relevant contribution.\n- The anomaly detection methods developed would likely be domain-specific and not broadly generalizable beyond this narrow log schema.\n- It doesn't push the frontier of AI interpretability, safety theory, or fundamental ML understanding in any meaningful way.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a reasonable applied research project with modest intellectual contribution. The framing as \"scientific discovery\" and \"interpretability\" overstates what's actually being proposed. The core methodological challenge (anomaly detection on structured operational logs) is not at the knowledge frontier. However, there IS a legitimate and interesting question: can compliance-grade logging provide actionable early-warning signal for model drift? That's a useful empirical study with some scientific value. It's not groundbreaking but it's not trivial either.\n\