irement in Recital 71. Manual documentation inevitably falls behind.\n- The integration point (experiment tracking → reporting engine) is a clean architectural boundary that's easy to prototype and iterate on.\n\n**Gaps/Concerns from my vantage:**\n- The proposal focuses on training-side artifacts (hyperparameters, dataset versions, validation metrics). Article 12(3) also requires logging of operational events (period of use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of natural persons in verification). This is a different technical domain — it's runtime/inference-time logging, not training-time metadata. The proposal doesn't address this.\n- However, the scope stated here (\"training hyperparameters, dataset versions, and validation metrics\") aligns with what Recital 71 calls out: \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes.\" So it covers part of the technical documentation requirement but not the logging requirements in Art 12(1)-(3).\n- As a development engineer, I'd want to know: what's the schema? What format are the reports (PDF, XML, structured JSON)? Who consumes them? The proposal is somewhat high-level on these specifics.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nThis is a technically viable, well-scoped engineering project that leverages existing infrastructure (experiment trackers) and adds a reporting layer. It addresses a genuine gap in the \"kept up to date\" requirement. From a prototyping speed perspective, this is very achievable — the hard part is requirements definition (what exactly goes in the report), not the technical implementation. The main concern is scope: it covers training/development documentation but not runtime logging, which is arguably the harder engineering problem under Art 12. But as a standalone piece, it's solid and shippable.\n\nI'd lean toward approve because:\n- Technically viable with existing tools\n- Fast to prototype (weeks)\n- Addresses a real requirement (documentation currency)\n- Clean archi