t carefully scoped, but the core deliverable is clear.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- Explicitly states integration into existing CI/CD pipeline. This means we're using what we already have rather than building parallel systems.\n- The version-controlled metadata registry concept aligns with practices already common in ML engineering (MLflow, DVC, etc.), so it's not requiring novel tooling development from scratch - more likely configuration and integration work.\n\n**Regulatory alignment (Article 12 / Recital 71):**\n- Directly addresses \"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system\" by making documentation dynamic rather than static.\n- Addresses \"comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform throughout their lifetime.\"\n- The linking of model checkpoints to training logs and risk assessments creates traceability that Article 12(2) requires.\n\n**Potential concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Real-time records\" could be interpreted very broadly and lead to scope creep. However, the specific deliverables (metadata registry, CI/CD integration, version-controlled linking) are concrete enough.\n- The cost of maintaining a queryable data store infrastructure is non-trivial but likely less than the cumulative cost of manual documentation updates over system lifetime.\n- One risk: if this becomes an end in itself (building the platform rather than using it for compliance), we could waste resources on over-engineering. But as stated, the scope seems reasonable.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-scoped proposal that leverages existing infrastructure, addresses a genuine regulatory requirement with efficiency gains over time, and doesn't require exotic new capabilities. From my resource-allocation perspective, this is a strong candidate - it's an integration project rather than a greenfield build, it reduces ongoing compliance costs, and it uses what we already have.\n\nI'd rate this highly. It's not 100 because \"real-time\" and \"dynamic, queryable data stores\" could introduce some scope risk, and the full build still requires engineering resources. But from my vantage point specifically (resource efficiency, existing infrastructure use, funding constraints), this is very well-aligned. I'd put it around 82-87.\n\nLet me settle on a decision: This clears my bar easily. The integration into existing CI/CD, the replacement of redundant static documentation with automated dynamic records, and the clear regulatory mapping make this an efficient use of resources. Approve.\n\nPromise score: 84. Strong from my vantage point but not perfect - some scope risk on \"real-time\" and \"dynamic queryable stores,\" and it still requires meaningful engineering investment upfront even if leveraged well.\n",
