t 1(a) systems (biometrics), but for other high-risk categories, the logging requirements are more principle-based (\"events relevant for\" identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, etc.). Making an automated validator work for the principle-based parts is harder than for the specific enumerated fields. However, you can operationalize those principles into concrete checkable conditions (e.g., \"does every log entry have a timestamp?\" \"is there a field indicating which risk scenario triggered?\" etc.).\n- The tool itself is low-risk to prototype: it's scripts + possibly a small service. No novel ML required.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is highly prototypable. A first useful version could be built in days to a couple of weeks — define the field schema, write validation logic, integrate with log output.\n- It directly improves prototyping quality: by catching logging gaps early (during R&D), it prevents rework later. This aligns well with my domain concern.\n- It's composable: each Article 12 requirement can be a separate validator module, so you can incrementally add checks as the system evolves.\n- The \"real-time\" aspect adds some complexity but not much — a simple event-driven check on log emission is doable quickly.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"All Article 12 requirements\" might be scope creep if interpreted too broadly (principle-based vs. specific fields). But even so, the technical approach (checklist/validation scripts) remains viable.\n- It's a supporting tool, not the core system — but that's fine; it reduces risk for the core system's prototyping.\n- Maintenance burden: as regulations get amended or interpretations evolve, the checklist needs updating. But this is minor and manageable.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point: This is technically very viable, easy to prototype quickly, directly improves the speed and quality of our R&D workflow by catching issues early, and has a well-bounded scope. The \"real-time\"