sk AI system could face significant penalties (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover under Art. 99).\n- Market reputation: demonstrating a systematic, institution-wide compliance infrastructure signals regulatory seriousness to market supervisors and counterparties.\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input references, and user IDs\" but Article 12(2) requires logging events relevant for (a) identifying risk situations or substantial modifications, (b) facilitating post-market monitoring, and (c) monitoring operation per Art 26(5). The proposal's description is somewhat narrow — it focuses on the Annex III point 1(a) minimums but may not fully address the broader traceability requirements of 12(2)(a)-(c).\n- \"Plug-and-play\" across \"diverse AI architectures\" is ambitious. Different high-risk systems have very different event structures. A truly generic library might either be too thin to capture meaningful compliance-relevant events, or require so much configuration that it's not really plug-and-play. This is a technical risk, but from my compliance vantage point, the key question is whether the output meets the regulatory standard.\n- The proposal doesn't mention retention periods, log immutability/tamper-proofing, format requirements for regulatory inspection, or how logs will be made available to authorities. Article 12 and related provisions (like Art. 8 on documentation) suggest logs need to be accessible and potentially produced to notified bodies or market surveillance authorities.\n- No mention of interaction with the broader technical documentation requirement under Article 17/Annex IV, which Recital 71 emphasizes should be kept up to date.\n\nHowever, as a screening exercise: this proposal is squarely on-target for my compliance mandate. It addresses the core obligation of Article 12(1) in a systematic way. The fact that it's a library (reusable across projects) rather than a one-off implementation is actually superior from a compliance standpoint because it creates institutional consistency. Even if the technical description is somewhat high-level, the direction is exactly right for reducing regulatory risk institutionally.\n\nThe concern about breadth (12(2)(a)-(c) beyond just Annex III 1(a)) is real but doesn't disqualify the proposal — it's a scope consideration that can be addressed in implementation. The core compliance architecture is sound.\n\nFor institutional survival: if we get hit with an enforcement action and show we have no systematic logging infrastructure, that looks like willful negligence. Having this library demonstrates organizational commitment to compliance.\n\nDecision: Approve. This is directly on-mission for my function. It reduces the probability of non-compliance across all projects simultaneously, creates a single auditable system, and signals institutional seriousness.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my mandate. The slight deductions are because (1) the description is somewhat narrow relative to full Article 12 scope (especially 12(2)(a)-(c) beyond Annex III), (2) no mention of log integrity, retention, or authority access requirements, and (3) \"plug-and-play across diverse architectures\" may be overpromising technically. But the core direction is exactly right for compliance. I'll go with 83.\n",
