"modular wrapper\" which suggests extensibility. Two engineers is a reasonable resource allocation for a shared library. Building on existing telemetry tools reduces development time and leverages proven infrastructure.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:** A unified logging library creates a single point of compliance accountability. If a regulator audits the institution, we can demonstrate that ALL high-risk systems have compliant logging through one standardized mechanism. This is much stronger than saying \"each project built its own thing.\" It also makes it easier to update when regulations change or when post-market monitoring reveals new event types need capturing.\n\n**Market reputation angle:** Being able to show customers and partners that we have a unified, auditable compliance infrastructure for AI logging is a significant differentiator in the B2B market, especially as EU AI Act enforcement ramps up.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Two engineers\" might be optimistic if the library needs to handle diverse system architectures, but for a wrapper around existing telemetry tools, it's plausible.\n- The proposal doesn't explicitly address retention periods or log integrity/immunity (though that may be covered elsewhere).\n- It focuses on Annex III 1(a) specific fields but should also cover general Article 12(1)-(2) requirements for ALL high-risk systems.\n\nOverall, this is a strong compliance proposal. It directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12 by creating a standardized mechanism. The risk of NOT having this (each project doing it differently or incompletely) is a significant regulatory exposure. I'd approve this.\n\nPromise score: This is very well-aligned with my function's concerns. It reduces regulatory risk, creates audit-ready infrastructure, and protects market reputation. However, the scope as described might be slightly narrow (focused on specific fields rather than the full Article 12(2) event categories), and two engineers might be tight if