of specific risk behaviors\" is an extremely strong claim. Formal verification of absence properties over unbounded operational logs is, in practice, often intractable or requires very restrictive assumptions. This could consume significant researcher time without yielding publishable results if the problem is fundamentally harder than assumed.\n- The proposal doesn't specify which systems it targets, what \"specific risk behaviors\" means operationally, or what subset of Article 12(3) logs it uses. Without that scoping, resource commitment is hard to bound.\n- It's a research project with publication as the primary output. As Lab Director focused on resource efficiency and funding constraints, I need to ask: does this convert into operational capability that reduces our compliance burden or risk exposure, or is it purely academic? The \"near-miss failure\" identification could have operational value, but the formal verification angle seems more academic than practical.\n- However, even if the full mathematical proof aspect doesn't pan out, the anomaly detection on logs component is directly useful for post-market monitoring (Article 72) and operational oversight (Article 26(5)). So there's a fallback value.\n\n**My assessment:** The key strength from my vantage point is that this proposal repurposes an existing mandatory artifact (logs) into a research substrate without requiring new capital expenditure. It uses existing R&D infrastructure (the logging systems we must build anyway). The researcher time required is moderate — it's an analysis/interpretation layer on top of data already being generated. The risk is scope creep into intractable formal verification problems, but the anomaly detection component alone has clear operational value for post-market monitoring.\n\nThe scientific ambition is real but potentially overreaching in the \"mathematically prove absence\" framing. However, as a research project (not an engineering deliverable), it's appropriate to explore ambitious questions — that's what R&D labs do. The publication output also serves institutional credibility with regulators who are still interpreting the Act.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's resource-efficient because it builds on mandatory infrastructure, it has clear operational fallback value (anomaly detection for post-market monitoring), and the scientific ambition is reasonable for an exploratory project. I'd approve but note the scoping risk in my rationale. My promise score would be moderate-high — maybe 72-78. It's promising because of the dual-use infrastructure argument, but I'm not at 90+ because the \"mathematical proof\" framing introduces uncertainty about whether the project will yield publishable results within a reasonable timeframe and budget.\n\nLet me settle on 74. The core idea (mining mandatory logs for safety signals) is excellent from an efficiency standpoint, but the formal verification ambition adds execution risk that could waste resources if not carefully scoped.\n",
