ake regulatory obligations seriously.\n\n**Concerns/Risks:**\n- The proposal says \"across various AI architectures\" — this is ambitious. R&D institutions work with rapidly evolving model architectures. A middleware layer that works today might not handle novel architectures developed in 18 months. This could create compliance gaps for newer systems until the framework is updated.\n- \"Guarantees we meet logging requirements by default\" — this is a strong claim. Article 12(2) requires traceability \"appropriate to the intended purpose of the system.\" A one-size-fits-all middleware may not capture events relevant to every specific use case. Compliance isn't just about having logs; it's about having the RIGHT logs for the right purpose.\n- The framework needs to handle Article 12(3)'s specific minimum requirements for biometric identification systems (Annex III 1(a)). Does this proposal account for that specificity, or is it generic?\n- As a compliance officer, I'm also concerned about data protection implications — logging all inputs/outputs could create GDPR issues if personal data is involved. The framework needs built-in privacy safeguards.\n- \"Eliminates fragmented record-keeping\" — good intent, but in an R&D institution where teams work semi-autonomously, adoption and integration across projects can be challenging. However, from a compliance perspective, mandating this as institutional policy (which a compliance officer would support) makes it more enforceable.\n- The proposal commits resources to building and maintaining this infrastructure. For a compliance officer, this is exactly the kind of investment that protects institutional survival — without proper logging, we face penalties under Article 99 (up to €35M or 7% global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations).\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective, this is squarely within my domain and addresses the core technical requirement of Article 