he specific data points needed under Article 12(3)(a)-(d) for Annex III point 1(a) systems. Timestamps, input references, system events — these map well to the regulatory text.\n\nConcerns/limitations:\n1. **Upfront cost**: Developing a \"plug-and-play module\" and centralized database requires significant upfront engineering investment before any project benefits. This is a capital expenditure against operating budget constraints. If the lab only has one or two high-risk projects, the ROI might not be as compelling.\n2. **Rigidity risk**: A single standardized framework may not accommodate all the diverse logging needs across different types of high-risk AI systems (Annex III covers very different domains — biometrics, critical infrastructure, employment, etc.). The flexibility of a \"common API\" is questioned.\n3. **\"Centralized secure database\"**: This could become a bottleneck or single point of failure. Also, security/compliance of the centralized store itself adds overhead.\n4. **Scope creep risk**: \"Eliminates redundant development effort\" sounds good in principle, but in practice, getting all research teams to adopt and correctly use a shared library can consume significant coordination and change-management resources.\n\nOverall assessment: From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is a strong proposal. The core idea — don't build the same compliance logging infrastructure N times for N projects — is exactly right. It directly addresses my domain concerns about stretching limited engineering resources and protecting budget for scientific work. The alignment with Article 12's specific requirements (timestamps, input data references, system events) shows regulatory awareness.\n\nHowever, I need to be careful: the proposal as stated is somewhat high-level (\"standardized internal software library,\" \"plug-and-play module\"). It doesn't address the specificity of different Annex III categories' logging needs, nor does it discuss maintenance b