p to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime of the AI system.\" An automated pipeline that triggers on model versioning directly addresses this \"up-to-date\" requirement without relying on human discipline.\n5. **Scales with the lab's output**: As the lab produces more model versions, the marginal cost of documentation stays roughly flat (automated), rather than growing linearly with manual effort.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n1. The proposal says \"regulatory-compliant format\" but doesn't specify which templates or who validates that the generated documents actually meet Article 12(2) and (3) requirements in full. There's a risk of automation creating false confidence — the docs get generated but might miss edge cases or specific requirements for particular Annex III categories.\n2. \"System limitations\" documentation is harder to auto-generate than hyperparameters and metrics. Limitations often require expert judgment, not just extraction from logs. The proposal glosses over this.\n3. It doesn't address Article 12(1) automatic event logging (the technical capability of the system itself), only the documentation side. But that's arguably a different workstream.\n4. Integration risk: tying to \"experiment tracking logs\" assumes those logs are comprehensive and well-structured. If they aren't, this becomes a data-cleaning project in disguise, which can balloon in scope and cost.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a high-leverage, relatively low-cost proposal that directly serves resource efficiency. It uses existing infrastructure (experiment tracking), automates a recurring task to free up scarce engineering resources for actual R&D, and scales well. The main risk is scope creep or underestimating the \"limitations documentation\" aspect, but the core concept — templating engine + experiment log integration triggered at versioning events — is sound and efficient.\n\nI would approve this. It's exactly the kind of proposal that a resource-constrained R&D lab should pursue: modest build cost, ongoing operational savings, leverages what's already there, and directly addresses a regulatory obligation without requiring new headcount or major infrastructure investment.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 78-82. It's clearly valuable from my perspective but has some implementation risks (the \"limitations\" auto-generation, assumption about log quality) that prevent me from giving it a 90+.\n",
