em states\" but doesn't explicitly address all the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (reference database, input data matches, identification of natural persons). However, as a framework/library, it could be extended to handle those — the proposal says \"baseline regulatory compliance.\"\n- The word \"baseline\" is important — it suggests this handles the general Article 12(1)-(2) requirements but may not cover all domain-specific logging needs. That's actually appropriate for a middleware layer; domain-specific elements would still need project-level implementation.\n- Risk: A single framework could become a single point of failure. If the library has a bug, ALL high-risk systems are affected. However, this is more of an engineering risk than a compliance one per se — and from a compliance standpoint, having one auditable system is actually better than many disparate ones.\n- The proposal doesn't mention data retention periods, access controls for logs (who can view/modify), or integration with post-market monitoring systems (Article 72). These are important compliance elements but might be considered implementation details rather than framework design issues.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- Without a unified approach, the institution faces fragmented compliance efforts. In an audit or market surveillance activity, demonstrating 5 different logging approaches across 5 projects is far more vulnerable than demonstrating one coherent system. This directly protects institutional survival.\n- Regulatory enforcement under the EU AI Act carries significant penalties (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices; up to €15M or 3% for other violations). Inadequate logging could be cited as a failure in multiple compliance areas simultaneously.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- A unified, well-documented logging framework signals organizational maturity. When interacting with notified bodies, market surveillance authorities, or business partners who due-diligence our AI systems, having a coherent story about how we handle Article 12 is a significant reputational asset.\n\n**Decision assessment:**\nThis is clearly aligned with my compliance mandate. It addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) directly, creates institutional consistency (reducing audit risk), and is practically necessary for an R&D institution with multiple high-risk projects. The \"baseline\" qualifier is appropriate — it doesn't overpromise to solve every domain-specific logging need but establishes the foundational infrastructure.\n\nI would approve this. It's a core compliance enabler, not a nice-to-have. Without it, the institution cannot reliably demonstrate Article 12 compliance across its portfolio of high-risk systems.\n\nPromise score: This is very promising from my vantage point. It directly addresses a mandatory requirement, reduces institutional risk, and creates a single audit trail. The main reservation is that \"baseline\" means additional work will be needed for specific Annex III categories, but the framework approach is correct. I'd put this at around 82-85. It's not a 95+ because it doesn't explicitly address all the Article 12(3) specifics or retention/access policies, and \"immutable\" needs to be carefully defined (what about corrections? GDPR considerations for logged personal data in Article 12(3)(d)?). But it's strongly aligned with my mandate.\n\nI'll go with 84.\n",
