2)) requires deep domain knowledge of how the system works — which is precisely the researcher's expertise. A two-person team spread across multiple R&D pods may not have the scientific depth to determine what constitutes a meaningful log event for each distinct research context.\n- The proposal doesn't address how this structure preserves or enhances intellectual merit. It's purely an operational/organizational restructuring. It doesn't advance the knowledge frontier, it doesn't improve the quality of research output, and its \"broader scientific impact\" is limited to possibly freeing some researcher hours.\n- Two compliance engineers for multiple pods is a very thin allocation. The risk is that they become bottlenecked, and researchers still end up doing significant collaborative work with them (negotiating what to log, how to interpret events), which doesn't actually free researcher time as promised.\n- From a \"broader scientific impact\" angle, the proposal doesn't contribute anything to the field's understanding of AI systems. It's an internal organizational change with no external scientific value.\n- The framing of \"protecting our scientific ambition and research velocity by offloading engineering overhead\" is somewhat reductive. Good research practice includes building systems that are observable, debuggable, and well-documented — which overlaps significantly with what Article 12 requires. Treating this as a burden to be eliminated rather than an opportunity to build better scientific infrastructure is a missed chance.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is primarily an operational/resource allocation proposal dressed up in research-protection language. It doesn't advance the knowledge frontier, it doesn't enhance intellectual merit of the research itself, and its broader scientific impact is minimal at best. The assumption that compliance engineering can be cleanly separated from scientific understanding of the system is questionable.