cific compliance document sections is non-trivial. This is an upfront investment.\n- Scope creep risk: \"transforms static documents into living records\" could balloon. The proposal needs to be scoped tightly to what the Act actually requires rather than becoming a full documentation platform.\n- However, the proposal specifically says it \"removes the burden of manual updating from research teams\" — this is directly about freeing R&D capacity for actual scientific work, which is my core concern.\n- The integration with existing version control means we're not building parallel infrastructure; we're extending what's already there.\n\n**Assessment:**\nFrom a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is one of the more cost-effective compliance investments I can imagine. The alternative — having researchers manually update technical documentation after every code change or data modification — is a direct tax on scientific productivity. The proposal leverages existing infrastructure (version control) and automates what would otherwise be ongoing manual labor. The upfront build cost is bounded, while the savings are continuous across the system's lifetime.\n\nThe main risk is scope: if this becomes a full document management platform rather than a focused automation layer on top of git, it could consume disproportionate engineering resources. But as described — \"version control triggers updates to compliance dossiers via integrated templates\" — it sounds appropriately scoped.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's efficient use of existing infrastructure, it frees researcher time for actual science (scientific ambition), and the ongoing cost is low relative to the compliance risk it mitigates. The funding constraint angle favors it: once built, the marginal cost per documentation update is near zero versus the recurring human hours of manual updating.\n\nDecision: Approve.\nPromise score: This is a strong fit for my vantage point. It's efficient, leverages existi