isk (inconsistent implementations across projects create compliance blind spots)\n- A unified framework makes it easier to demonstrate compliance to regulators during audits\n- The \"lifetime\" aspect is addressed by building logging into the pipeline architecture rather than as an afterthought\n- Market reputation: having a robust, unified compliance infrastructure signals serious commitment to regulatory obligations\n\nConcerns/limitations from my vantage point:\n- Does it specifically address Article 12(3) requirements for Annex III 1(a) systems? The proposal mentions \"event tracking and data lineage\" but doesn't explicitly call out the specific logging requirements (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, natural person identification). However, as a framework, it could be designed to accommodate these.\n- The \"small engineering team\" might be underestimating the complexity of meeting all Article 12 requirements plus Recital 71's broader documentation expectations.\n- \"Plug-and-play\" is appealing but compliance requires that the logging actually captures what regulators need — a generic framework might miss system-specific risk events (Article 12(2)(a)).\n- The proposal focuses on implementation efficiency rather than regulatory specificity.\n\nOverall: This is a strong, directly relevant proposal to Article 12 compliance. It addresses the core mandate of automatic recording over lifetime, promotes consistency (which regulators value), and reduces institutional risk by ensuring no team inadvertently fails to implement adequate logging. As a Compliance Officer focused on institutional survival and regulatory compliance, this is squarely in my wheelhouse.\n\nI'd approve this. The promise score should be high — maybe 82-85. It's not a 100 because the proposal as stated is somewhat generic and doesn't explicitly map to all sub-requirements of Article 12 (especially 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems), but it's clearly the right architectural ap