he development teams to provide exact reference database and human verifier identity\" — if logs are properly structured per Article 12, this should be straightforward. If they're not, it's identifying gaps that need fixing (which is valuable but also means there's implementation work lurking).\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:** This proposal leverages existing logs and documentation rather than requiring new tools or systems. It's essentially a process/workflow exercise using what we already have. That's efficient from my perspective.\n\nThe strength: it's low-cost, uses existing personnel and infrastructure, directly addresses Article 12 compliance (which is mandatory for high-risk systems), and catches problems before regulators do. The \"document gaps found\" part creates a feedback loop that could inform where to direct implementation resources.\n\nThe weakness from my vantage point: periodic audits are somewhat duplicative of what good project management would catch anyway. If our logging infrastructure is properly built (which is a separate implementation effort), these checks should be trivial. The proposal doesn't specify frequency, which could range from very lightweight (quarterly 2-hour exercise) to resource-intensive (monthly multi-day exercises). Without specification, there's risk of scope creep into becoming a standing operational burden on development teams.\n\nAlso: \"challenging development teams\" — as Lab Director, I'm sensitive to any proposal that creates adversarial dynamics between functions or pulls engineers away from their primary work in an unstructured way. But if framed as periodic and structured, this is manageable.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a reasonable, low-cost compliance activity that uses existing resources efficiently. It's not asking for new funding or infrastructure. The main concern is frequency and scope management to prevent it from becoming a drain on R&D time. But the proposal as stated seems approp