mpliance posture, which is favorable in regulatory interactions.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Demonstrating that we go beyond minimum compliance and use our logging infrastructure intelligently enhances our reputation with regulators, clients, and the market.\n- It positions us as a sophisticated actor in AI governance.\n- \"Transforming a regulatory burden into a scientific asset\" is good PR language but also practically true — the data is already there.\n\n**Risks/Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- This is framed as a \"research initiative\" — it's exploratory rather than deterministic. As compliance officer, I need to ensure that our compliance obligations are met through engineered solutions, not research bets. However, this doesn't replace the obligation to build the logging system; it enhances its utility.\n- Unsupervised learning for drift detection is a well-established field (MLOps), so the technical risk is moderate.\n- The scope seems bounded: parse logs → detect patterns → flag risks. This isn't an open-ended research project.\n- One concern: if this is positioned as \"research,\" there's a risk it gets deprioritized or fails to produce actionable outputs in time for regulatory deadlines. But the proposal itself doesn't say that — leadership will decide on timing.\n\n**Resource commitment:**\n- The lab has already committed resources to building Article 12-compliant logging (it's mandatory). This proposal adds an analytics layer on top. The marginal cost is the ML engineering work, which is less than building a new system from scratch.\n- It reuses existing data infrastructure.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with my compliance mandate. Article 12 doesn't just say \"store logs\" — it says logs must be capable of enabling risk identification and post-market monitoring. A tool that actively parses those logs to detect drift and risk triggers is essentially the operational implementation of Articles 12(2)(a) and (b), Article 72, and Article 26(5). Without some form of analysis capability, we'd be storing data but not actually \"facilitating post-market monitoring\" or \"identifying situations that may result in risk.\" This proposal closes a gap between having logs and actually using them for the purposes the Act mandates.\n\nI would approve this. It's directly supportive of compliance obligations, reduces regulatory risk, enhances reputation, and leverages infrastructure we're building anyway. The promise score should be high — maybe 85-90. I'd hold back from 100 because it's framed as a \"research initiative\" which introduces some uncertainty about deliverables and timelines, and as compliance officer I prefer deterministic engineering solutions over research bets. But the alignment with my mandate is very strong.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise = 87.\n",
