der: could there be a modest positive? Standardized recording infrastructure could incidentally improve data provenance and reproducibility in research settings. But the proposal as stated is framed entirely in compliance terms — \"timestamps, input references, session IDs\" — which are operational/audit events, not scientific metadata (model versions, hyperparameters, training data provenance, evaluation metrics).\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is a necessary engineering task but has minimal intellectual merit or research advancement. It doesn't push the knowledge frontier, it doesn't contribute to scientific understanding of AI systems, and its broader impact is confined to regulatory compliance infrastructure. It's useful work but not research-advancing work.\n\nMy assessment: This clears the bar for being a reasonable engineering initiative, but from the specific vantage point of knowledge frontier / intellectual merit / broader scientific impact, it falls short. It's infrastructure plumbing for compliance. I would reject it from my perspective because it doesn't advance research or contribute to scientific understanding. However, it's not zero — there's a tiny sliver of reproducibility benefit if implemented well.\n\nI'll assign a low promise score, maybe 15-20, and reject.\n",
