atabase against which input data has been checked - \"input matches\" partially covers this but not explicitly the reference DB ✓/partial\n(c) input data for which search led to a match - \"input matches\" covers this ✓\n(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art 14(5)) - \"user IDs\" covers this ✓\n\n**Strengths from compliance perspective:**\n- Directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic recording\n- Standardized approach means consistent compliance across all high-risk systems (reduces risk of gaps)\n- CI/CD integration ensures it's baked in from deployment, not an afterthought\n- \"Prevents costly retrofitting\" is a strong institutional survival point - if we build it wrong and have to redo, that's expensive and risky\n- Centralized system makes audit trails easier for regulators\n- Addresses the Annex III 1(a) minimum requirements reasonably well\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- It's somewhat high-level; doesn't explicitly map to all of Article 12(2)(a), (b), (c) event types\n- \"User IDs\" is vague - does it capture the specific verification role required by Art 14(5)? Or just any user?\n- The reference database logging (Art 12(3)(b)) isn't explicitly called out, though \"input matches\" might imply it\n- No mention of log retention period or data integrity measures (though these might be in the technical spec)\n- No mention of how logs will be made available to notified bodies or market surveillance authorities\n- The proposal is engineering-focused; as compliance officer I'd want explicit mapping to each sub-article requirement\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" - does the centralized database have a retention strategy that matches the AI system's lifecycle?\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- A unified framework is actually smart for institutional risk management. If we build 5 separate logging systems, we have 5 points of potential non-compliance. One standardized framework = one thing to get right and maintain.\n- CI/CD integration means new models can't be deployed without the logging capability - this is a strong control.\n- Prevents retrofitting costs which could be significant if we scale up high-risk systems.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- Demonstrating a unified, well-designed compliance infrastructure to regulators and clients shows institutional maturity.\n- Easier to present in audits - one system rather than fragmented logs.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid proposal that addresses the core of Article 12. It's not perfect - it could be more specific about which event types correspond to Art 12(2)(a), (b), (c) - but as an initial framework/approach, it hits the right targets. The CI/CD integration is particularly valuable from a compliance standpoint because it creates a structural control that prevents non-compliant deployments.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It directly addresses the regulatory requirement, reduces institutional risk through standardization, and the CI/CD gate is a strong compliance control. The gaps (explicit event-type mapping, retention policy, regulator access) are implementation details that would be in the technical specification rather than requiring rejection of the overall approach.\n\nI'd give it around 72-78. It's clearly aligned with what Article 12 requires and the institutional strategy is sound. I'm docking points for:\n- Not explicitly mapping to all Art 12(2) sub-categories\n- Vague on retention/lifetime aspects\n- Doesn't mention log integrity, accessibility to regulators\n- \"User IDs\" needs to be more specific about verification roles (Art 14(5))\n\nI'll go with 74. It's a strong proposal that I'd approve but want the engineering team to tighten up the spec against each sub-article before implementation begins.\n",
