y reports\" means when a regulator comes asking, we're ready\n5. Reduces the risk of non-compliance due to outdated or incomplete documentation\n6. Supports traceability requirements in Article 12(2)\n7. The fact that it's automated reduces the burden on compliance staff and reduces human error\n8. \"Without disrupting R&D workflow\" is important for institutional survival - you can't have compliance overhead so heavy it strangles innovation\n\n**Concerns from compliance perspective:**\n1. It says \"pulls metadata from Git and training logs\" - but does it capture ALL the elements required? Article 79(1) risk situations, substantial modifications, post-market monitoring data (Art 72), operation monitoring (Art 26(5))? The proposal seems focused on development documentation rather than operational logging.\n2. It doesn't explicitly address the specific logging requirements in Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, verification personnel identification)\n3. \"Transforms raw development data into regulatory-ready reports\" - who defines what's \"regulatory-ready\"? There's a risk the tool might not capture all required elements.\n4. The proposal is somewhat vague on governance: who validates that the auto-generated documentation is actually complete and accurate? Automated compliance can create false confidence.\n5. It doesn't mention retention periods, access controls for regulators, or format requirements.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\nHaving automated, synchronized technical documentation significantly reduces the risk of failing a regulatory inspection due to incomplete records. This is a direct institutional survival benefit. If a regulator comes and we can't produce current technical documentation, that's a serious compliance failure.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\nBeing able to demonstrate robust, automated compliance infrastructure signals to market partners, clients, and regulators th