rithms, data, training/testing/validation processes, risk-management system documentation. The proposal captures datasets, hyperparameters, system states — this aligns well with the \"algorithms, data, training, testing and validation processes\" mentioned in Recital 71.\n\n**Compliance benefits:**\n- Automation reduces human error in record-keeping (critical for compliance)\n- Full lineage capture means you can reconstruct what happened at any point (traceability)\n- Queryable database enables regulatory audits\n- Versioning ensures records reflect the state at the time of operation\n- \"Over the lifetime\" is addressed by continuous logging\n- Eliminates manual documentation gaps that are common audit findings\n\n**Risks/concerns from compliance perspective:**\n- The proposal focuses heavily on R&D experiment lineage. Article 12 applies to the high-risk AI *system* in deployment, not just research experiments. However, having full provenance from development through deployment strengthens the compliance story significantly.\n- \"Over the lifetime of the system\" — need to ensure this doesn't stop at lab stage but extends into production/operational life. The proposal says \"execution telemetry directly into a queryable database\" which could extend beyond R&D.\n- For Annex III 1(a) systems, specific requirements (d) about identifying natural persons in verification may not be fully addressed by \"metadata hooks into training frameworks.\" But this is a general infrastructure layer that could support those.\n- The proposal says it \"satisfies legal traceability\" — as a compliance officer, I'd note that it's a strong foundation but the specific Annex III 1(a) requirements need explicit mapping. However, the proposal doesn't claim to be exhaustive; it's a foundational layer.\n\n**Market reputation:**\n- Demonstrating robust automated provenance tracking is a strong market signal of regulatory seriousness\n- Reduces audit risk and potential non-conformity findings\n-