        "reasoning_content": "The user wants me to evaluate proposal P2688 from the vantage point of a Development Engineer at an R&D institution focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal:\n\n\"Efficient Audit Trail Compression Research. A research project aimed at developing verifiable methods for storing massive event logs without compromising the traceability required by Article 12. It focuses on reducing data footprints. Researchers will explore sketching algorithms and cryptographic hashing to create condensed yet audit-ready records of system inputs and outputs. This transforms a regulatory cost center into an opportunity for scientific innovation in high-efficiency data management.\"\n\nFrom my vantage point as a Development Engineer concerned with technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping:\n\n1. **Technical viability concerns:**\n   - Article 12 requires \"automatic recording of events (logs)\" over the lifetime of the system. The key word here is that these logs must enable traceability, identifying risks, facilitating post-market monitoring, etc.\n   - Compression via sketching algorithms and cryptographic hashing: There's a fundamental tension here. Sketching algorithms (like Count-Min sketches, HyperLogLog) are lossy by nature — they give approximate results. For audit trails that need to identify specific events, specific inputs/outputs, and trace exact sequences, lossy compression is problematic. You can't reconstruct the actual input data or the exact reference database used from a sketch.\n   - Cryptographic hashing (e.g., Merkle trees) preserves integrity verification but doesn't let you \"read back\" the original data — you can verify that something was logged, but you can't retrieve what it actually said unless you store the original alongside the hash.\n   - Article 12(3) specifically requires recording actual input data, reference databases used, identification of natural persons involved in verification. These are concrete data points that can't be meaningfully \"sketched\" or hashed into a condensed form while remaining audit-ready for regulators who need to inspect specific records.\n\n2. **Speed/quality of prototyping concerns:**\n   - This is framed as a \"research project\" — it's exploratory, not a deliverable prototype. From my vantage point, this doesn't give me a working logging system I can integrate into a high-risk AI product within the compliance timeline.\n   - The EU AI Act has specific deadlines (high-risk systems under Annex III: August 2026 for most). We need implementable solutions NOW, not research into whether compression might work someday.\n   - \"Verifiable methods\" and \"audit-ready records\" are vague — what does audit-ready mean to a regulator? If a national supervisory authority asks to see the log of a specific event on a specific date, can I produce it from a compressed sketch? Probably not in the form they need.\n   - The framing as \"transforming a regulatory cost center into an opportunity for scientific innovation\" is marketing language that masks the fact this doesn't solve the immediate prototyping need.\n\n3. **Practical engineering perspective:**\n   - Standard structured logging (JSON, protobuf) with append-only storage, retention policies, and integrity checks (hash chains) already solves 90% of what Article 12 requires. The \"massive\" data footprint concern is real but solvable with standard engineering practices (tiered storage, cold/warm/hot data separation).\n   - The research into novel compression doesn't unblock any prototyping bottleneck I face right now.\n   - There's a risk that if we build logging on top of an experimental compression layer and it fails audit scrutiny, we'd have to rebuild the entire pipeline — high rework cost.\n\n4. **Where there IS merit:**\n   - For very large-scale systems (e.g., biometric matching under Annex III 1(a)), log volume could genuinely be enormous.\n   - Hash chains for integrity verification are actually a sensible engineering practice and align with \"verifiable\" requirements.\n   - But this is more of an implementation detail than a research project.\n\nMy assessment: This proposal is not technically viable as a solution to Article 12 compliance in the near term. The core regulatory requirement is that specific, identifiable events be recorded and retrievable — lossy compression fundamentally conflicts with this. It doesn't accelerate my prototyping; it introduces experimental dependencies into what should be a straightforward engineering task (structured logging + integrity verification). From speed/quality of prototyping standpoint, I need working logging infrastructure now, not research into whether we can make logs smaller in 2-3 years.\n\nDecision: Reject.\nPromise score: Maybe around 15-20. There's some legitimate interest in efficient storage at scale, and hash-chain integrity is a good pattern, but as framed — a research project using sketching algorithms for \"condensed yet audit-ready records\" — it doesn't meet the bar for technical viability or prototyping speed. The lossy nature of sketching fundamentally conflicts with the specific data recording requirements in Article 12(3). I'll give it around 15.\n",
