r market reputation: demonstrating a structured, documented compliance process signals seriousness to partners, clients, and regulators.\n\n**Weaknesses/limitations:**\n- The proposal is somewhat generic in its execution details — it says \"a comprehensive internal review process\" without specifying methodology, timeline, or accountability structures. But as a screening decision, the conceptual soundness matters more than implementation detail at this stage.\n- It relies on \"the legal department\" to do the classification mapping. From my compliance vantage point, I'd want this to be a joint effort (legal + compliance + technical), but that's an implementation detail, not a fundamental flaw in the proposal itself.\n- The scope is appropriate: it's about classification and gap identification, not trying to solve every logging problem at once. This is resource-efficient.\n\n**From institutional survival:** If we don't know which systems are high-risk, we can't comply with Article 12 (or Articles 8-15, 72, 79, etc.). This is a prerequisite for all other compliance work. The risk of NOT doing this is severe — it's the difference between a targeted remediation and a catastrophic \"we didn't even know\" scenario during a regulatory inspection.\n\n**From regulatory compliance:** This directly supports compliance with Article 12 by ensuring we apply it to the correct scope. It also supports Articles 8-15 (requirements), 72 (post-market monitoring), 79 (serious incidents) by establishing which systems trigger those obligations.\n\n**From market reputation:** A documented, systematic classification process demonstrates regulatory maturity. If a partner or client asks \"how do you ensure your AI complies with the EU AI Act?\", having this audit trail is powerful. Conversely, being caught without any such analysis would be reputationally devastating.\n\nThis proposal clears my bar easily. It's foundational, low-risk to pursue (it's an internal review process, not a massive engineering commitment), and the downside of NOT doing it is severe for all three of my concern areas.\n\nPromise score: I'd give this very high — maybe 90-93. It's nearly essential. The only reason I'm not at 100 is that as stated, it's somewhat procedural without specifying governance structure (who signs off on classifications? What happens when classification is borderline? Is there a review cycle?). But the core concept is exactly right and foundational.\n\nDecision: approve\nPromise: 92\n",
