        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P2489 is about creating an \"Open Standard for High-Risk AI Record-Keeping\" — defining industry-standard schemas for logs under Article 12, publishing a white paper, releasing a reference implementation of a standardized logging API, and positioning the lab as thought leader in AI governance.\n\nLet me assess this from my technical viability and prototyping speed/quality perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability Concerns:**\n- \"Define industry-standard schemas for what constitutes an appropriate log under Article 12 across different AI architectures\" — This is extraordinarily ambitious. Different AI architectures (transformers, classical ML, hybrid systems, RAG pipelines, agentic systems) have fundamentally different operational characteristics. What \"logs\" mean for a deterministic rule-based system vs. a probabilistic LLM-based system are almost incomparable. Creating a single standard schema that works \"across different AI architectures\" is technically infeasible as a unified thing — you'd need architecture-specific sub-schemas at minimum, and the boundaries between them would be extremely messy.\n- The Article 12 requirements themselves are somewhat abstract (\"events relevant for\" identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operations). Translating these into concrete log schemas is non-trivial engineering work that depends heavily on the specific system's architecture, deployment context, and intended purpose. There isn't a clean \"one schema fits all.\"\n- The reference implementation of a \"standardized logging API\" — what would this actually look like? A REST endpoint? An SDK? A sidecar container? The technical design space is enormous and the proposal doesn't constrain it at all.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping Concerns:**\n- This is a leadership initiative aimed at thought leadership, not at solving a concrete engineering problem we face. It's a standards-body activity (like IETF or ISO work) dressed up as an R&D deliverable.\n- Publishing a white paper and releasing a reference implementation \"for the global research community\" is a massive undertaking that would consume enormous engineering resources with no direct product outcome for our AI systems.\n- The scope is essentially unlimited: \"across different AI architectures\" could mean hundreds of architecture types. You can't prototype this in any reasonable timeframe to a quality standard.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective, this would pull engineers away from actually building and deploying logging capabilities into our own high-risk AI systems (which is what Article 12 actually requires us to do as a deployer/provider).\n- The \"thought leadership\" framing suggests the primary goal is reputational/strategic rather than technical. While that's valid for the organization, from my function's perspective, it's not a technically viable engineering project I can prototype and deliver with quality in a reasonable timeframe.\n\n**What would actually be technically viable?**\n- Building logging infrastructure into our specific high-risk AI systems to meet Article 12(1)-(3)\n- Designing log schemas for the specific architectures we deploy\n- Creating internal tooling that makes it easy for our developers to add compliant logging\n\nThe proposal as stated is too broad, too architecturally ambiguous, and oriented toward thought leadership rather than technical delivery. It's essentially a standards-body project that would consume engineering resources without producing shippable technical artifacts that solve concrete problems.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge: if scoped down to \"define our internal logging architecture for our specific high-risk systems,\" there's something useful here. But as written, it's an industry-wide standardization effort with unlimited scope and a primary goal of thought leadership rather than engineering delivery.\n\nMy assessment: This is not technically viable as stated from my vantage point. The scope is unbounded (\"across different AI architectures\"), the deliverable is ambiguous (a white paper + reference implementation for \"the global research community\"), and it doesn't produce concrete engineering artifacts I can prototype, test, and ship. It would slow down our actual compliance work rather than accelerate it.\n\nDecision: Reject\nPromise score: I'd give this maybe 15-20. There's a kernel of useful thinking (we do need to think about what logs should look like), but the framing as an industry standard with thought leadership goals is not something I can technically prototype or deliver at quality. The scope is too broad and the technical feasibility of a single cross-architecture standard is low.\n",
