t scale. This makes the prototyping timeline uncertain.\n- On the other hand, simpler versions (threshold-based alerts on drift metrics like KL divergence, PSI, population stability index) are very quickly prototypable and would cover a meaningful subset of the requirement.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A basic prototype using existing anomaly detection libraries (e.g., Isolation Forest, DBSCAN, or even simple z-score alerts on model output distributions) could be built in days to weeks. That's very fast.\n- The \"meta-monitor\" framing suggests a more complex, possibly custom ML system that would take months to develop and validate. This is where prototyping speed suffers.\n- The proposal doesn't specify what the inputs actually are in practice — Article 12 logs could be quite heterogeneous depending on the specific high-risk system (biometric vs. employment vs. critical infrastructure). A one-size-fits-all meta-monitor may not be feasible.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe core engineering idea (apply statistical monitoring to compliance logs) is technically viable and prototypable quickly in a basic form. However, the proposal as stated leans toward a research-oriented framing (\"research project,\" \"training a meta-monitor\") that suggests longer timelines and less clear deliverables. The technical viability of the *basic* version is high; the viability of the full \"meta-monitor\" concept is more uncertain because it depends on having well-defined signals for what constitutes \"substantial modification.\"\n\nI think this is a solid proposal that I'd lean toward approving, but with reservations about scope. From my engineering vantage point:\n- The basic SPC/anomaly detection layer is very prototypable (high technical viability, fast to build)\n- The full meta-monitor concept is more research-y and less clearly scoped\n- It does add value beyond what Article 12 strictly requires (which is just \"automatic recording of events\") — it's an enhancement layer\n\nI'll approve it because the core technical approach is sound and a useful prototype can be built quickly, but I'll give a moderate promise score reflecting that the full vision has some uncertainty in scope and that it goes beyond the minimum regulatory requirement.\n\nLet me settle on a promise score: The basic SPC monitoring of logs is very doable (maybe 75-80 for that piece). The \"meta-monitor\" research component adds uncertainty. Overall, I'd say around 62-68. It's technically sound in its core but the research framing and the ill-defined target (\"substantial modification\") create prototyping risk. I'll go with 65.\n",
