e framework is a strong signal to enterprise customers, partners, and regulators that the institution takes EU AI Act obligations seriously. It demonstrates maturity in governance. This supports market positioning.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:** The EU AI Act carries significant penalties. Article 12 non-compliance could lead to enforcement actions. A systematic approach to logging reduces tail risk of a compliance failure being discovered during market surveillance or an incident investigation under Article 79.\n\nOverall, this is strongly aligned with my function's priorities. It directly addresses the core technical requirement of Article 12(1), provides structural assurance across the organization, and creates an audit-ready state. The CI/CD mandate is particularly effective as a control mechanism. I would approve this with high confidence.\n\nPromise score: This is very strong for compliance. The main reservation is that it's framed at a program level and I'd want to see the specific telemetry mappings per system category, but the direction is exactly right. I'll give it around 90-92. It's not a perfect 100 because the proposal as stated doesn't explicitly address the differentiated requirements of Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) systems (biometrics verification persons, reference databases), and \"guarantees baseline compliance\" is aspirational language rather than a structural guarantee. But it's very close to what I'd want to see.\n\nI'll go with 91.\n",
