persons involved in verification. This requires domain-specific hooks beyond generic telemetry.\n  - \"Immutable database\" has operational implications: storage growth over system lifetime, retention policies aligned with regulatory requirements, queryability for audits vs. immutability tradeoffs.\n  - The proposal says it satisfies 12(1) and 12(2) but doesn't address 12(3) (the minimum logging for biometric systems). That's a gap.\n  - Cross-project standardization (\"consistently across different research projects\") is ambitious — different AI models have very different architectures (CNNs, transformers, ensemble methods, etc.). A single middleware API may need extensive adapters per model type.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This is a foundational infrastructure piece. Building it first makes sense because everything else depends on having logs.\n- However, the scope as described (\"integrated into all high-risk AI models\") is large. For prototyping speed, you'd want to get a minimal viable logging layer working for one model class quickly, then expand. The proposal as stated doesn't articulate a phased approach — it reads like a big-bang \"build the whole framework\" effort.\n- The quality of prototyping could be helped by this: if the logging infrastructure is in place early, you can iterate on models without worrying about compliance instrumentation later. That's a strong argument for prioritizing it.\n- Risk: if the middleware API design is wrong or too rigid, it becomes a bottleneck. Every new model architecture would need to conform to its interface. This could slow prototyping if the abstraction doesn't generalize well.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThe core technical concept is sound and well-grounded in existing engineering patterns. It addresses the right regulatory requirement (12(1) and 12(2)). The main concerns are:\n1. Scope ambiguity — \"intercepts system calls\" is vague for heterogeneous AI pipelines.\n2. Gap on 12