s** — This is problematic because Article 12(3) specifically requires recording specific identifiable information: start/end times of each use, the reference database used, input data that led to a match, and identification of natural persons involved in verification (Art. 14(5)). Differential privacy adds noise to data, which would likely make logs non-compliant with the specificity required by Article 12(3). You can't differentially privatize \"the identification of the natural persons involved\" — that's a specific requirement. So there's a fundamental tension: the techniques proposed might actually undermine compliance rather than enhance it.\n\n4. **Technical viability concern**: The proposal conflates two different things — (a) ensuring logs are tamper-proof/verifiable, and (b) protecting privacy of data within those logs. ZKPs could address (a) in theory but at enormous engineering cost. Differential privacy addresses (b) but conflicts with the specificity requirements of Art. 12(3). The proposal doesn't clearly articulate how these techniques would actually satisfy the specific logging mandates rather than undermine them.\n\n5. **Speed/quality of prototyping**: This is a long-horizon research project. ZKP implementations are complex, require specialized cryptographic expertise, and have limited production track record for this use case. Prototyping this on \"existing high-risk system architectures\" sounds like it would take many months or years before a working prototype exists, during which the lab needs to be compliant NOW (the AI Act has phased implementation timelines).\n\n6. **The proposal frames this as \"a novel contribution to the knowledge frontier of secure AI governance\"** — this is an academic positioning. My job is to ensure we have technically viable solutions that can be prototyped quickly and well. A \"knowledge frontier\" project is not aligned with committing scarce implementation resources toward meeting regulatory deadlines.\n\n**Decision**: Reject. From my vantage point, this proposal is too theoretical, the technical approaches proposed (ZKPs at logging scale, differential privacy on logs) have serious viability concerns that could actually undermine compliance with Art. 12(3)'s specificity requirements, and it doesn't offer a fast path to a working prototype. It's an interesting academic question but not something I would commit implementation resources to given my mandate of technical viability and prototyping speed/quality.\n\n**Promise score**: I'd give this maybe 15-20. The problem it identifies is real (privacy vs. traceability tension), and there might be elements worth exploring long-term, but as a proposal for committing implementation resources toward technically viable, quickly-prototypable solutions? It falls well short. I'll go with 18.\n",
