\" (real-time sync, bidirectional consistency checks) adds complexity\n- The risk assessment linking is the weakest point - risk assessments are often qualitative documents written by humans, not easily auto-generated from code artifacts\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The proposal conflates two things: (1) technical documentation of the system itself (architecture, algorithms, data provenance) and (2) compliance-specific content (risk management system documentation, specific regulatory language). The former is very automatable; the latter much less so.\n- \"Audit-ready without manual documentation\" is an overpromise. Auditors will want to see human judgment documented in risk assessments, not just auto-generated links.\n- That said, as a prototyping target, building a pipeline that auto-generates 70-80% of technical documentation from model registry metadata is very viable and high-value.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a dev engineering standpoint, this is technically feasible as a prototype within a reasonable timeframe. The core mechanism (Git sync + model registry metadata → structured docs) is well-trodden territory. The main risk is scope creep into \"fully automated compliance documentation\" which is harder than it sounds. But as stated, the proposal is about linking and syncing, not auto-generating legal/compliance narratives, so it's more reasonable.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar for prototyping viability. It's a well-scoped engineering problem with known solutions for most components. The promise score should reflect that it's solid but has some overpromising in the \"audit-ready without manual\" framing - I'd rate it around 72-78. Let me settle on 75.\n\nDecision: approve (the core technical work is viable and prototypic)\nPromise: 75 (solid engineering project, well-understood components, but the \"fully automated audit-readiness\" framing sets expectations higher than what auto-sync actually delivers)\n",
