ents are explicitly listed.\n- The proposal frames this as \"maintaining legal traceability\" but lossy compression and sampling fundamentally challenge whether traceability is truly maintained. This is a regulatory risk that could invalidate the entire research output.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- The framing as a \"peer-reviewed scientific contribution\" is appealing from an institutional prestige standpoint. It transforms compliance cost into publication potential.\n- However, I need to be honest: will lossy compression of audit logs actually publish? The novelty might be limited — data compression for log files is well-studied. The specific constraint here (regulatory minimums) makes it a constrained engineering problem rather than an open research question. The \"intelligent sampling\" angle is more interesting but also riskier from compliance standpoint.\n- Funding constraints: A \"small team\" on a \"new track\" — the overhead of a new research track (project setup, reporting, governance) might exceed the value for a small team. Would it be more efficient to assign 1-2 people within existing teams to investigate this as a side project?\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal creates a NEW track rather than leveraging existing infrastructure. If we already have ML systems, data engineering teams, or compliance-focused engineers, could this work be embedded in their existing tasks with minimal additional overhead?\n- The \"new research track\" language suggests organizational creation — new project codes, possibly new budget lines, new review cycles. That's overhead I'd prefer to avoid for a \"small team.\"\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe core insight (logging has performance costs; reducing them is valuable) is sound and practically important. However:\n1. The regulatory risk of lossy compression/sampling undermining Article 12 compliance is significant and the proposal hand-waves this away with