ing regulatory audits or market surveillance authority requests.\n6. Input hashes support traceability of what data went into the system - relevant to Art 12(3)(c) for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification, etc.) though this is a general framework not specific to that category.\n\n**Concerns from compliance perspective:**\n1. The proposal mentions \"input hashes\" but Article 12(3) requires recording of actual \"input data for which the search has led to a match\" - hashes alone may not satisfy this requirement for Annex III point 1(a) systems. However, this is a general framework and the specific requirements would be implemented per system.\n2. The proposal says \"across various AI architectures\" but doesn't explicitly address the full lifetime aspect - logs must persist over the entire lifetime of the system, not just during development/testing.\n3. It's a general infrastructure proposal - it provides the mechanism but may need additional work to ensure specific event types required by Art 12(2)(a), (b), (c) are actually captured for each use case.\n4. The \"single configuration flag\" is great for adoption, but compliance needs to verify that when enabled, ALL required events are captured - not just timestamps and user IDs. The proposal mentions \"event timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" which is a subset of what may be needed.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is strongly aligned with my mandate. It directly implements the core technical requirement of Article 12(1) - automatic recording of events. It standardizes traceability (Recital 71), reduces human error in enabling logging, and creates a consistent audit trail. For institutional survival, having a standardized, well-documented logging framework is essential for demonstrating compliance to market surveillance authorities. For market reputation, it shows the institution takes regulatory obligations seriously at an architectural level.\n\nThe main gap is that it's somewhat high-level - \"event timestamps, input hashes, and user IDs\" may not be sufficient on its own, but as a foundational framework with modular extensions, it's the right starting point. The compliance value here is very high because without this infrastructure, every individual project would need to build its own logging, leading to inconsistencies, gaps, and ultimately non-compliance.\n\nI'll approve this. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. It's strongly aligned but the specific event types mentioned are a subset of what's needed, and it doesn't explicitly address lifetime persistence or the specific Annex III requirements. But as a foundational framework, it's excellent compliance infrastructure.\n\nLet me settle on 83 - strong alignment with Article 12 core requirements, reduces compliance risk significantly, but needs supplementation to fully cover all event types required by Art 12(2) and (3).\n",
