ticle 12 text has multiple sub-requirements across paragraphs 1, 2, and 3.\n- The scope mentions \"Annex III point 1(a)\" systems specifically for the minimum fields in paragraph 3, but the proposal doesn't clearly delineate which systems get which level of testing.\n- It's a prototype-stage tool - useful early on but needs to be maintained as requirements evolve and as systems progress from prototype to production.\n- However, the core idea is sound: having an automated way to verify compliance with logging requirements before deployment is exactly what a compliance function would want.\n\n**From my vantage point (institutional survival, regulatory compliance, market reputation):**\nThis is highly relevant. If we deploy a high-risk AI system without proper Article 12 logging capabilities, we face:\n- Fines under the EU AI Act (up to €35M or 7% of global turnover for prohibited practices, up to €15M or 3% for other violations)\n- Reputational damage if regulators find non-compliant systems in the market\n- Inability to demonstrate traceability during post-market monitoring\n- Potential inability to respond to serious incident reporting (Article 73) without proper logs\n\nAn automated test suite that validates logging compliance is a practical, low-cost way to ensure we catch gaps early. It's not a replacement for full technical documentation or lifecycle management, but it's a valuable component of the compliance assurance process.\n\nThe proposal is well-scoped and directly addresses a regulatory requirement I'm responsible for ensuring is met. The \"mock audits\" concept is particularly relevant because it simulates what a notified body or market surveillance authority would check.\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a focused, practical tool that directly supports compliance with Article 12. The promise score should reflect that while it's valuable, it's one component of a larger compliance program and the proposal as stated is somewhat simplified in its description of what needs to be tested. But from my vantage point, having automated verification of logging requirements before deployment is high-value.\n\nI'll approve this with a strong promise score - maybe 78-82. It's directly on-target for my function but it's a supporting tool rather than a comprehensive solution, and the description slightly under-specifies the full scope of Article 12(3) requirements.\n",
