ents required by Article 12\" but doesn't explicitly address all three sub-categories in 12(2) (a), (b), (c). It mentions inputs, outputs, and timestamps, which maps somewhat to 12(3)(a), but not clearly to (b) reference databases or (d) identification of natural persons.\n- \"Stream them directly to a central repository\" — data protection implications? Where is this repository located? GDPR compliance for logging personal data (especially if biometric systems are involved)? This could be a compliance issue in itself.\n- \"Keeping prototyping fast for researchers\" — from my vantage point, I need to ensure the SDK doesn't become a \"nice-to-have\" that gets bypassed during rapid prototyping. There needs to be enforcement mechanism.\n\n**Assessment from Compliance perspective:**\nThis is fundamentally aligned with what Article 12 requires. A standardized, institutional-level logging solution is exactly what a compliance officer would want to see — it ensures consistency, reduces the risk of projects forgetting or incorrectly implementing logging requirements, and creates an audit trail that demonstrates organizational commitment to compliance. The \"over the lifetime\" requirement (12(1)) is better served by a persistent, maintained SDK than by ad-hoc per-project solutions.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat generic and doesn't explicitly address all the specific data points in 12(3), nor does it address retention periods, access controls, or the interaction with post-market monitoring (12(2)(b)). But these are implementation details that could be addressed during development. The core concept — a unified SDK to ensure Article 12 compliance across the organization — is strongly aligned with my mandate.\n\nFrom an institutional survival and market reputation standpoint: having a demonstrable, standardized approach to logging shows regulators and customers that the institution takes compliance seriously. If audited by the AI Office or national competent authorities, being able to say \"we have an institutional SDK that ensures all high-risk systems log per Article 12\" is far more defensible than \"each project team was responsible for their own logging.\"\n\nI think this clears my bar. It's a core compliance enabler. I'll approve it but note the gaps in my rationale. My promise score should reflect that while the direction is right and important, there are specific regulatory details (12(3) specifics, data retention, GDPR interplay for personal data in logs) that need to be nailed down. I'd say around 78-82 — strongly supportive of the concept but with reservations about completeness of the proposal as stated.\n\nLet me settle on 80. The core idea is exactly what compliance needs, but the proposal as written is a skeleton that doesn't yet demonstrate full coverage of all Article 12 sub-requirements, particularly 12(3)(b) and (d), nor does it address log retention, integrity/immutability, or data protection concerns for the central repository.\n",
