uce something we can actually use.\n4. \"Enhances our institutional influence\" is a strategic/institutional goal, not a technical one. From my vantage point, this doesn't accelerate our prototyping speed or improve the quality of our deliverables in any direct way.\n5. There's no clear timeline for when this standard would be published and available for use. In the meantime, we still need to implement Article 12 compliance in our own systems NOW (or within whatever deadline applies). We can't wait for a multi-institutional standard before building our logging infrastructure.\n6. The scope is vague: \"common data schemas for AI logs that satisfy Article 12 across various research institutions\" — this could become an unbounded project. Article 12 covers different categories (general requirements in paras 1-3, specific minimums for biometric systems in para 4). Trying to cover all of these in one schema is a large undertaking.\n7. However, there IS some technical merit: if we end up with a well-defined schema, it could reduce prototyping time for future logging implementations by giving us a reference structure. But this is speculative and far-off.\n\n**Key concern:** This proposal essentially diverts engineering resources away from building actual compliant systems toward an advocacy/standardization effort. From my vantage point on technical viability and prototyping speed/quality, I would be very cautious about committing scarce implementation resources to leading a multi-institutional working group when we still need to build our own logging infrastructure.\n\nThe proposal doesn't describe any concrete engineering deliverable that accelerates our own compliance work. It's fundamentally a standards-body activity dressed up as an R&D project. While it might have long-term value, the short-to-medium term cost in engineering time and opportunity cost is significant, with no guarantee of producing something we can actually prototype against in a useful timeframe.\n\nMy