lf a significant engineering investment. It requires dedicated engineering resources, ongoing maintenance, and potentially slows initial deployment if the framework isn't ready when a project needs it.\n- The scope is broad — \"across different AI architectures\" means this is ambitious. Getting it right for all architectures could take substantial time before it delivers value.\n- However, the counterargument: without this shared infrastructure, every new high-risk system will need bespoke logging implementations, which is far more expensive over time and harder to audit consistently.\n- The \"intercepts model calls\" language suggests a somewhat technical approach that may or may not fit all architectures seamlessly — some research systems might not be structured around discrete \"model calls.\"\n- Centralized immutable database: good for compliance (tamper-evidence), but adds an infrastructure dependency and potential bottleneck.\n\nOverall assessment from my vantage point: This is a high-leverage investment. The key value is amortization — building the logging layer once rather than N times across projects. It directly serves resource-allocation efficiency by preventing redundant compliance work in each research team. It preserves scientific velocity by making compliance \"invisible\" to researchers through the middleware abstraction. The main risk is over-engineering or scope creep on the initial framework, but even a minimal viable version (timestamps + start/end of use + basic event capture) would serve most Article 12(1)-(2) requirements and could be extended for Annex III point 1(a) specific logging later.\n\nThe balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints is well-served here: the lab doesn't need to divert researchers from their core work to handle compliance plumbing. The effective use of existing R&D infrastructure is enhanced because the middleware sits alongside rather than replacing research code.\n\nI'd rate this fairly high — maybe 7