ften find unexpected performance characteristics only in real implementation.\n- It's an evaluation, not a solution. From my vantage point (technical viability of solutions), this doesn't produce a viable technical solution — it produces numbers that inform decisions about hardware procurement or data retention policies. Those are important but they're operations/infrastructure concerns more than development engineering concerns.\n- That said, if the audit reveals your current infra can't handle lifelong logging, that's a critical finding that affects how you architect the system. So it has some value as a constraint-setter for prototyping.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. It's not harmful, and understanding capacity constraints before building is sound practice. But from my specific vantage point of technical viability and prototyping speed/quality:\n- It doesn't advance any specific technical solution toward implementation.\n- The \"simulation\" approach may be less effective than just prototyping and measuring.\n- It's more an infrastructure planning exercise than a development engineering activity.\n- However, it does set constraints that affect architectural decisions for the logging system.\n\nI'll lean toward reject or low-promise approve. Let me think...\n\nActually, I think I should reject this. My vantage point is technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping. This proposal:\n1. Is an evaluation, not a solution — it doesn't make any technical approach more viable.\n2. Adds a gate before prototyping can begin (or at least before architecture decisions are locked in).\n3. The \"simulation\" methodology is less reliable than just building and measuring.\n4. It addresses infrastructure capacity, which is important but not the core engineering challenge of Article 12 compliance (the hard part is designing what to log, how to capture risk-relevant events, ensuring traceability — not whether your disks are big enough).\n\nI'll reject it w