        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2459\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core insight—building a shared logging layer once rather than letting each project team reinvent it—is sound and reduces long-term engineering duplication, which matters under our budget constraints. However, the proposal's scope ('all R&D pipelines,' 'mandatory internal API across all production environments') is substantially over-broad relative to what Article 12 actually requires (logging only for *high-risk* systems), meaning we would sink scarce engineering time into retrofitting exploratory and non-high-risk workstreams that have no regulatory obligation, while the mandate creates friction that slows scientific iteration. Additionally, the proposal builds from scratch without addressing whether our existing run-tracking and telemetry infrastructure can be extended rather than replaced—a critical resource question I cannot sign off on without a cost-benefit analysis against what we already have in place.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 42\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "The user asks me to evaluate proposal P2459 from the vantage point of a Lab Director at an R&D institution developing AI systems subject to the EU AI Act. My domain of concern is: resource-allocation efficiency, the balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints, and effective use of existing R&D infrastructure.\n\nLet me analyze this proposal carefully:\n\n**Proposal P2459: \"Unified Compliance Logging Framework\"**\n- A standardized software library integrated into all R&D pipelines to automate event recording for high-risk systems\n- Ensures logs are generated consistently regardless of model architecture\n- Engineering team will develop a mandatory internal API\n- Automatically captures timestamps, input data, and user IDs across all production environments\n- Eliminates human error in record-keeping\n- Meets strict technical mandates (Article 12)\n\nNow let me assess from my vantage point:\n\n**Resource-allocation efficiency:**\n- Building a \"unified\" framework across ALL R&D pipelines is a massive undertaking. The lab likely has diverse model architectures, experimental stages, and different levels of formality. Forcing everything through one mandatory API is resource-intensive.\n- However, if you're going to build logging anyway (it's legally required), building it once as a shared library rather than having each team build their own could be more efficient in aggregate. The \"build once, reuse everywhere\" logic has merit.\n- BUT: the proposal says \"all R&D pipelines\" and \"all production environments.\" This is a very broad scope. Not all R&D work produces high-risk AI systems. Many experiments are exploratory, not yet at the stage where they're deployed as high-risk systems. Building mandatory logging into ALL pipelines from day one is premature and wastes engineering resources on projects that may never become high-risk systems or may pivot entirely.\n- The proposal conflates \"high-risk AI system\" (which triggers Article 12) with everything in the lab's pipeline. This is a scope error that would burn resources unnecessarily.\n\n**Balance between scientific ambition and funding constraints:**\n- A mandatory internal API that engineers must integrate into everything creates friction for researchers. It slows down experimentation. Every new experiment now requires compliance plumbing before it can run.\n- The framing of \"institutional survival\" is correct — yes, we need to comply. But the solution proposed is heavyweight. A lighter-touch approach (logging layer activated only when a system crosses into high-risk classification) would preserve scientific agility.\n- Engineering time spent building and maintaining this framework is time NOT spent on research. The proposal doesn't account for ongoing maintenance costs of keeping the library current as architectures evolve.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- The proposal says \"integrated into all R&D pipelines\" which implies retrofitting existing systems. This is disruptive to ongoing work.\n- However, if the lab already has some telemetry or logging infrastructure (most modern ML labs have basic run-tracking via tools like MLflow, W&B, etc.), a truly unified framework might duplicate or conflict with what's already in place rather than leveraging it.\n- The proposal doesn't mention whether existing infrastructure can be extended vs. building from scratch. This is a significant gap in resource planning.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThe direction is right (automated logging is required by Article 12, and consistency across systems reduces compliance risk), but the execution as proposed is over-scoped and potentially wasteful:\n1. \"All R&D pipelines\" is too broad — should be scoped to systems classified or expected to become high-risk.\n2. \"Mandatory internal API\" creates friction that hampers scientific agility.\n3. Doesn't leverage existing infrastructure (run trackers, telemetry).\n4. Engineering cost of building AND maintaining this across heterogeneous architectures is substantial and ongoing.\n5. The \"eliminate human error\" framing is reasonable but the solution is heavier than necessary for many use cases.\n\nFrom my vantage point, I'd want to approve a NARROWER version: build a logging library that's available and recommended (not mandatory) for systems approaching high-risk classification, leveraging existing infrastructure where possible. The proposal as written is too broad in scope and too rigid in implementation, which would drain engineering resources from research and create unnecessary overhead for non-high-risk work.\n\nHowever, there IS value in having a consistent logging approach. If we have 50+ projects that might become high-risk, building it once does save costs vs. each team building independently. The question is whether \"mandatory across ALL pipelines\" is the right mechanism.\n\nI'll reject this as proposed because:\n- Scope is too broad (all R&D pipelines vs. only high-risk systems)\n- Mandatory integration creates unnecessary friction and resource drain for non-applicable work\n- Doesn't account for leveraging existing infrastructure\n- The engineering cost-to-benefit ratio is poor when applied to the full scope described\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 45-50. The underlying idea has merit but as scoped it's wasteful of resources. From a pure resource-allocation standpoint, this would tie up engineers in building compliance plumbing for workstreams that don't need it yet. But I'm not giving it a very low score because the core insight (build once, standardized logging) is correct — just the scope and mandatoriness are wrong.\n\nLet me settle on 42. It's directionally right but the implementation as described would be a poor use of scarce engineering resources given the over-broad scope and failure to leverage existing infrastructure.\n",
