 and real-world performance evolution would be highly valuable to the broader ML community, which has historically relied on static benchmarks. This could shift research paradigms toward longitudinal evaluation. The data itself (anonymized operational logs from regulated systems) is a unique resource that no other group can access at this scale.\n4. **Symbiosis with regulation**: Using mandated logs as a research substrate is clever — it's not an additional data collection burden but repurposing what the system must already record. This makes the research program sustainable without requiring separate infrastructure.\n\n**Concerns:**\n1. The proposal says \"frontier AI systems\" at the end, which slightly misaligns with Article 12 (which applies to high-risk AI systems, not necessarily frontier models). But this could be a minor terminological slip or an aspirational framing.\n2. Privacy and data governance concerns: These logs will contain sensitive operational data. The proposal doesn't address anonymization or ethical review of using production system data for publication. However, from a pure research merit standpoint, this is an implementation detail rather than a fundamental flaw in the research question.\n3. \"Advanced statistical anomaly detection\" is somewhat generic — it doesn't specify what makes this methodologically novel versus applying standard change-point detection to time series. But the framing of \"latent failure modes\" suggests going beyond simple drift detection.\n4. The proposal is relatively high-level as a program description, which is appropriate for an ideation exercise.\n\n**Assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is genuinely at the knowledge frontier. The longitudinal behavior of deployed AI systems — especially under regulatory constraints that mandate logging — is a largely unexplored empirical territory. The intellectual merit is strong: identifying how and why validated systems diverge from their benchmark performance in real-world operation is one of the most important open questions in applied ML. The broader scientific impact potential is high because it could establish new evaluation methodologies for AI reliability that go beyond static testing.\n\nThe fact that regulatory mandates create a structured, longitudinal data source that researchers can leverage (rather than having to build their own expensive telemetry infrastructure) makes this both scientifically interesting and practically feasible.\n\nI would approve this from my vantage point. It addresses a genuine gap in the literature, uses a unique data source, and has high potential for peer-reviewed impact. The promise score should be high — I'd say around 82-88. Let me settle on 85: it's strong research but somewhat methodologically underspecified (\"advanced statistical anomaly detection\" is vague), and the connection to \"frontier AI systems\" at the end is slightly off-target relative to Article 12's scope of high-risk (not necessarily frontier) systems. But the core research question — longitudinal behavioral analysis of deployed AI using mandated logs — is excellent.\n\nI'll go with approve, promise 85.\n",
