ices for prototyping speed.\n\n**Decision rationale:** From my vantage point, this is technically viable and addresses a real infrastructure gap needed for Article 12 compliance. However, the framing as a \"specialized storage architecture\" suggests custom development where off-the-shelf solutions would be faster to prototype. The core ideas (time-series ingestion, compression, tiering) are sound but not novel enough to warrant dedicated scarce resources when existing tools can deliver 80% of the value at much lower prototyping cost. That said, if our AI systems generate truly unusual volumes or patterns that off-the-shelf TSDBs can't handle, custom work might be justified — but the proposal doesn't make that case clearly.\n\nI think this is a moderate-approval situation. It's technically sound and addresses a real need, but from a prototyping-speed perspective, I'd want to see justification for why off-the-shelf isn't sufficient before committing scarce implementation resources. The idea is viable but potentially over-engineered relative to what we actually need in the near term.\n\nLet me settle on: approve with moderate promise score. It's technically viable, addresses a real compliance infrastructure gap, and the engineering patterns described are sound. But I have reservations about the \"specialized\" framing vs. adopting proven tools for faster prototyping. I'll give it around 62-68 range.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. My job is to assess technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping. Is this technically viable? Yes, absolutely — time-series DBs with tiering are well-understood. Can we prototype this quickly? If we use existing tools, yes. The proposal as written implies custom development (\"the team will implement a time-series database\") which slows prototyping down unnecessarily. But the underlying concept is sound and necessary.\n\nI'll approve it at around 65. It's a solid infrastructure piece that's technically very viable, but I'd push back