        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2249\",\n      \"rationale\": \"While leading an industry standardization effort has reputational appeal, this proposal does not directly implement Article 12 logging obligations for the lab's own high-risk systems and instead commits scarce resources to a multi-year open-source project whose regulatory adoption is uncertain; furthermore, the Act's deliberate flexibility—'appropriate to the intended purpose' (Art. 12(2))—means a single generic schema risks being insufficient or even counterproductive if a supervisor finds our system-specific logging inadequate because it followed a template rather than addressing the particular risk profile of each deployed system.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 32\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Compliance Officer whose domain is institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal P2249 suggests:\n1. Developing an open-source schema for logging/record-keeping tailored to high-risk AI systems\n2. Publishing a white paper with technical specifications\n3. Leading a working group with other R&D institutions to standardize formats\n4. Releasing an open-source implementation\n\nFrom a compliance officer's perspective, let me think about what this does and doesn't do:\n\n**What it gets right:**\n- Article 12 requires automatic logging over the system lifetime. Having a well-defined schema for what needs to be logged is genuinely useful.\n- Recital 71 emphasizes \"comprehensible information\" and traceability — a standardized format could help ensure consistency.\n- Leading a working group positions the lab to influence how \"appropriate to the intended purpose\" (Art. 12(2)) gets interpreted, which is where significant regulatory ambiguity exists.\n- An open-source implementation reduces risk of misinterpretation if it becomes an industry standard.\n\n**What concerns me from a compliance/institutional survival standpoint:**\n- This is a multi-year, resource-intensive project that does NOT directly implement Article 12 for the lab's own systems. The regulation requires OUR high-risk AI systems to have logging capabilities NOW (or within the applicable transition period). Developing an \"open standard\" doesn't satisfy our obligation to have compliant systems operational.\n- The proposal is framed around \"shaping regulatory interpretation\" and \"maintaining scientific leadership\" — this is a strategic/branding goal, not a compliance implementation goal. As Compliance Officer, my job is to ensure the lab's systems comply. This proposal diverts scarce resources away from actual implementation toward thought-leadership activities.\n- There's no guarantee that any open standard we develop will be adopted by regulators or industry. We could spend significant time and resources on this while our own Article 12 compliance remains incomplete.\n- \"Appropriate to the intended purpose\" (Art. 12(2)) is deliberately flexible — it varies by system type, sector, risk level. A single \"standard schema\" may not capture the necessary variability. Over-standardizing could actually create compliance risk if a regulator says our logging doesn't sufficiently address system-specific risks because we followed a generic template.\n- The working group with other R&D institutions introduces coordination overhead and potential conflicts of interest (competitive institutions sharing detailed technical approaches).\n- Publishing specifications publicly before regulatory guidance is finalized could lock the lab into interpretations that don't survive contact with final implementing acts or ECHA/NCA guidance.\n\n**On institutional survival:**\n- If our high-risk systems go live without adequate logging because we're busy building an \"open standard,\" that's a direct compliance failure. The cost of non-compliance (fines up to 7% of global turnover under Art. 99) dwarfs any benefit from thought leadership.\n- However, if this is positioned as a parallel track that doesn't delay actual implementation, it could add marginal value in terms of market reputation and regulatory relationships.\n\n**On market reputation:**\n- Leading a standardization effort does build reputation with regulators and industry peers. This has real value for institutional survival — being seen as a constructive participant rather than a laggard.\n- But the \"scientific leadership\" framing suggests the lab may be more interested in prestige than in actually closing compliance gaps.\n\n**My decision:**\nThis is not a bad idea in absolute terms, but from my vantage point (compliance implementation and institutional survival), it's a secondary activity that risks competing with primary obligations. It doesn't directly implement Article 12 for our systems. The resources required are significant. The regulatory payoff is uncertain (we don't know if this standard will be adopted). And there's a risk of over-reliance on a \"standard\" when the regulation deliberately requires case-by-case appropriateness.\n\nHowever, it does have some compliance value:\n- Defining what to log helps ensure we're not missing elements\n- Engaging with regulators through standardization builds goodwill\n- An open-source implementation could serve as our internal tool if adopted\n\nBut the core issue is: this is a strategy proposal, not an implementation plan. It doesn't tell me how OUR systems will comply by the deadline. As Compliance Officer, I need to see concrete implementation of Art. 12 logging for each high-risk system we operate. This proposal is at best complementary and at worst a resource distraction.\n\nI'll reject it because:\n1. It doesn't directly implement our compliance obligations\n2. The \"appropriate to intended purpose\" language means a one-size-fits-all standard may be insufficient or even counterproductive\n3. Resource commitment to an open-source project with uncertain adoption is high-risk for institutional survival\n4. The primary framing (shaping interpretation, scientific leadership) signals this is a branding/strategy play rather than a compliance play\n\nPromise score: It has some value in terms of regulatory engagement and long-term positioning, but from my narrow vantage point of ensuring we comply by the deadline with our scarce resources, it's maybe a 30-35. It's not worthless (it could inform our internal schema), but it's not something I'd back without hesitation. I'll say 32.\n",
