ed in years, not months.\n- There's no clear prototype here. What would we build first? A subset schema for one type of high-risk system? Even that is ambiguous.\n\n**Relevance to my domain (technical viability + speed/quality of prototyping):**\n- The proposal is framed around \"shaping how AI is governed worldwide\" and \"broader scientific impact.\" This is a mission/vision statement, not an engineering deliverable. It doesn't tell me what system we're building, what the API looks like, what the first prototype demonstrates, or how it accelerates our prototyping cycles.\n- For my team, the practical need is: can our AI systems technically allow automatic recording of events (logs)? Do we have a clear data model for those logs? A JSON-LD vocabulary for documentation is upstream and abstract from that concrete engineering problem.\n- The collaboration aspect with \"other EU research institutions\" adds coordination cost without adding technical depth to what we actually need to build.\n\n**Potential upside:**\n- If such a standard existed, it could make compliance tooling easier — you could write validators, cross-reference documents, etc. But that's speculative and far-future.\n- A well-designed schema for logging data (as opposed to documentation) would be more directly useful to my team.\n\n**Verdict:**\nFrom my vantage point, this is a standards/governance project with very long time-to-value, unclear technical scope, and no direct path to improving our prototyping speed or system viability. It's well-intentioned but misaligned with what I can concretely evaluate as technically viable and rapid to prototype. The \"global transparency standard\" framing makes it even more diffuse — there's no single clear deliverable I can assess for technical risk or implementation feasibility.\n\nI'll reject this. My promise score should reflect that while the idea isn't crazy (machine-readable documentation is a reasonable direction), from my specific vantage point of enginee