vague — without specific thresholds, this could be interpreted as negligible allocation. As Compliance Officer, I'd want defined minimums tied to the risk level of each system.\n- It says \"initial architectural phase\" — but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires very specific logging (periods of use, reference databases, input data for matches, identification of verifying persons). The proposal doesn't specify that different risk categories need different levels of traceability engineering.\n- \"Update all project charters\" — this is operationally sound but the proposal doesn't address what happens to projects already in flight. Compliance gap: existing high-risk systems being developed under old charters wouldn't have this budget line.\n- It's a governance/funding framework, not an implementation plan. It commits resources but doesn't specify what \"traceability engineering\" actually entails technically. However, as a screening decision, the proposal is about the allocation mechanism itself, not the technical detail.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is fundamentally sound from a compliance perspective. The EU AI Act creates hard obligations (Article 12 uses \"shall\"), and if our systems don't have logging capability built in, we simply cannot deploy them as high-risk systems in the EU market. This proposal institutionalizes the commitment to meeting that obligation at the resource level. It's not a technical solution, but it's a necessary governance prerequisite. Without it, compliance becomes an afterthought competing for scraps of budget against research priorities.\n\nThe vague \"small percentage\" and lack of tiering by risk category are concerns, but the core principle — mandatory, upfront, charter-level commitment to traceability engineering — is exactly what a compliance function needs to protect institutional survival. The cost of not having this (failed conformity assessment, market access denial, regulatory penalties under Article 99