\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a technical viability standpoint: the proposal conflates privacy engineering with regulatory compliance in a way that may actually create non-compliance risk (if anonymized logs can't satisfy the \"comprehensible information\" requirement of Recital 71). The specific techniques proposed (DP, SMPC) are poorly matched to the actual requirements of Article 12 logging.\n\nFrom a prototyping speed/quality standpoint: this is an open-ended research project with no clear prototype deliverable, no timeline, and high technical risk. It would consume engineering resources on fundamental research questions while leaving the basic compliant logging architecture unbuilt.\n\nI'm going to reject this. The promise score should reflect that while privacy in logs is a legitimate concern, this particular framing (DP + SMPC for log recording) has low technical viability for near-term prototyping and may conflict with the regulatory text's own requirements. I'd put it around 15-20 — there's a kernel of valid concern (privacy in logged data), but the proposed approach is misaligned with both the regulation and our prototyping capabilities/timeline.\n\nLet me settle on 18. There IS a real privacy consideration in what gets logged, but this proposal's specific technical path is not viable for near-term prototyping, may create compliance conflicts, and doesn't address the actual engineering challenge (build a compliant logging system with appropriate data protection).\n",
