d more time on actual research. The one-time engineering investment (scripts) pays back over time through reduced ongoing manual effort. This is a good balance — it doesn't require massive capital expenditure, just some engineer-hours to write the scripts.\n\nCons/Concerns:\n1. **Implementation cost**: Engineering team time to build and maintain these scripts is a non-trivial cost, especially if the lab has limited engineering bandwidth. However, this is bounded compared to ongoing manual documentation costs.\n2. **Scope creep risk**: \"Regulatory-compliant template\" could become complex as requirements evolve. But this is manageable.\n3. **Dependency on existing tooling quality**: If MLflow or version control metadata isn't well-maintained by researchers (which it often isn't in practice), the pipeline will produce garbage documentation. This requires some cultural change, but it's a softer cost than building new infrastructure.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a strong proposal from my vantage point. It directly addresses resource-allocation efficiency by converting recurring manual labor into a one-time engineering investment plus low maintenance. It leverages existing infrastructure (MLflow, git) rather than requiring new purchases. It frees up researcher time for scientific work. The cost is bounded and the return is continuous over the system lifetime. This is exactly the kind of \"force multiplier\" that makes regulatory compliance sustainable within a research lab's budget.\n\nI would approve this. Promise score: I'd say around 82-85. It's very well-aligned with my concerns, but there's some execution risk (researcher data hygiene, ongoing maintenance) and it's not a silver bullet — it handles the \"up to date documentation\" aspect but doesn't fully solve all of Article 12's logging requirements (like automatic event recording in the system itself). Still, from resource efficiency perspective, it's excellent.\n\nLet me settle on 83.\n",
