ally allow automatic recording. It's not asking us to build an AI that interprets those logs.\n- Article 12(2) says logging capabilities shall \"enable\" recording of events relevant for risk identification, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. This is about the *capability to log*, not about building a separate AI system to analyze the logs afterward. The proposal seems to misread or over-scope the requirement.\n- From a prototyping standpoint, what you actually need to build for Article 12 compliance is: structured logging infrastructure with appropriate fields (timestamps per 12(3)(a), reference database identifiers per 12(3)(b), input data per 12(3)(c), verifier identification per 12(3)(d)). This is well-understood engineering. Wrapping this in a \"research initiative on AI safety\" adds complexity and timeline risk without addressing the actual requirement directly.\n- The proposal says it will make us \"publication-worthy\" — from my vantage point, that's not relevant to whether we can prototype something viable and fast.\n\n**What would be more technically sound:**\n- Build structured logging infrastructure that captures all required fields per Article 12(3).\n- Ensure logs are queryable and exportable for downstream analysis (which could include the anomaly detection described, but as a separate concern).\n- The \"risk mapping\" AI is a nice-to-have at best, and it's not what the regulation actually requires.\n\n**My assessment:** This proposal misidentifies the engineering task. Article 12 requires building logging *capability*, not an AI system that analyzes logs for risk. As stated, this is a research exploration with no clear prototype deliverable, no technical specification, and significant timeline uncertainty. From my vantage point (technical viability and speed/quality of prototyping), this doesn't clear the bar. It's not a viable engineering proposal — it's a research interest dressed as compliance response. The actual compliance work (