t in early rather than bolting on later. This actually accelerates prototyping of new high-risk systems since logging infrastructure is already there.\n- The proposal mentions protecting market reputation as a motivation, which is somewhat orthogonal to the technical implementation, but doesn't detract from the technical soundness.\n\n**Technical viability:** High. Structured logging libraries with standardized schemas are extremely well-trodden territory in software engineering. The main technical risk is scope creep on \"universal\" - but that's manageable with a layered approach (core + extensions).\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping impact:** Positive. If every new high-risk project starts with the logging framework already integrated, engineers don't have to design and implement it from scratch each time. This accelerates prototyping. The risk is if the framework is too rigid or heavy-handed, it could slow down rapid iteration. But a well-designed SDK (thin wrapper, async, non-blocking) shouldn't impede prototyping speed.\n\n**Does it actually meet Article 12?** It addresses 12(1) directly (automatic recording of events). It partially addresses 12(2)(a)-(c) through the \"system states\" and event capture. For 12(3), the specific Annex III 1(a) requirements (biometric matching, reference databases, etc.) would need project-specific extensions, but the base framework provides the infrastructure for that.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a technically viable, well-scoped engineering proposal that directly serves Article 12 compliance while also improving prototyping speed by making logging a default capability. The \"universal\" framing has some risk of over-generalization, but the core idea (shared library + schema) is standard good practice. I'd approve this with moderate-to-high confidence. It's not a novel research bet - it's solid engineering infrastructure that clearly works and clearly helps.\n\nPromise score: I'd say around 72-78. It's technically sound, well