        "reasoning_content": "The researcher's domain of concern is: the knowledge frontier, intellectual merit of research, and broader scientific impact.\n\nLet me evaluate proposal P2427 from this vantage point.\n\n**Proposal P2427: Privacy-Preserving Traceability Research**\n\nThis proposes exploring cryptographic methods (zero-knowledge proofs) to log system events without storing sensitive raw input data, addressing the tension between Article 12's logging requirements and data privacy laws (GDPR). The team would develop protocols proving a match occurred without recording actual identity/content, and publish findings in peer-reviewed venues.\n\n**From the knowledge frontier / intellectual merit / broader scientific impact perspective:**\n\nIntellectual Merit:\n- This is a genuinely interesting intersection of cryptography, regulatory compliance, and data privacy. Zero-knowledge proofs applied to regulatory logging is a non-trivial problem space. The core question — can you satisfy \"automatic recording of events\" while not storing the raw sensitive data? — is intellectually rich.\n- However, there's a significant tension here: Article 12(3) specifically requires logging \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" and \"the identification of the natural persons involved in the verification.\" A ZK proof that says \"a match occurred\" without recording WHAT was matched or WHO verified it may not actually satisfy the regulatory text. The regulation demands traceability, and ZK proofs that hide the content arguably defeat the purpose of traceability as defined in Recital 71 (\"Having comprehensible information on how high-risk AI systems have been developed and how they perform\").\n- That said, there's a legitimate research question here: where is the boundary between \"traceable\" and \"privacy-preserving\"? Can you design logging architectures that satisfy Article 12 while minimizing data exposure? This is a real open problem at the intersection of formal methods, cryptography, and regulatory compliance.\n\nKnowledge Frontier:\n- ZK proofs applied to AI system auditing is an emerging area. The specific application to EU AI Act compliance logging is novel. There's limited prior work on exactly this intersection.\n- However, ZK proofs are well-studied in their own right; the novelty here is primarily in the application domain and the regulatory framing. The \"frontier\" push is moderate — it's an applied extension of existing cryptographic techniques to a new problem context rather than a fundamental breakthrough.\n\nBroader Scientific Impact:\n- If successful, findings could influence how regulators think about logging requirements globally (not just EU). Other jurisdictions adopting AI regulation frameworks would face the same tension.\n- Publication in peer-reviewed venues is appropriate and expected.\n- The impact is potentially significant for privacy engineering as a field, but it's somewhat niche — it sits at the intersection of law, cryptography, and systems engineering rather than pushing any single scientific discipline forward dramatically.\n\nConcerns from my vantage:\n- The proposal is somewhat vague on what specific cryptographic constructions would be explored. ZK proofs come in many flavors (zk-SNARKs, zk-STARKs, etc.), and the computational overhead matters for \"automatic recording of events.\"\n- There's a risk that the research conclusion might be trivially negative: Article 12(3)(c) says you must record \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" — this is quite specific. If the law literally requires storing the data, then ZK proofs don't help; they just prove existence without content, but the regulation demands content recording. The research might end up confirming that the tension is irreconcilable at the cryptographic level and must be resolved at the policy/data-protection-minimization level (e.g., pseudonymization, retention limits) rather than through ZK proofs specifically.\n- That said, even a well-characterized negative result or a precise characterization of WHERE in the regulatory text privacy can and cannot be preserved is scientifically valuable.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a legitimate research question with moderate-to-good intellectual merit. It pushes at the boundary between two important domains (regulatory compliance and cryptographic privacy). The knowledge contribution would be meaningful but not groundbreaking — it's an application and analysis exercise more than a fundamental discovery. The broader impact is real but bounded to the AI-regulation-crypto intersection. I'd say this clears my bar for intellectual merit and frontier-pushing, though it's not a 100. It's a solid B+ research proposal from the knowledge-frontier perspective — interesting, publishable, with some genuine novelty in the application framing, but the core cryptographic techniques are well-established and the regulatory constraint might limit what can actually be achieved.\n\nI'll approve it. The promise score: I'd put this around 62-68. It's a good research question, intellectually meritorious, with clear publication path and some novelty in the intersection. But it's not a frontier-breaking discovery; it's more of an applied analysis that could go either way (positive or negative result) but is valuable either way. I'll say 65.\n",
