e side: building a \"unified\" framework that works across ALL high-risk AI projects is itself a significant engineering effort before any project benefits. There's a risk of over-engineering - trying to build one system that handles all cases perfectly vs. getting 80% coverage quickly.\n- The \"standardized APIs and middleware\" approach could become a bottleneck if the framework doesn't fit well with a particular architecture. Researchers might work around it or fight against its abstractions, slowing down prototyping.\n- There's a risk of building a framework before the requirements are fully understood across all project types. Premature abstraction can lead to rework.\n\n**Article 12 specific considerations:**\n- The proposal mentions \"timestamps, input data, and user IDs\" which maps roughly to Art 12(3)(a), (c), (d). But it doesn't explicitly mention the \"reference database\" (Art 12(3)(b)) or the broader logging requirements in Art 12(2) about identifying risk situations, facilitating post-market monitoring, etc.\n- The scope as described seems to cover the Annex III point 1(a) minimum but may not fully address Art 12(2)'s broader traceability requirements for other high-risk systems.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable infrastructure project. It addresses a real need and the architecture pattern (middleware/library) is proven. However, from my perspective as someone focused on prototyping speed:\n- The \"unified across ALL projects\" ambition risks becoming a slow, heavy framework that takes too long to build and is hard to adapt.\n- A more pragmatic approach might be a lightweight SDK with clear extension points, rather than a monolithic unified framework.\n- But the core idea - centralize logging so researchers don't each build their own - is sound and will pay off in speed once built.\n\nThe technical risk is moderate (not novel tech, but cross-cutting concerns are hard to get right). The prototyping benefit is real but somewhat 