y.\n- Con: \"Integrates with our existing frameworks\" is vague. If we have many different frameworks, the SDK needs adapters for each. That's a maintenance burden and could slow things down during integration.\n- Con: A centralized store introduces network I/O on every logged event. For high-throughput prototyping (e.g., training loops), this could add latency or become a bottleneck if not designed async. The proposal says \"lightweight\" but doesn't address the I/O path clearly.\n- Con: \"Immutable store\" — for rapid prototyping, immutability can be annoying. If I'm iterating fast and logging test runs, I don't want every debug run to persist in an immutable archive indefinitely. There needs to be a staging/local mode vs. production mode distinction. The proposal doesn't mention this.\n- Con: The scope is \"all high-risk projects.\" Building one SDK that works for all of them requires broad upfront investment before any project benefits. This is a platform play, not a quick win.\n\n**Regulatory alignment (from my technical vantage):**\n- Article 12(1): \"technically allow for the automatic recording of events\" — decorators/wrappers directly serve this. Good fit.\n- Article 12(2)(a-c): The SDK needs to capture events relevant to risk situations, post-market monitoring, and operation monitoring. A generic \"timestamps, input metadata, session IDs\" is a baseline but doesn't obviously cover (a) \"identifying situations that may result in the system presenting a risk.\" That requires domain-specific event definitions. The SDK provides plumbing but not the semantic layer.\n- Article 12(3): For Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometrics), specific fields are needed (period of use, reference database, input data for matches, identification of natural persons). A generic SDK would need extensions or hooks for these domain-specific fields. The proposal doesn't address this.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable engineering approach. It's the right arch