        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1943\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The proposal misidentifies the technical challenge: Article 12(3) specifies operational events (session start/end, reference database, matched input data, verifier identity), not internal model activations or semantic states. Standard async logging with structured serialization and compression already handles event-level traceability at negligible latency for inference workloads; 'selective activation tracing' and 'semantic hashing' are solving a problem the regulation does not pose. Framing this as an exploratory research project rather than a targeted engineering build also slows prototyping without clear deliverable mapping to compliance obligations.\",\n      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n      \"promise\": 18\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as a Development Engineer focused on technical viability of solutions and speed/quality of prototyping.\n\nThe proposal P1943 is about developing compression and sampling techniques for automatic logging in large-scale neural networks to maintain regulatory traceability without sacrificing real-time performance.\n\nLet me assess this from a development engineering perspective:\n\n**Technical Viability:**\n- The core problem is real: Article 12(1) requires \"automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" For large-scale neural networks, logging every event naively could indeed introduce latency overhead.\n- However, let me think critically about whether this is actually a technical problem that needs novel research to solve:\n  - Standard logging infrastructure (async writers, ring buffers, compressed log formats) already exists and is well-understood in software engineering.\n  - \"Selective activation tracing\" and \"semantic hashing\" sound like buzzwords that may not map cleanly to what Article 12 actually requires. The regulation asks for recording of *events* — not necessarily every internal neural activation. Article 12(3) gives specific examples: start/end times, reference databases, input data matching results, identification of natural persons verifying results.\n  - The regulation's logging requirements are about operational events (when the system was used, what inputs were given, what outputs matched), not about tracing every internal neuron firing. So \"activation tracing\" and \"semantic hashing\" seem to be solving a problem that isn't actually posed by the regulation.\n\n- The proposal frames this as a research project with experimentation. From a prototyping speed perspective, this is concerning:\n  - It's framed as exploratory (\"research project,\" \"we will experiment with\") rather than engineering a known solution.\n  - The techniques mentioned (selective activation tracing, semantic hashing) are not obviously the right tools for what Article 12 requires. You don't need to trace activations to log that the system was used at time X and processed input Y against reference database Z.\n  - A priority-based logging algorithm based on \"risk-threshold triggers\" introduces complexity: how do you determine risk thresholds? This adds a classification problem before the logging even happens, which could introduce failure modes.\n\n- Speed/quality of prototyping concern:\n  - Standard asynchronous logging with structured data (JSON lines, protobuf) and compression (zstd, lz4) already solves most of the \"overhead\" problem for event-level logging.\n  - The proposal doesn't clearly articulate what specific technical gap exists that standard engineering solutions can't fill.\n  - As a research/exploration project rather than an implementation project, it's slower to deliver tangible results and harder to prototype quickly.\n\n- However, there IS a legitimate kernel here: for systems where logging must happen in real-time (e.g., medical device AI, autonomous driving), the logging pipeline itself cannot be a bottleneck. But this is a well-trodden engineering problem with known solutions (async I/O, backpressure handling, log shipping to separate storage).\n\n- The proposal conflates \"events\" (as defined by the regulation) with internal model state (activations, semantic representations). This is a fundamental misunderstanding of what needs to be logged. You don't need to log every activation; you need to log operational events.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering standpoint, this proposal:\n1. Misidentifies the technical problem — the regulation asks for event logging (operational), not model-internal tracing. Standard async logging already handles this well.\n2. Proposes research/exploration rather than targeted implementation, which is slower to deliver value.\n3. Uses techniques (activation tracing, semantic hashing) that don't obviously map to the actual regulatory requirements in Article 12(3).\n4. The \"priority-based risk-threshold triggers\" adds unnecessary complexity and a potential single point of failure — if your risk classifier fails, you miss logs.\n\nHowever, I should acknowledge: there IS a legitimate engineering challenge in designing logging that's low-overhead for very high-throughput systems (millions of inference calls per second). But the solution to that is well-understood systems engineering, not novel research into \"semantic hashing\" and \"activation tracing.\"\n\nI'll reject this. The technical problem as framed doesn't match what Article 12 actually requires, and the proposed approaches are over-engineered for a standard logging infrastructure challenge. It would consume prototyping resources without delivering clear regulatory compliance value. The promise score should reflect that while there's a tiny kernel of legitimate concern (latency-sensitive logging), the proposal as written is misdirected and too research-oriented for our engineering function.\n\nI'd give it maybe an 18-22. There's a small sliver of real problem, but the framing and proposed solutions are wrong for what we need to deliver.\n",
