eware\" aspect is appropriate from a resource efficiency standpoint because it prevents the fragmentation problem where 10 teams build 10 slightly different logging solutions. It creates economies of scale in both build and maintenance.\n\nOne concern: the proposal says \"absolute regulatory compliance\" which is overpromising, but that's a language issue, not a structural flaw. The architecture described (timestamps, input hashes, versioned reference databases) aligns well with Article 12 requirements.\n\nAnother concern: it doesn't explicitly address the Annex III 1(a) specific logging requirements in paragraph 3 of Article 12, but \"versioned reference databases\" and the general framework could accommodate that as a configuration option.\n\nFrom my vantage point, this is a high-value proposal because:\n- It maximizes resource efficiency (one build, many users)\n- It leverages existing infrastructure (experiment tracking integration)\n- It reduces ongoing per-project compliance costs (automation vs. manual)\n- It creates a single maintenance target rather than N targets\n\nI would approve this. The promise score should be high - maybe 82-85. I'd say 83 because while the architecture is sound and resource-efficient, there are some implementation risks (mandatory middleware can create bottlenecks, \"absolute compliance\" is overpromising, and it may need significant adaptation for Annex III 1(a) specific requirements). But from a pure resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, this is exactly the kind of proposal I'd back.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 83.\n",
