 It addresses Article 12(3)(b) and (c) well, but what about 12(3)(a) - recording start/end dates of each use? And 12(3)(d) - identification of natural persons involved in verification? The proposal focuses on data provenance but doesn't explicitly cover the temporal logging or human identification aspects. However, these might be handled by other systems (application-level logging for timestamps, access control/audit logs for user identity). So this is a component that fits into a broader compliance architecture rather than being a complete solution.\n3. \"Integrate our existing data versioning tools directly into the runtime environment\" - this sounds like it should be feasible without major new procurement. The key question is whether the integration work itself is proportionate to what we already have. If our data versioning tools are already in place and just need a thin adapter layer, this is very cost-effective.\n4. From a funding constraints perspective: this seems like a moderate engineering effort (integration work, not research) that delivers ongoing compliance value across the lifetime of all Annex III systems we deploy. The ROI case is strong because it's a one-time integration cost against recurring manual documentation costs and regulatory risk.\n\n5. Scientific ambition vs. compliance: This doesn't pull resources away from scientific R&D in a major way - it's infrastructure plumbing that enables continued operation under the new regime. Without something like this, we'd either face non-compliance or burn senior engineering time on manual record-keeping.\n\nOverall assessment: This is a well-scoped, efficiently-resourced proposal that leverages existing infrastructure to address specific regulatory requirements. It's not over-engineered (no unnecessary complexity mentioned), it addresses real compliance gaps, and the ongoing cost savings from automation are meaningful. The main gap is that it doesn't cover all of Article 12(3) - but it doesn't claim to be a complete solution either; it's specifically about data provenance/lineage which maps to (b) and (c).\n\nI would approve this. It's efficient, uses existing infrastructure, has clear regulatory value, and the resource commitment seems proportionate to the ongoing compliance burden it eliminates.\n\nPromise score: I'd put this around 78-82. It's a solid, well-targeted engineering task that addresses real requirements efficiently. Not a 90+ because it's one component of a broader compliance picture and \"immutable\" might introduce unnecessary complexity if over-engineered. But from my resource-allocation vantage point, it's very attractive: existing tools, clear scope, recurring savings.\n",
