ng gaps while they're cheap to fix (during development) rather than during a regulatory audit or post-deployment incident.\n- The implementation cost is low: it's essentially a data validation layer on top of existing logging infrastructure. No new architecture needed. You're not building a distributed system; you're writing validators against known schemas.\n- It fits naturally into CI/CD pipelines - run validators as part of test suites, or deploy as a sidecar that tails logs during staging.\n- The \"prototype testing phase\" focus is smart - it means this isn't a full production observability platform (which would be expensive and slow to build), but a targeted compliance check that can be stood up quickly.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n- \"Real-time during prototype testing phase\" - if the logging infrastructure itself isn't mature yet, you're validating logs that may not exist in the expected format. But this is actually a feature: it forces the team to implement proper logging early.\n- The scope seems limited to Article 12(3) (Annex III point 1(a) systems - biometric identification). For other high-risk categories, you'd need different validators. But as an initial prototype tool, starting with one category is reasonable and fast.\n- \"Avoiding costly retrospective fixes\" - valid engineering concern. Retrofitting logging into a system that was built without it is genuinely expensive compared to building it in from the start.\n\n**My decision:**\nFrom my vantage point (technical viability + prototyping speed/quality), this is a low-effort, high-value proposal. It's technically straightforward, bounded in scope, and directly supports faster compliance-aware prototyping. The implementation would be quick (days to weeks, not months), the risk of failure is very low (it's validation logic, not novel ML or distributed systems), and it immediately improves the quality of prototypes by catching a class of bugs (missing log fields) that would otherwise surface late.\n