sal doesn't specify ANY technical details about HOW the logging will be implemented. No architecture, no technology choices, no interface specifications. It's purely an organizational/budgeting proposal.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- Positive: Having a pre-built, reusable logging component library that a shared team maintains means I don't have to design my own logging from scratch each time. This accelerates prototyping.\n- Negative: A \"shared service team\" model can be slow for rapid iteration. If I'm in a tight prototyping cycle and need to modify logging behavior for testing, going through a central team adds latency. The proposal doesn't mention any self-service or developer-facing tools that would let me handle routine logging changes myself while escalating complex issues.\n- Negative: The \"percentage of general R&D budget\" is vague. If the percentage is too small, the shared team becomes a bottleneck. If it's too large, it's over-allocated. This uncertainty creates risk for prototyping timelines.\n- Positive: By separating compliance costs from research grants, it ensures that prototyping resources aren't unexpectedly diverted to compliance work mid-project. This actually protects prototyping speed and quality by making resource availability more predictable.\n\n**My assessment:**\nThis is primarily an organizational/financial proposal with a technical component (the shared service team for logging). From my vantage point:\n- The concept of having specialized, reusable logging infrastructure maintained by experts IS technically sound and would improve both viability and prototyping speed (you don't rebuild the wheel).\n- However, the \"shared service team\" model risks becoming a bottleneck for rapid prototyping cycles. There's no mention of developer self-service, API-first design, or how to handle urgent prototyping needs.\n- The proposal is vague on technical specifics - it tells me WHO will do it but not HOW they'll do it technically.\n- Th