cted simultaneously. Versioning strategy matters.\n- The \"centralized\" aspect could become a performance bottleneck or single point of failure during prototyping when teams want to work independently.\n- For truly novel AI architectures (e.g., agentic systems, multi-model pipelines), the \"system states\" that need capturing might not fit neatly into a generic middleware template.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a solid, technically viable proposal that directly addresses my core concern about prototyping speed. The pattern of shared compliance infrastructure is well-proven in other regulated industries (medical devices, financial services). The main risks are: (1) upfront build cost before benefit materializes, (2) the \"immutable ledger\" and \"centralized\" language suggesting over-engineering for early-stage prototypes, (3) the risk that Annex III templates become either too rigid or too generic.\n\nBut fundamentally, a well-designed logging SDK/middleware that projects can integrate in hours rather than weeks is exactly what preserves prototyping velocity while ensuring compliance. This is technically very feasible - we're talking about structured logging with configuration profiles, not moonshots. I'd approve this but note the execution risks.\n\nPromise score: I'm quite positive on this from my vantage point. The core idea (shared logging SDK to preserve speed) is exactly right. The \"immutable ledger\" and \"centralized\" aspects are implementation details that could be adjusted. I'd say around 78-82. It's technically sound, directly serves my mandate, but has some over-engineering risk in the specific language used (\"ledger\", \"centralized\") that could slow actual prototyping if taken literally.\n\nI'll go with 80.\n",
