ciency - you're not spending 20% of your compute budget on logging pipelines for a research prototype that will never be deployed as a high-risk system.\n\n**Weaknesses/Concerns:**\n- A quarterly cross-functional review board is an overhead cost itself. In a small R&D lab, dedicating senior people to quarterly compliance audits could eat into the very resources you're trying to protect. The administrative burden needs to be calibrated.\n- \"Mandatory compliance map for every funded grant\" - this adds a bureaucratic step. But from my perspective, this is actually reasonable because it forces the risk classification to happen at project inception rather than retrofitting later (which is far more expensive).\n- The quarterly cadence might be too infrequent if projects evolve rapidly in R&D. A project that starts as exploratory could shift toward a high-risk deployment mid-quarter. But this is a minor concern and could be addressed with trigger-based reviews.\n- The proposal is somewhat generic - it doesn't specify what the classification criteria are beyond \"EU AI Act risk level.\" But that's arguably appropriate since the regulation itself defines the categories.\n\n**Resource-allocation perspective:**\n- This is fundamentally a force multiplier for our limited resources. Without it, we'd either (a) over-comply everywhere and starve our research budget, or (b) under-comply and face regulatory penalties. The middle path of risk-based allocation is exactly what my function should be advocating for.\n- It also protects existing R&D infrastructure: you don't want to retrofit logging into systems that were never designed with it in mind if they're not actually high-risk. Planning ahead saves infrastructure costs.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis proposal hits the nail on the head from my vantage point. It's a structural/governance mechanism that directly serves resource-allocation efficiency. The main risk is implementation overhead (the board, the quarterly cadence), bu