hout needing DP.\n- The scientific ambition here (DP applied to audit logging) exceeds what's needed for the regulatory purpose in most cases.\n- However, if we ARE building Annex III 1(a) systems AND have sensitive PII concerns, there IS a genuine gap that needs solving — but the solution might be more nuanced than full DP.\n\n**Effective use of existing infrastructure:**\n- The proposal doesn't mention how it leverages our existing logging/infrastructure stack.\n- It seems to propose building new cryptographic and DP mechanisms from scratch or integrating complex libraries.\n\nMy assessment: This is an ambitious research-oriented approach to what might be a more straightforward engineering problem. From a resource-allocation efficiency standpoint, committing scarce R&D resources to build DP-based audit logging infrastructure when simpler approaches (encryption at rest, access controls, pseudonymization with separate key stores) might meet both the regulatory and privacy requirements seems like a poor use of limited funding. The proposal also has potential compliance issues (anonymizing verifiers in 1(a) systems would violate Article 12(3)(d)). However, it does identify a real tension and the underlying question is valid — how do we handle PII in logs while maintaining auditability?\n\nI'll lean toward reject because:\n1. It's over-engineered for the actual regulatory requirement\n2. It may create compliance risk by over-anonymizing\n3. The resource cost is high relative to a simpler engineering solution\n4. It doesn't clearly integrate with existing infrastructure\n5. The scientific ambition exceeds what funding constraints justify\n\nBut it's not a zero — the problem identified is real, and if we do build Annex III 1(a) systems, some form of privacy-preserving logging will be needed. So I'd give it maybe a 30-40 promise score but reject it as stated because it's too ambitious for our resource constraints and may not even be compliant.\n\nLet me settle on: reject,