hasizes \"traceability\" and that information should be \"drawn in a clear and comprehensive form.\" A unified framework promotes clarity and comprehensiveness by standardizing formats.\n6. Market reputation: if we can demonstrate to clients and partners that we have a robust, institutional-level logging infrastructure, it strengthens our credibility as a compliant AI developer. This is especially important in the EU market where regulatory compliance is becoming a competitive differentiator.\n\n**Potential concerns:**\n1. \"Integrates with current compute clusters\" - this might be too narrow. Not all high-risk AI systems run on the same compute infrastructure. Some may run in cloud environments, edge devices, or client-side deployments. A framework limited to \"current compute clusters\" might not cover all deployment scenarios. However, as a starting point and modular API approach, it could be extended.\n2. The proposal is somewhat high-level - it doesn't specify what the logs actually contain, retention periods, access controls, or how they interface with post-market monitoring (Article 72). But this is an implementation detail that can be specified in development.\n3. \"Minimizes per-project engineering burden on researchers\" - while operationally attractive, I need to ensure this doesn't create a situation where the infrastructure team becomes a single point of failure for compliance. If the unified library has a bug, all systems are affected simultaneously. However, from a compliance standpoint, having one auditable system is actually better than N disparate implementations.\n4. The proposal doesn't explicitly mention log retention periods, data protection considerations (GDPR interplay with logging personal data, especially under Article 12(3)(d) which requires identifying natural persons), or immutability of logs. But again, these are implementation details.\n\n**Compliance bar assessment:**\n- Does it address a mandatory requirement? YES - Article 12 is m