f the lab already has fragmented logging and there's no unified solution, this could consolidate and actually save resources in the long run. The open-source angle means the maintenance burden isn't permanently on the lab.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. From a pure resource-efficiency standpoint, I'd want to see:\n- Whether existing tools are being leveraged vs. replaced\n- Whether scope is limited to high-risk systems (as required) rather than \"every experiment\"\n- Whether the open-source community will actually take on maintenance\n\nThe proposal as stated is somewhat ambitious in scope (\"for every experiment performed in the lab\") which goes beyond what Article 12 requires. This over-scoping wastes resources. But the fundamental direction is right.\n\nI'll lean toward approval but with moderate promise score, because the direction is correct and the dual-benefit argument holds, but the implementation as described has resource-allocation concerns (over-scoping, potential duplication of existing infrastructure). Actually, let me reconsider - my job is to approve or reject based on whether it clears my bar. \n\nThe key question: does this proposal commit scarce resources wisely? The answer is nuanced:\n- The direction (automated provenance) is correct and necessary for compliance\n- But \"for every experiment\" over-scopes beyond legal requirement\n- Building a new SDK may duplicate existing tools\n- However, the open-source model could reduce long-term cost\n\nI think I'll approve it but with a moderate promise score around 62-68. It's directionally right and the dual-benefit is compelling, but the implementation details as described raise efficiency concerns that would need to be addressed in execution. Actually, let me reconsider - the proposal says \"for every experiment performed in the lab\" which is broader than what Article 12 requires (which is for high-risk systems). This over-scoping is a resource waste from my vantage point. But it also serves