d/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- The core infrastructure (Prometheus + Grafana + Alertmanager) can be stood up very quickly — days to a couple of weeks for a functional prototype. This is fast.\n- However, the \"compliance\" layer on top — mapping specific regulatory requirements to specific alerts, defining what thresholds constitute \"substantial modification,\" ensuring all Article 12(3) fields are captured — requires domain expertise and careful design beyond just standing up dashboards.\n- The prototype would be achievable quickly for the monitoring infrastructure part, but the \"compliance meaning\" layer is where it gets harder and more nuanced.\n\n**Overall Assessment:**\nFrom a pure development engineering perspective:\n- The tech stack choice (Prometheus/Grafana) is pragmatic and well-understood. Good for speed.\n- The core concept (log analysis → threshold alerts → compliance flags) is technically feasible and straightforward to prototype.\n- The risk is that the proposal oversimplifies what \"anomaly detection\" means for AI systems. A naive threshold approach might miss subtle drift or generate too many false positives. But as a starting point/prototype, it's viable.\n- It doesn't fully address the structured data requirements of Article 12(3) for biometric systems (specific fields like reference database, input data matches, verification person IDs) — Prometheus/Grafana is metrics-oriented, not necessarily suited for structured event logging with rich metadata. You'd likely need a different storage layer (e.g., Elasticsearch, or a relational DB) alongside it for the full Article 12(3) requirements.\n- As a prototype to demonstrate compliance monitoring capability, it's fast and doable.\n\nI think this is technically viable as a starting point. The Prometheus/Grafana stack is the right choice for the metrics/alerting layer. The main concern is that it oversimplifies drift detection and may not fully cover the structured logging requirements of 12(3), but 