        "content": "{\n  \"screenings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1725\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The core principle is sound and necessary: Article 12 binds only high-risk systems, so we must have a documented process to determine which of our projects fall within scope before any logging architecture can be designed or resources allocated. However, several elements as written create compliance risk from my vantage point: the 'brief' quarterly cadence is inadequate for the analytical complexity of classification (Annex I product-safety pathways are entirely absent, and substantial-modification reclassification under Article 113 can shift a system's status mid-quarter); the language 'formally exempted' implies an internal authority to carve out legal obligations rather than to determine their applicability; and the framing around 'protecting scientific ambition' signals a minimisation motivation that, if produced in a regulator interview or audit, would read as premeditated scope-narrowing rather than good-faith risk assessment. The concept is a prerequisite for everything else we do under the Act, but this specific formulation needs substantial tightening before I could stand behind it in front of a notified body or market surveillance authority.\",\n      \"decision\": \"approve\",\n      \"promise\": 52\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze this proposal from my vantage point as Compliance Officer concerned with institutional survival, regulatory compliance, and market reputation.\n\nThe proposal is a \"High-Risk Classification Triage Protocol\" — an internal process to determine which projects are high-risk vs. low-risk under the EU AI Act, so that Article 12 logging requirements only apply where legally required.\n\nLet me assess this from my compliance perspective:\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Correctly identifies that not all AI systems fall under \"high-risk\" — Article 12 only applies to high-risk AI systems. So classifying properly is a prerequisite for compliance.\n- A quarterly review process with lead scientists mapping against Annex III criteria is reasonable governance.\n- Preventing over-application of requirements to low-risk systems is efficient and legally sound — you don't need to comply with Article 12 if the system isn't high-risk.\n\n**Concerns from a compliance standpoint:**\n- The proposal says \"Projects deemed low-risk are formally exempted from the heavy Article 12 technical requirements.\" This is where I get nervous. The EU AI Act's classification isn't just about Annex III. There's also Annex I (product safety), and the concept of \"substantial modification\" could shift a system's risk category over time. A quarterly review might miss mid-quarter shifts in intended purpose or deployment context that could reclassify a system as high-risk.\n- The phrase \"formally exempted\" is risky language. The compliance officer shouldn't be \"exempting\" projects from legal requirements — the law determines applicability, not an internal triage. If a regulator later determines the system WAS high-risk and you applied a \"triage protocol\" to avoid logging, that looks like deliberate circumvention rather than good faith classification.\n- \"Protects our scientific ambition by preventing the unnecessary application of restrictive protocols to exploratory research\" — this framing is concerning from a compliance perspective. It suggests the primary motivation is to avoid regulatory burden rather than to correctly determine legal applicability. If this document ended up in front of a regulator or court, that language could be read as evidence that the institution was motivated by convenience rather than genuine risk assessment.\n- The \"brief\" quarterly review is concerning for adequacy. Classification under the EU AI Act can be complex — it involves looking at intended purpose, context of use, whether the system is a component of another high-risk product, etc. A \"brief\" process might not capture all the nuances.\n- However, the core idea — that you need to know which systems are high-risk before applying Article 12 — is fundamentally correct and necessary. You can't comply with a regulation you're not subject to.\n\n**Institutional survival angle:**\n- If we fail to classify correctly in either direction (calling something low-risk when it's high-risk, or calling everything high-risk and wasting resources), both are problems. The first is a compliance failure; the second is an operational waste that could slow our competitive edge.\n- A documented classification process actually PROTECTS us — if we can show we systematically evaluated each project against Annex III/Annex I criteria, that's a defense in case of regulatory challenge.\n\n**Market reputation angle:**\n- A transparent, well-documented triage process demonstrates institutional maturity to partners, customers, and regulators.\n- But the framing around \"protecting scientific ambition\" could be perceived as the institution trying to minimize its compliance obligations rather than genuinely assessing risk.\n\n**Net assessment:**\nThe core idea is sound and necessary — you MUST determine which systems are high-risk before applying Article 12. However, the execution details raise red flags:\n- \"Brief\" review is insufficient for the complexity of classification\n- Quarterly frequency may be too infrequent given that system purposes can shift\n- The language around \"exempting\" and \"protecting scientific ambition\" suggests a motivation to minimize compliance rather than correctly determine it\n- No mention of Annex I (product safety) high-risk systems, only Annex III\n- No mention of the substantial modification pathway that could reclassify a system\n\nBut the fundamental concept is correct and necessary. Without SOME form of classification process, we'd either over-comply on everything (wasting resources) or under-comply (regulatory violation). The proposal gets the right direction but has concerning execution details and framing.\n\nI think this is a borderline case. The idea is necessary, but as written it has compliance risks in its framing and execution details. I'll lean toward approve because without some form of triage, we can't even begin to comply with Article 12 (you need to know what's in scope first). But the promise score should reflect my reservations about the specific implementation described.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposal says \"ensures we only apply rigorous logging where legally required.\" This is correct in principle. The risk I see is more about execution than concept. A compliance function would absolutely want to establish a classification process — it's prerequisite to everything else. Even if this specific implementation has rough edges, the direction is right and necessary.\n\nI'll approve but with a moderate promise score reflecting that while the concept is essential, the specific framing and execution details as proposed need significant refinement before I'd feel comfortable endorsing them in a regulatory submission or audit.\n",
