ors and customers.\n\n**Concerns/Weaknesses:**\n1. The proposal focuses on \"timestamps, input references, and session IDs\" - but Article 12(3) for Annex III point 1(a) systems requires more specific things: (b) reference database against which input data has been checked, (c) input data for which search led to a match, (d) identification of natural persons involved in verification. The proposal's description seems to capture some but not all of these specifically. However, it does say \"input references\" which could encompass the reference database, and \"session IDs\" could support person identification.\n2. It says \"wrapper around our existing telemetry tools\" - this is practical and reduces implementation burden, but I need to ensure that wrapping existing tools actually captures ALL required event types. Existing telemetry might not have been designed with regulatory requirements in mind.\n3. The proposal doesn't explicitly address retention periods, log integrity (tamper-proofing), or accessibility for regulators during audits. But these might be out of scope for a \"record-keeping\" SDK and handled elsewhere.\n4. From a compliance perspective, the key question is: does this ensure that ALL high-risk AI systems we develop will have compliant logging? A single dependency helps, but only if adoption is mandatory across all teams. The proposal implies this (\"single technical dependency rather than fragmented, manual efforts\").\n\n**Overall assessment from Compliance Officer view:**\nThis is a strong proposal that directly addresses the core requirement of Article 12(1). It reduces institutional risk by eliminating fragmentation. It supports our ability to demonstrate compliance to regulators. It protects market reputation by showing systematic approach. The main gap is whether it fully covers all sub-requirements of Article 12(3), but the architecture (a shared SDK) is the right approach and can be extended.\n\nFrom my vantage point: This directly serves re