can slow things down.\n- However, the proposal says it's a \"library\" and \"modular API\" — if well-designed, the overhead per integration should be minimal (a few lines of code to initialize the logger, and events get captured automatically). This is similar to how OpenTelemetry works — low friction once integrated.\n- The risk: if the framework itself needs to support Article 12(3) specifics for Annex III point 1(a) systems (recording start/end times, reference databases, input data matches, identification of natural persons), that's more domain-specific than generic logging. A truly \"unified\" framework would need pluggable event schemas per system type.\n- From a prototyping speed perspective: if the library is well-designed with sensible defaults and minimal boilerplate, it could actually SPEED UP compliance work for individual teams (they don't each reinvent the wheel). The one-time cost of building the framework is offset by not having N teams build N logging solutions.\n- Quality: centralized framework means consistent quality, easier testing of the logging layer itself, and fewer edge cases across projects.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a technically sound, well-scoped engineering proposal. It addresses Article 12(1) (automatic recording of events/logs over lifetime) directly, and provides infrastructure for 12(2)(a), (b), (c). The technical approach (modular API, immutable storage, cross-environment deployment) is standard and proven. From my vantage point:\n\n- Technical viability: High. This is well-understood engineering with no fundamental blockers.\n- Impact on prototyping speed: Slight initial overhead for integration, but net positive over time as it eliminates per-project reinvention. The key risk is if the framework becomes rigid or slow to adapt — but \"modular API\" mitigates that.\n- Quality of prototyping: Positive. Consistent logging means better debugging, easier compliance verification, fewer ad-hoc patches.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar comfortably. It's a solid engineering investment with clear technical feasibility and positive impact on both speed (long-term) and quality. The main risk is execution scope creep (\"unified across all environments\" can become a never-ending integration project), but the proposal as stated is reasonable.\n\nDecision: Approve.\nPromise score: I'd give this around 82-85. It's technically sound, well-motivated by the regulation, and aligns with good engineering practice. The slight deduction is for the \"across all environments\" scope which can become unwieldy in an R&D context where pipelines are heterogeneous and evolving rapidly, and because a truly unified framework across very different AI system types (LLM fine-tuning pipelines vs. real-time inference) requires careful abstraction that could slow initial delivery. But fundamentally it's the right approach and technically achievable.\n",
