is is good marketing language, from a compliance standpoint, the obligation in Article 12 is to HAVE logging capability that enables recording of relevant events. The detection engine goes beyond the minimum but also creates additional operational complexity and potential points of failure.\n- \"Real time\" alerts — Article 12 doesn't mandate real-time; it mandates that logs enable identification of situations. Real-time is a stretch goal that increases implementation cost without being strictly required by the text. However, for institutional survival (catching issues before they escalate), near-real-time monitoring is valuable.\n- The proposal commits scarce resources to developing custom anomaly detection algorithms. This is a significant R&D investment. Is it necessary for compliance? Article 12(2)(a) requires logging capabilities that ENABLE recording of events relevant to identifying risk situations. It doesn't strictly require automated detection — human review of logs could satisfy the requirement. However, at scale, automated detection becomes practically necessary.\n\n**Compliance assessment:**\n- The obligation under Article 12 is to have logging capability. The proposal goes beyond by adding active detection. This is a \"nice to have\" that exceeds minimum compliance but significantly reduces risk of non-compliance in practice.\n- For institutional survival: if we rely solely on passive logs and miss a substantial modification, we could face enforcement action. An automated detection engine materially reduces this risk.\n- For market reputation: demonstrating active monitoring beyond mere logging is a positive signal to regulators and customers.\n- The main risk is over-engineering: committing scarce resources to an AI-on-AI solution when the core obligation (having proper logs) might not yet be fully implemented. However, I don't know the current state of their logging infrastructure from this proposal alone.\n\n**Decision:** This is a strong proposal t