f successful, compressed telemetry and cryptographic logging could benefit not just AI Act compliance but any domain requiring audit trails for complex systems (autonomous vehicles, medical devices, financial trading).\n\n**Concerns from my vantage point:**\n- \"Small cross-functional team\" — is this enough to make genuine intellectual contributions? Compressed telemetry might be solvable with existing compression techniques. Cryptographic proofs of execution are extremely hard problems; a small team might not push the frontier meaningfully unless they're targeting very specific sub-problems.\n- The scope seems broad: \"compressed telemetry AND cryptographic proofs of execution\" — these are two quite different research directions. A small team might spread too thin and produce incremental rather than frontier-level work.\n- There's a risk this becomes an engineering optimization project (make logs smaller) dressed up as research. The line between \"apply existing compression to log data\" (engineering) and \"develop new information-theoretic frameworks for what constitutes sufficient evidence of correct AI execution\" (research) is important. The proposal text doesn't clearly commit to the latter.\n- However, the phrasing \"without incurring prohibitive storage costs\" suggests they're aware of the practical constraint, which gives it grounding.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe idea has genuine intellectual merit IF pursued at the right level of abstraction. The intersection of verifiable computation, differential privacy, and information-theoretic compression for ML system observability is a real frontier with no dominant solution yet. The broader scientific impact could be significant — new primitives for \"auditable but private\" execution are valuable beyond AI Act compliance.\n\nHowever, the proposal as stated is somewhat vague on what specifically constitutes the research contribution vs. engineering application. A small team exploring both compressed telemetry AND cryptographic proofs might not achieve depth in either. But the core idea — that regulatory constraints can be turned into well-posed scientific problems with broader applicability — is sound and has intellectual merit.\n\nI'd say this clears my bar as a researcher. It's at the intersection of several active research areas (verifiable computation, privacy-preserving ML, information theory) applied to a concrete problem. The broader impact is real. The intellectual merit depends on execution depth, but the problem space itself is frontier-adjacent.\n\nI'll approve with a moderate-to-high promise score. It's genuinely interesting from a research perspective, though the breadth vs. depth concern for a small team keeps it from being a 100. I'd put it around 72-78. The core scientific questions are real and underexplored, but the proposal as written risks becoming an engineering task rather than pushing a knowledge frontier.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 74.\n",
