t risks creating two separate log stores with synchronization issues. The Article 12 requirement is that the system \"shall technically allow for the automatic recording of events (logs) over the lifetime of the system.\" If the filtering layer introduces any delay, loss, or inconsistency, that's a compliance gap.\n- The tool is an *export* utility — it doesn't itself ensure logging capability exists at the system level (Article 12(1)). It's downstream of the actual logging architecture. This means it's useful but not sufficient on its own for Article 12 compliance.\n- However, as a compliance officer, I care about being able to demonstrate compliance. An audit-ready export tool is extremely valuable for that purpose. It reduces the risk that during an audit, we can't produce logs in a timely or comprehensible manner.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nThis is a useful supporting tool but carries some design risks if the filtering logic is too narrow. From my vantage point (regulatory compliance and institutional survival), this is a reasonable proposal to pursue — it directly supports our ability to demonstrate compliance during audits and post-market monitoring reviews. The key risk is in the \"extracts only\" language, which I'd want to flag as requiring conservative scope definition, but the core concept is sound.\n\nIt's not a substitute for proper Article 12(1) logging architecture at the system level, but it's a valuable complement. From my compliance perspective, having audit-ready exports dramatically reduces our exposure during regulatory interactions.\n\nI'd approve this — it addresses a real operational need in demonstrating compliance, supports traceability (Recital 71), and aids post-market monitoring. The promise score: it's genuinely useful but not a standalone solution to Article 12 compliance. I'd put it around 68-72. It helps with the \"comprehensible information\" requirement and makes our regulatory interactions much smoother, but it doesn't by itself 