irst before running.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- This is my primary concern. A \"mandatory middleware layer\" in an R&D lab is a significant constraint on prototyping speed. Researchers iterating on models, trying different architectures, swapping data pipelines — all of this gets slowed down if every change requires re-integrating with the logging framework.\n- However, the upside is that once built, it eliminates repetitive logging work per project. If done well (like OpenTelemetry's SDK approach), the integration cost per new system could be minimal.\n- The \"unified\" and \"standardized\" aspects are good for consistency but risky if the lab has diverse technical stacks. A one-size-fits-all middleware might not fit all architectures cleanly, leading to workarounds or delays.\n- The proposal doesn't mention how this handles evolving systems — in R&D, prototypes change rapidly. If the logging framework is too rigid, it becomes a bottleneck.\n- \"Immutable storage\" adds infrastructure complexity but is relatively standard (append-only logs with cryptographic hashing).\n\n**Quality considerations:**\n- Centralized logging improves quality and consistency of compliance evidence.\n- Reduces human error in record-keeping — valid point.\n- But \"eliminating the risk of human error\" is too strong — misconfiguration, incorrect event classification, or gaps in what gets logged could still occur.\n\n**My overall assessment:**\nThe core idea is technically sound and aligns well with Article 12's requirements for automatic logging. The middleware pattern is proven. However, from my vantage point on prototyping speed, the \"mandatory\" integration into ALL pipelines in an R&D setting is a concern. It could become a bottleneck if not designed with modularity and low-friction integration in mind. The proposal as stated is somewhat high-level — it doesn't address how different architectures will integrate, what the performance overhead looks like, or how R&D teams c