on pipeline. This proposal addresses the technical documentation aspect (Recital 71) more than the Article 12(1) log-recording requirement per se. Though it does help with traceability.\n\n**Speed/quality of prototyping:**\n- The stated benefit is preventing \"documentation from becoming a bottleneck that slows down the speed of prototyping iterations.\" From my vantage point, this is actually a very relevant pain point. In practice, when you're iterating on models rapidly, manually updating compliance docs after every change is tedious and error-prone. Automating the *structural* parts (hyperparameters, versions, architecture specs) does remove friction.\n- However, I need to be careful: this tool adds a layer of complexity to the prototyping pipeline itself. If the documentation generator breaks or becomes slow, it could actually *slow down* prototyping. The \"trigger on every successful model merge\" approach means every prototype iteration now has a downstream dependency (the doc generation step). If that's not well-engineered (async, non-blocking), it could hurt speed.\n- The quality angle: auto-generated documentation from experiment trackers tends to be technically accurate but may miss the interpretive/narrative context that a regulator or auditor would want. It's a good starting point but not a complete solution.\n\n**Resource commitment concern:**\n- Building this is moderate effort: connect existing MLOps tooling, build templates, wire up CI/CD hooks. Not a massive undertaking, but not trivial either — you need to maintain the templates as regulatory requirements evolve, handle edge cases in experiment tracking metadata, ensure the generated document actually satisfies the regulatory text (not just looks complete).\n- The risk is building something that gives 70% of the value but requires ongoing maintenance to keep the templates aligned with what regulators actually expect.\n\n**My assessment:**\nFrom a development engineering standpoint, this is technic