ed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- A working prototype of this is quite fast to build: a CI pipeline with Jinja2 or similar templates that reads from structured metadata (JSON/YAML alongside code) and produces Markdown/PDF compliance documents. You could have something running in days, not weeks.\n- The quality bar for \"regulatory-grade documentation\" is high though. The initial prototype would likely produce documents that need human review before they're audit-ready. But as a *starting point* to reduce manual burden, it's viable.\n- The risk: over-engineering the automation. If you try to make every single doc update fully automatic from a git commit, you'll spend weeks on edge cases that don't matter for compliance. A simpler approach (structured metadata + templated generation with human-in-the-loop approval) is more practical and faster to deliver.\n\n**From my specific vantage point:**\n- The core technical concept is sound and prototyped patterns already exist in the industry.\n- It addresses a real pain point (keeping docs updated) that would otherwise slow down our development cycles if done manually.\n- A prototype is very achievable quickly - this isn't moonshot engineering.\n- The main concern is scope creep: \"transforms static documents into living records\" could balloon into a massive project if we try to make it fully autonomous. But as a scoped prototype (CI-triggered doc regeneration from structured metadata), it's very doable.\n- It doesn't directly conflict with prototyping speed - in fact, it could *improve* our iteration cycles by reducing the documentation overhead per release/change.\n\nI think this clears my bar for technical viability and has good prototyping potential. The concept is well-grounded in existing engineering patterns, a prototype can be built quickly, and it addresses a genuine workflow bottleneck. I'll approve it with a moderately high promise score - it's viable and useful but the \"fully automated\" framing needs careful sc