ion balance**: By offloading compliance logging to infrastructure, researchers can focus their time on actual science rather than regulatory plumbing. This protects scientific throughput.\n\n**Concerns/weaknesses:**\n1. The proposal is somewhat generic — \"standardized software library\" and \"middleware layer\" are broad descriptions. The actual engineering complexity of intercepting inputs/outputs across *different models* (possibly different architectures, training paradigms, inference pipelines) could be significant. A one-size-fits-all middleware might not work well if the lab's high-risk systems vary substantially in architecture.\n2. \"Integrated into our primary compute cluster\" — this is good for efficiency but could create a single point of failure or bottleneck. If the logging layer has latency issues, it could slow down training/inference across all projects simultaneously.\n3. The proposal doesn't address maintenance costs. A shared library needs ongoing updates as models evolve, new compliance requirements emerge (the AI Act is being supplemented by guidelines), etc. Who maintains this? This is a recurring resource commitment.\n4. \"Prevents individual researchers from wasting limited time building redundant logging tools\" — this assumes the redundancy actually exists or will exist. If the lab only has 1-2 high-risk systems, the overhead of a shared framework might exceed the cost of a targeted solution.\n\n**Overall assessment:**\nFrom my resource-allocation vantage point, this is a well-motivated proposal. The core logic — amortize compliance infrastructure development across multiple projects rather than duplicating effort per project — is sound and directly addresses funding constraints and researcher time. The integration into existing compute infrastructure (rather than new hardware) shows awareness of the existing R&D infrastructure constraint.\n\nThe main risk is engineering scope creep: building a truly universal middleware for \"all high-ri