d, etc.) but not the analytical/narrative parts.\n\n**Speed/Quality of Prototyping:**\n- This directly addresses a bottleneck: engineers spending time writing compliance documentation instead of iterating on models.\n- If integrated into CI/CD, it's passive - no additional step for the developer. The doc is generated as part of the build/deploy pipeline.\n- This reduces friction in prototyping because you don't need to \"stop and document\" before deploying.\n- MLflow/W&B integration is a natural fit since most ML teams already use these tools.\n- Custom scripts are a risk - they add maintenance overhead, but this is standard engineering work.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- The scope claim (\"auto-generate the technical documentation required by Recital 71\") overstates what can be automated. This could lead to false confidence that compliance is handled when it's not fully covered.\n- \"Custom scripts\" is a bit vague - but in practice this means a moderate development effort (a few engineer-weeks for initial implementation, ongoing maintenance).\n- The tool doesn't address Article 12(3) specific logging requirements for Annex III point 1(a) systems (biometric identification), which are more about runtime event logging than documentation generation.\n\n**Overall assessment from my vantage point:**\nThis is a technically viable solution that addresses a real pain point for prototyping speed. The core technical approach (parsing structured data sources and generating markdown/PDF reports in CI/CD) is well within standard engineering capabilities. It won't solve 100% of the documentation requirement, but it solves a meaningful and recurring portion that currently requires manual effort every time a model version changes. The integration with tools we already use (MLflow/W&B) makes it low-friction to adopt.\n\nThe main risk is scope creep or overpromising - if leadership thinks this \"solves\" Recital 71 compliance, they might underinvest in the narrative/analytical documentation parts that still need human input. But as a development tool to reduce repetitive manual work, it's solid.\n\nI'd rate this quite favorably from my perspective. It's technically feasible, addresses a real speed bottleneck, leverages existing infrastructure, and the implementation path is clear. I'd approve it with a high promise score, maybe 78-85. The overstatement of scope (\"auto-generate the technical documentation required\") slightly concerns me because if we commit to this expecting full compliance coverage, we'll be disappointed, but as a prototyping speed tool it's genuinely useful.\n\nLet me settle on: approve, promise 82.\n",
