ployment check alone might suffice, and then periodic spot-checks rather than full quarterly drills on every system.\n\n**Effective use of existing R&D infrastructure:**\n- This proposal doesn't build new infrastructure; it uses what's already there (logs, documentation). That's efficient in that sense.\n- But the \"reconstruction\" exercise requires researchers to actually engage with logs and trace paths — this is labor-intensive and could be partially automated if logging is well-designed. If logging is good, reconstruction should be straightforward; if it's poor, the drill exposes that but at high human cost each quarter.\n- It creates a feedback loop: bad logs → painful drills → motivation to fix logs. That's actually a useful mechanism for continuous improvement of logging quality without needing separate \"improve your logging\" projects.\n\n**Assessment:**\nThe core idea has merit — verifying that documentation matches actual system behavior is exactly what Article 12 requires (\"kept up to date, appropriately throughout the lifetime\"). The drill format is clever as it creates accountability and surfaces gaps. However:\n\nPros from my vantage:\n- Uses existing infrastructure (logs, docs) rather than requiring new builds\n- Pre-deployment timing avoids disrupting active research\n- Creates a feedback mechanism that incentivizes good logging without separate projects\n- Quarterly cadence is reasonable (not too frequent, not too rare)\n- Protects against expensive post-market corrections\n\nCons from my vantage:\n- \"Mandatory\" and applies to all systems — in a lab with many early-stage experiments, this could be burdensome\n- Requires dual staffing (compliance + researchers) quarterly\n- The \"reconstruct the decision path using ONLY available logs\" requirement could be very labor-intensive for complex AI systems where the \"decision path\" is not a simple linear trace\n- Risk of becoming bureaucratic theater if not tied to actual corrective actions\n\nOver