nd somewhat vague. \"Semantic logging systems\" and \"standard API for recording latent space transitions and attention weights\" — the technical challenges here are enormous. Latent spaces in different architectures (transformers, diffusion models, etc.) have very different properties. A \"standard API\" that works across architectures is a non-trivial research problem.\n- The proposal conflates interpretability with compliance in a way that might not be well-grounded. Attention weights and latent transitions are necessary but far from sufficient for understanding decision logic. Calling this \"decision logic\" capture overstates what these signals actually provide.\n- The scientific value depends heavily on whether the resulting data is actually usable for interpretability research, or whether it's just very high-dimensional noise. There's no clear hypothesis about what specific interpretability questions this data would answer.\n- From a pure knowledge frontier perspective, the idea of structured inference-time internal state logging is novel but the \"so what\" for scientific discovery isn't fully articulated. What new scientific understanding does recording attention weights during deployment (as opposed to in controlled analysis) actually yield?\n\n**Broader scientific impact:**\n- If successful, this could create a corpus of real-world model internals that doesn't exist today. That has potential value for the interpretability community.\n- The standardization aspect could have field-wide impact if adopted.\n- However, the proposal as stated is more of an engineering/systems project with research aspirations than a deep scientific investigation into new knowledge.\n\n**Intellectual merit:**\n- The core insight (regulatory logs can be enriched to serve dual compliance + science purposes) has genuine intellectual merit.\n- But the specific technical claims (that capturing attention weights = capturing \"decision logic\") are somewhat reductive from an interpreta